const e=[{id:1,code:"FACT-101",icon:"HardHat",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Personal protective equipment (PPE)",subtitle:"Personal protective equipment",summary:"Knowing your personal protective equipment and how to use it correctly",blocks:[{body:"Personal protective equipment (PPE) is the last line of defense against injury: before relying on it, hazards must be eliminated or reduced through engineering controls and safe work procedures (Hierarchy of Controls). In factories, wearing PPE is not optional; it is mandatory."},{heading:"1. Main types of PPE",body:"Each part of the body has its own specific equipment:",points:["Head: hard hat — protects against falling objects and impacts.","Eyes: safety glasses — protect against dust, chemicals and flying particles.","Ears: hearing protection (earmuffs or earplugs) — against loud machine noise (85 dB(A) and above).","Hands: gloves — against cuts, heat and chemicals.","Feet: steel-toe safety boots — against crushing and heavy falling objects.","Body: high-visibility (hi-vis) clothing in bright colors — so drivers and other workers can see you."]},{heading:"2. Choosing, fitting and checking PPE",body:"PPE protects only if it suits the hazard, fits the wearer and is in good condition:",points:["Choose PPE from the risk assessment and the SDS of the substances in use, not from habit.","Check every item before use: cracks in the hard hat, scratched lenses, torn gloves or damaged boots.","Gloves must match the hazard: a cut-resistant glove does not stop chemicals, and a chemical glove must be the type named in the SDS.","Replace damaged or worn items, and report missing PPE to your supervisor instead of working without it."]},{heading:"3. Duties of managers and workers",body:"PPE works only as a system shared by managers and workers:",points:["The employer assesses the hazards, provides suitable PPE in the right sizes, trains workers to use it and replaces it when it is damaged.","The worker wears the PPE correctly for the whole task, looks after it and reports any defect.","Hearing protection works only if it is worn the whole time in the noisy area; taking it off, even briefly, lowers the protection.","Legal PPE requirements: follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/ppe_tutorial.mp4",title:"PPE",description:"The correct way to put it on"}],quiz:[{question:"According to European standards, when does wearing hearing protection become mandatory?",options:[{text:"50 dB",correct:!1},{text:"70 dB(A) and above (averaged over an 8-hour workday)",correct:!1},{text:"85 dB(A) and above (averaged over an 8-hour workday)",correct:!0},{text:"120 dB(A) and above (averaged over an 8-hour workday)",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under European Directive 2003/10/EC, when the average noise level over an 8-hour workday reaches 85 dB(A), hearing protection must be worn. OSHA also requires a hearing conservation program at 85 dBA."},{question:"PPE is the last line of defense against injury. What must come first?",options:[{text:"Buying more expensive protective equipment",correct:!1},{text:"Eliminating or reducing the hazard",correct:!0},{text:"Asking workers to work faster to cut exposure time",correct:!1},{text:"Waiting until an injury shows where the hazard is",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before relying on PPE, hazards must be eliminated or reduced through engineering controls and safe work procedures (Hierarchy of Controls); PPE is the last line of defense and is still mandatory in factories."},{question:"Why do workers wear high-visibility (hi-vis) clothing in bright colors?",options:[{text:"To protect the skin from chemicals and heat",correct:!1},{text:"To show which department each worker is in",correct:!1},{text:"To keep the body warm in cold workshops",correct:!1},{text:"So drivers and other workers can see them",correct:!0}],explanation:"Hi-vis clothing in bright colors makes a worker easy to see for drivers and other workers; protection from chemicals and heat comes from other PPE, such as gloves."},{question:"A worker's hard hat has a visible crack but still fits well. What should he do?",options:[{text:"Replace it before the next task, since the crack can fail on impact",correct:!0},{text:"Keep using it, because it still fits and the crack is small",correct:!1},{text:"Cover the crack with tape and use it until the end of the month",correct:!1},{text:"Wear it backwards so the crack faces away from the work",correct:!1}],explanation:"Damaged PPE must not be used: a cracked shell can fail on impact. Report it and replace it before the work continues."},{question:"A worker is going to pour a corrosive cleaning chemical. How do you choose his gloves?",options:[{text:"Use the glove type that the SDS names for that chemical",correct:!0},{text:"Any thick work gloves, since they protect against heat and cuts",correct:!1},{text:"Cotton gloves, because they are comfortable and soak up splashes",correct:!1},{text:"No gloves at all, since washing the hands afterwards is enough",correct:!1}],explanation:"The SDS (section 8) names the protection for each substance. A cut-resistant or cotton glove does not stop chemicals, and cotton holds them against the skin."},{question:"In a noisy area, a worker takes out his earplugs now and then to talk. What is the problem?",options:[{text:"Each time, the noise reaches his ears and his protection drops",correct:!0},{text:"There is no problem as long as he wears them most of the shift",correct:!1},{text:"Earplugs only matter at the end of the shift, when he is tired",correct:!1},{text:"It is only about comfort; noise harms hearing only when it hurts",correct:!1}],explanation:"Hearing protection works only if it is worn the whole time in the noisy area; taking it off, even briefly, lets the noise reach the ears. Use agreed hand signals or step out of the noisy area to talk."}],steps:[{title:"Know the hazard",detail:"Before the task, read the risk assessment, the work instructions and the SDS, and find out what could hit, cut, burn, splash or deafen you."},{title:"Select the right PPE",detail:"Take the equipment the task requires for each part of the body; ask your supervisor if you are not sure."},{title:"Inspect it before use",detail:"Check each item for damage, the right size and a good fit; do not use damaged PPE.",critical:!0},{title:"Wear it correctly the whole time",detail:"Fasten the chin strap, insert earplugs fully and fit the glasses close to the face; keep everything on while you are in the hazard area.",critical:!0},{title:"Remove, clean and store",detail:"Take off contaminated PPE without touching the outside with bare skin, clean or dispose of it as instructed, store it clean and dry and report any defect."}],warnings:["Never work without the PPE the task requires: PPE does not remove the hazard, but without it the hazard reaches your body directly.","Never use damaged PPE or PPE made for another hazard: a cracked hard hat or the wrong glove gives false confidence and can fail when it is needed."],tips:["Mark your own PPE with your name so it fits you and you know its history.","Ask for another size or model if PPE is uncomfortable; PPE that hurts is often taken off.","Supervisors set the example: wear the same PPE as the workers in every hazard area."],activity:{prompt:"You are a supervisor. A new worker is about to grind a metal part. He has a hard hat and gloves but no safety glasses, and he says the job is very short. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop the task until he has safety glasses suitable for grinding, and explain why.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: grinding throws flying particles; short jobs injure eyes too, and a supervisor must not allow work without the required PPE."},{text:"Let him work, because the job is short.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an eye injury takes a split second; a short task does not reduce the hazard."},{text:"Tell him to turn his face away while grinding.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: turning away is not protection; particles bounce and can still reach the eyes."},{text:"Lend him your own ordinary glasses.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: ordinary glasses are not safety glasses; they can shatter and let particles in from the sides."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the paint section, a worker is mixing a solvent. The SDS calls for chemical gloves and eye protection, but the store has run out of chemical gloves and he is wearing cotton gloves. You are the shift supervisor. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Stop the mixing until the right chemical gloves are available, and report the shortage.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the SDS decides the PPE; cotton soaks up the solvent and holds it against the skin."},{text:"Let him continue in cotton gloves and wash his hands afterwards.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: cotton gloves absorb the solvent and increase contact with the skin."},{text:"Give him a second pair of cotton gloves to wear on top.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: more layers of the wrong material do not stop a chemical."}]},{prompt:"While you arrange new gloves, the worker tells you that the solvent soaked through his glove onto his hand a few minutes ago. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Remove the contaminated glove, flush the skin with plenty of running water, follow the first aid in the SDS and call 122 if needed.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: remove the contamination quickly and follow the SDS first aid section; then record the event."},{text:"Wipe his hand with a dry cloth and let him rest.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: wiping leaves the chemical on the skin; flushing with running water is the first aid measure."},{text:"Wait to see whether the skin turns red before doing anything.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: some chemicals harm the skin or pass through it before any sign appears; act at once."}]}],realCase:"A worker in a metal workshop was told to wear safety glasses while cutting steel. He took them off because they kept fogging up and went on working near the grinder. A small metal particle hit his eye, and he needed hospital treatment and several days off work. The investigation found that the workshop had only one model of glasses and no anti-fog type. Management bought glasses in several sizes and anti-fog models, trained supervisors to check PPE at the start of each shift and began recording every near miss."},{id:2,code:"FACT-102",icon:"Cog",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Machine safety",subtitle:"Machine safety and guarding",summary:"Protecting workers from the moving parts of machinery",blocks:[{body:"Industrial machines are extremely powerful and can cause serious injuries or the loss of a limb in a split second."},{heading:"1. Machine guards",body:"These safeguards must be fitted on all hazardous machines:",points:["Fixed guards: to enclose moving belts and gears.","Emergency stop button (E-Stop): must be within the operator's reach and colored red on a yellow background.","Safety stop sensors: the machine stops if a hand or body comes close to the danger zone."]},{heading:"2. Golden rules",body:"Before and during work:",points:["Never remove a machine guard while the machine is running.","Do not wear loose clothing, chains or rings, and tie back long hair, so nothing gets caught in the machine.","If a machine malfunctions, stop it immediately with the E-Stop button, and never put your hand inside the machine without applying the LOTO procedure."]},{heading:"3. Before starting a machine",body:"A short check at the start of each shift prevents many injuries:",points:["Guards are in place and fixed; interlocked doors and safety sensors work.","The E-Stop is within reach and is tested as the work instructions say.","The working area is clean, with no tools, rags or offcuts on the machine.","Only trained and authorized workers operate the machine.","A missing guard or a fault is reported, and the machine is not started until it is fixed."]},{heading:"4. Jams, cleaning and maintenance",body:"Many serious machine injuries happen during non-routine work such as clearing jams, cleaning and adjusting:",points:["Stopping the machine with the E-Stop is not enough for work inside it: apply LOTO (see the electrical safety lesson).","Wait until every moving part has stopped completely; some parts keep turning after the power is cut.","Release or block stored energy, such as springs, pressure and raised parts, as the procedure says.","Use the tools made for clearing jams, never your hands, inside the danger zone.","Refit and check every guard before restarting, and warn everyone that the machine is about to start."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/machine_guard.png",title:"Machine guards",description:"An example of a correct guard"}],quiz:[{question:"Is it acceptable to remove a machine guard while the machine is running?",options:[{text:"Yes, if necessary",correct:!1},{text:"No, it is never acceptable",correct:!0},{text:"Only for cleaning",correct:!1},{text:"Only with the supervisor's permission",correct:!1}],explanation:"Removing a machine guard while the machine is running is one of the most dangerous behaviors. Cleaning and repairs are carried out only after the machine has been stopped and locked out (LOTO)."},{question:"A machine malfunctions while you are working on it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop it at once with the E-Stop button",correct:!0},{text:"Reach in quickly and clear the jam by hand",correct:!1},{text:"Keep it running and tell the supervisor later",correct:!1},{text:"Remove the guard to see what went wrong",correct:!1}],explanation:"If a machine malfunctions, stop it immediately with the E-Stop button, and never put your hand inside the machine without applying the LOTO procedure."},{question:"Why must you not wear loose clothing, chains or rings near machines?",options:[{text:"They make the controls harder to read",correct:!1},{text:"They break the dress code, nothing more",correct:!1},{text:"They can get caught in the machine",correct:!0},{text:"They can damage the machine guards",correct:!1}],explanation:"Loose clothing, chains, rings and long hair can get caught in the machine, and a machine can cause a serious injury in a split second; tie back long hair too."},{question:"A safety sensor keeps stopping a press during production. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Report it and have the cause checked; the sensor stays active",correct:!0},{text:"Cover the sensor with tape until the order is finished",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the sensor off and ask the operator to watch his hands",correct:!1},{text:"Run the press more slowly so the sensor stops it less often",correct:!1}],explanation:"A sensor that stops the machine is either doing its job or faulty; either way a qualified person checks it. Disabling a safety device removes the barrier between the worker and the danger zone."},{question:"You pressed the E-Stop and want to clear a jam inside the machine. Is that enough before you reach in?",options:[{text:"No; the energy must be isolated with LOTO and checked first",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, because the E-Stop cuts all energy from the machine",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if you tell a coworker not to touch the controls",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if the jam is close to the edge of the machine",correct:!1}],explanation:"The E-Stop is for emergencies: someone can reset it, and stored energy may remain. Before any work inside the machine, apply LOTO and verify that it cannot move."},{question:"Which worker is dressed safely to operate a drilling machine?",options:[{text:"Close-fitting sleeves, long hair tied back and no rings",correct:!0},{text:"A loose work coat, buttoned up to keep it clean",correct:!1},{text:"A long scarf tucked into a loose shirt",correct:!1},{text:"Short hair, a watch and a chain bracelet on the wrist",correct:!1}],explanation:"Loose clothing, jewelry and long hair can be caught by rotating parts: sleeves must fit close, long hair is tied back and rings, chains and bracelets are removed."}],steps:[{title:"Stop the machine",detail:"Press the E-Stop or stop it with the normal control, and warn the people nearby.",critical:!0},{title:"Do not reach in",detail:"Keep your hands and body out of the danger zone, even if the machine seems to have stopped.",critical:!0},{title:"Report and isolate",detail:"Tell the supervisor; an authorized person applies LOTO, with a lock and tag on every energy source.",critical:!0},{title:"Verify that nothing can move",detail:"Wait for all parts to stop, release stored energy and, with everyone clear, try the start control to confirm the machine does not move; then return it to off.",critical:!0},{title:"Fix, refit the guards and restart",detail:"Clear the jam with suitable tools, refit and check the guards; each lock is removed only by its owner, and everyone is warned before the restart."}],warnings:["Never bypass, block or tape over a safety sensor or interlock to keep production going: it removes the only barrier between the worker and the moving parts.","Never rely on the E-Stop alone when you work inside a machine: someone can reset it; only LOTO keeps the energy isolated."],tips:["Learn where the E-Stop of each machine is before you need it.","Keep the floor around machines clean and dry so no one slips toward the moving parts.","If a guard makes the work hard, tell the supervisor so the guard can be improved, not removed."],activity:{prompt:"A packing machine keeps jamming. The production manager asks the operator to remove the guard for the rest of the shift so the jams can be cleared faster. You are the operator's supervisor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Refuse: the guard stays on, jams are cleared only after stopping and LOTO, and the cause of the jams is reported for repair.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: production pressure never justifies removing a guard; fix the cause of the jams instead."},{text:"Allow it, but tell the operator to be very careful.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: care does not stop a machine; a moment of distraction is enough for a serious injury."},{text:"Allow it only if a second worker watches the operator.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a watcher cannot stop moving parts in time; the guard is the protection."},{text:"Ask the operator to clear the jams with a stick while the machine runs.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a running machine can pull in the stick and the hand with it; stop and isolate first."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a conveyor line, a worker's sleeve is caught near a roller and he is being pulled toward it. You are the nearest worker. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Hit the nearest E-Stop or pull-cord at once and shout for help.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stopping the machine is the fastest way to stop the pull; then help him."},{text:"Grab the worker and pull him back while the conveyor keeps running.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you can be pulled in too, and the machine keeps pulling; stop it first."},{text:"Run to the office to tell the supervisor.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: seconds count; stop the machine yourself, then call for help."}]},{prompt:"The conveyor has stopped. The worker's arm is still trapped between the roller and the belt, and he is bleeding. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Keep the machine stopped and isolated, call 115 and 122, control the bleeding with pressure and do not restart or reverse the conveyor.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a restart or reverse can cause more injury; trained rescuers and maintenance free the arm under control while you control the bleeding."},{text:"Run the conveyor in reverse to free the arm quickly.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an uncontrolled movement can worsen the injury; leave the release to 115 and the maintenance team."},{text:"Remove the guard and pull the arm out by force.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: pulling a trapped limb can tear tissue and increase bleeding; wait for trained rescuers while you control the bleeding."}]}],realCase:"In a food factory, a worker saw product stuck inside a mixer. The mixer had stopped but had not been isolated. He opened the cover and reached in. Another worker, who did not know anyone was at the mixer, pressed start, and the first worker's hand was badly injured. The investigation found that the cover interlock had been disabled weeks earlier and that no one used LOTO for short cleaning jobs. The company repaired the interlock, gave every worker a personal lock and made LOTO part of every cleaning and jam-clearing task."},{id:3,code:"FACT-103",icon:"PlugZap",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Electrical safety",subtitle:"Industrial electrical safety (LOTO)",summary:"Electrical hazards and the lockout/tagout (LOTO) system",blocks:[{body:"Electricity in factories runs at high voltage; an electric shock can cause death or severe burns."},{heading:"1. Lockout/tagout system (LOTO)",body:"This is the most important procedure before repairing or cleaning machinery:",points:["Lockout: isolate the main power source and apply your personal lock to it.",'Tagout: attach a tag that reads "Work in progress — do not switch on", with your name and the date.',"The key stays only with the person doing the repair; before starting, verify that the machine is truly de-energized."]},{heading:"2. Cable inspection",body:"Always check:",points:["Do not use cut cables or cables with exposed wires.","Electricity and water do not mix; do not use electrical equipment in wet areas."]},{heading:"3. The LOTO sequence",body:"LOTO controls every energy source, not only electricity (pressure, springs, moving parts, heat):",points:["Prepare: identify every energy source of the machine from its procedure.","Notify: tell the operators and everyone affected that the machine will be shut down.","Shut down and isolate: stop the machine normally, then open the isolating switches and close the valves.","Lock and tag: each worker puts on his own personal lock and a tag with his name and the date.","Release stored energy: discharge, bleed or block it as the procedure says.","Verify: with everyone clear, try to start the machine and test for voltage, then return the controls to off."]},{heading:"4. Everyday electrical hazards",body:"Many electrical accidents start with small faults that were ignored:",points:["Do not overload sockets or plug extension cords into each other.","Keep the space in front of electrical panels clear and the panel doors closed.","Only qualified electricians open panels or repair wiring; everyone else reports the fault.","Report heat, a burning smell, sparks or a breaker that keeps tripping at once.","Never touch a person who may still be in contact with electricity: cut the power first (see the first aid lesson)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/loto_safety.mp4",title:"LOTO",description:"How do we use the lock?"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the purpose of LOTO?",options:[{text:"Cleaning",correct:!1},{text:"Isolating and locking out energy before repairs",correct:!0},{text:"Increasing machine speed",correct:!1},{text:"Locking the workshop door at the end of the shift",correct:!1}],explanation:"The LOTO system is used so that no one can switch the power back on while a worker is working on the machine (OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147)."},{question:"You have applied your lock and tag. What must you do before starting the repair?",options:[{text:"Give the key to a coworker for safekeeping",correct:!1},{text:"Switch the machine on briefly to test the lock",correct:!1},{text:"Verify that the machine is truly de-energized",correct:!0},{text:"Start at once, since the lock alone makes it safe",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before starting, verify that the machine is truly de-energized; the key stays only with the person doing the repair, so no one can switch the power back on."},{question:"You find a cable that is cut and has exposed wires. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Use it only in dry areas",correct:!1},{text:"Do not use it",correct:!0},{text:"Wrap it in tape and keep using it",correct:!1},{text:"Use it for light tools only",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not use cut cables or cables with exposed wires; an electric shock can cause death or severe burns."},{question:"Three workers will repair the same machine. How should it be locked out?",options:[{text:"Each worker puts his own lock on the isolator, using a multi-lock hasp",correct:!0},{text:"One lock for the whole team, with the key kept by the supervisor",correct:!1},{text:"One worker locks it and tells the others when it is safe to start",correct:!1},{text:"No locks are needed if the three of them work together the whole time",correct:!1}],explanation:"Each worker is protected only by his own lock. With a multi-lock hasp, the machine cannot be started until the last worker has removed his lock."},{question:"A machine is switched off at its control panel. Is it safe to start repairing it?",options:[{text:"No; isolate and lock the main source, then verify zero energy",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, if the control panel shows that the machine is off",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if you put a warning sign on the control panel",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if the repair takes only a few minutes",correct:!1}],explanation:"A control button can be switched on by someone else. Before a repair, the main energy source is isolated and locked, and the absence of energy is verified."},{question:"You notice a burning smell and a hot cover on an electrical panel. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Keep people away and report it at once for safe isolation",correct:!0},{text:"Open the panel door to find out which wire is burning",correct:!1},{text:"Cool the hot panel cover down with a wet cloth",correct:!1},{text:"Keep working and tell the supervisor at the end of the shift",correct:!1}],explanation:"Heat and a burning smell are warning signs of an electrical fault. Do not open or touch the panel; keep people away and report it so that a qualified electrician isolates the power. If there is fire, raise the alarm and call 115."}],steps:[{title:"Stay clear",detail:"Do not touch damaged cables, wet equipment or a panel that is sparking or hot; keep other people away.",critical:!0},{title:"Cut the power only if it is safe",detail:"Switch off at the breaker or isolator only if you can reach it without touching the faulty equipment or standing in water.",critical:!0},{title:"Report and mark",detail:"Tell the supervisor and the electrician, and mark the equipment as out of service."},{title:"Repair only under LOTO",detail:"Only a qualified person repairs it, after isolating, locking, tagging and verifying that no energy remains.",critical:!0},{title:"Fire or injury: call for help",detail:"If there is a fire or someone is hurt, raise the alarm, call 115 and 122 and never use water on live equipment.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never remove another person's lock or tag, even if the work seems finished: that worker may still be inside the machine.","Never work on equipment that is only switched off at its control button: someone else can switch it on; isolate and lock the main source."],tips:["Keep a personal lock with a single key that only you hold.","Inspect the cables and plugs of portable tools before each shift.","Electricians check their voltage tester on a known live source before and after each test."],activity:{prompt:"At the end of the shift, an electrician finds a lock and tag on a conveyor isolator. The worker who put them on has gone home, and production wants the conveyor for the night shift. What should happen?",options:[{text:"The lock stays: the owner is contacted, and if he cannot come, the supervisor follows the site procedure for removing a lock after checking that no one is at risk.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only the owner removes his lock; the controlled exception procedure is used only after confirming that the machine is clear and safe."},{text:"Cut the lock, since the worker is no longer on site.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the worker may have left the machine in an unsafe state or someone may be inside; removal follows a controlled procedure."},{text:"Start the conveyor from the local control and leave the lock on.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: bypassing an isolation defeats LOTO and can injure anyone working on the machine."},{text:"Remove the tag but leave the lock in place.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the tag says who isolated the machine and why; removing it hides the warning and makes nothing safer."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the packing area, water from a leaking pipe is spreading toward an extension cord that powers a sealing machine. A worker is about to step into the water to unplug it. You are the supervisor. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Stop him and switch off the circuit at the breaker, which you can reach from a dry place.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: water and electricity do not mix; cut the power from a safe, dry point before anyone goes near."},{text:"Let him unplug it quickly, as long as his hands are dry.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: standing in water near live equipment can cause an electric shock, even with dry hands."},{text:"Throw a towel over the water and carry on working.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the hazard remains; the power must be cut and the leak stopped."}]},{prompt:"The power is off. A worker who touched the sealing machine earlier says he felt a tingle in his hand but feels fine now. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Send him for a medical check (call 122 if he has any symptoms) and keep the machine out of service until an electrician checks it under LOTO.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: everyone who has had an electric shock is checked medically, and the machine stays out of service until it is inspected."},{text:"Nothing more, since he feels fine.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: everyone who has had an electric shock needs a medical check, even if they feel fine."},{text:"Switch the power back on to see whether the machine is still faulty.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: testing by switching on exposes everyone again; an electrician checks it under LOTO."}]}],realCase:"Two technicians were repairing a pump. One of them switched it off at the control panel but did not lock the isolator. While they worked, an operator from the next shift saw that the pump was off and switched it on to keep production going. One technician received an electric shock and fell, and he was treated in hospital. The investigation found that the factory had no personal locks and that its LOTO procedure existed only on paper. The factory bought personal locks and tags, trained all technicians and supervisors and began checking LOTO on every repair."},{id:4,code:"FIRE-104",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers — complete training",subtitle:"Fire extinguisher — complete training (PASS)",summary:"A detailed look at extinguisher types, fire classes and the PASS technique",blocks:[{body:"A fire extinguisher is a key piece of equipment for putting out small fires in their early stage. In a factory, every employee must know how to use an extinguisher quickly and correctly. Knowing the types of extinguishers and the classes of fire will protect your life and the lives of your coworkers. A portable extinguisher discharges for only a few seconds, so it must be aimed correctly. If the fire grows or the smoke becomes heavy, leave the extinguisher, get out and call 115."},{heading:"1. Fire classes under NFPA",body:"Under the American system (NFPA), fires are divided into 5 classes, and each class requires a suitable extinguisher. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.",points:["Class A (ordinary solids): wood, paper, fabric, plastic. Extinguisher: water, foam, ABC powder.","Class B (flammable liquids and gases): gasoline, kerosene, oil, alcohol, gas. Liquids: CO₂, ABC powder or foam; for alcohol and other polar solvents, CO₂, dry chemical or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam, because ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop. (Never use water!)","Class C (energized electrical equipment): switches, transformers, electrical panels, generators. Extinguisher: CO₂, ABC powder, clean agent. (Do not use water or foam unless the label shows a Class C rating or an electrical-test marking, as on some water-mist units!)","Class D (metals): combustible metals — magnesium, titanium, sodium and aluminum powder. Extinguisher: only a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal. (Water can cause an explosion!)","Class K (kitchen): cooking oils and fats. Extinguisher: wet chemical. (Water spreads burning oil!) — under EN 2 this is Class F."]},{heading:"2. Types of extinguishers in factories",body:"Color is not the fire class. Under BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols. Classes below are in NFPA letters:",points:["Water extinguisher: all red, no band — for Class A only (wood, paper). Never use it on electrical equipment or oil!","CO₂ extinguisher: black band in the UK — for Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). It releases a very cold gas and the horn becomes extremely cold; hold only the handle, never the horn, or you will get a cold burn! CO₂ has a short range and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires: start from the distance on its label and move closer only as the fire gets smaller. In small rooms, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ reduces the oxygen level.","ABC dry powder extinguisher: blue band in the UK — for Classes A, B and C (not D or K). It is the most common type. It discharges a white powder; drawbacks: it reduces visibility and contaminates equipment.","Foam extinguisher (AFFF): cream band in the UK — for Classes A and B. It spreads a foam blanket over the liquid and cuts off the oxygen. Do not use it on electrical equipment!","Clean agent extinguisher: its rating depends on the model, so read the label (often B and C in NFPA letters; some models also A) — for sensitive equipment and server rooms. It leaves no residue and does not damage electronic equipment.","Special extinguisher (Class D): for combustible metals in factories — its agent must be listed on the label for the metal in use. Never put water on burning metal!"]},{heading:"3. The PASS technique — in detail",body:"Before you use an extinguisher, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door. PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers; Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions:",points:["P = Pull: pull out the safety pin. This pin prevents the extinguisher from being discharged by accident.","A = Aim: aim the extinguisher nozzle at the base of the fire — not at the flames! The fuel of the fire is at the base.","S = Squeeze: squeeze the extinguisher handles firmly to discharge the agent.","S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side across the base of the fire until the fire is completely out; then keep watching the area so the fire does not reignite."]},{heading:"4. Monthly extinguisher inspection",body:"Visual check every month, by the person in charge (NFPA 10):",points:["Pressure gauge, if there is one: the needle must be in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.","Safety pin and tamper seal: must be in place and unbroken.","Nozzle: must not be broken or blocked.","Extinguisher body: must not be rusted, cracked or damaged.","Inspection tag and label: the tag shows the date of the last inspection; the label is readable and faces out; the extinguisher is in its place, visible and easy to reach.","Note: keep these separate. Inspection and maintenance once a year by a qualified technician; recharge after any use, even a short one; 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years; hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder) every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers, and every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells. A test date is not a replacement date. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed."]},{heading:"5. Specific hazards in factories",body:"A factory is a high-risk environment for these reasons:",points:["Chemicals: solvents, alcohol, thinner — all of them are Class B.","High-voltage electricity: electrical panels and transformers — Class C (NFPA).","Wood dust and flour dust: in some factories these can cause a dust explosion.","Welding and cutting: welding sparks can start Class A and Class B fires.","Fuel storage: must be in a dedicated room or outside the building, with a suitable extinguisher (foam or powder) nearby.","Placement: mount extinguishers near doors and walkways; the travel distance to a Class A extinguisher must not exceed 23 meters (75 feet), and for liquids 9-15 meters (NFPA 10)."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fire_ext_factory.mp4",title:"Fire extinguishers in factories",description:"The PASS technique and extinguisher types on video"}],quiz:[{question:"In NFPA letters, which fire classes is a CO₂ extinguisher rated for?",options:[{text:"A and D",correct:!1},{text:"B and C",correct:!0},{text:"A and K",correct:!1},{text:"D and K",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ is suitable for flammable liquids (B) and electrical equipment (C), because it leaves no residue and does not conduct electricity. Under EN 2, C means gas fires, and CO₂ is not rated for them."},{question:"In the PASS technique, where should the extinguisher nozzle be aimed?",options:[{text:"At the flames",correct:!1},{text:"At the base of the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Away from the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the smoke near the ceiling",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, because that is where the fuel is."},{question:"Why must water never be poured on a cooking-oil fire (Class K)?",options:[{text:"It has no effect",correct:!1},{text:"Water spreads the burning oil and creates a large flare-up",correct:!0},{text:"The oil soaks up the water, so the fire only burns more slowly",correct:!1},{text:"The water itself burns",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water on hot oil boils instantly and throws the burning oil into the air, creating a large fireball. Use a wet chemical extinguisher or a lid, and turn off the heat."},{question:"Before you use an extinguisher, which situation is a no-go?",options:[{text:"The fire is between you and your only exit",correct:!0},{text:"The label shows the class of the burning material",correct:!1},{text:"A coworker has raised the alarm and called 115",correct:!1},{text:"You were trained in PASS during the last drill",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the fire is between you and your only exit, it can trap you if it grows. Every go/no-go answer must be yes: small fire, right extinguisher, exit behind you, alarm raised and 115 called, trained, and no smoke or heat blocking the way out."},{question:"How far from the fire should you be when you start the discharge?",options:[{text:"At the distance on the label, moving closer as the fire gets smaller",correct:!0},{text:"Always at one fixed distance, whatever the extinguisher type",correct:!1},{text:"As close as possible, so that none of the agent is wasted",correct:!1},{text:"So far away that you cannot feel any heat at all",correct:!1}],explanation:"There is no single distance for every extinguisher: the range depends on the model and the agent, so the label and the manufacturer's instructions decide. Move closer only as the fire gets smaller, and keep your exit behind you."},{question:"A small fire starts in a deep fryer in the staff canteen. Which action is correct?",options:[{text:"Turn off the heat if it is safe and use the wet chemical extinguisher as its label says",correct:!0},{text:"Use the water extinguisher and aim at the base of the flames in the fryer",correct:!1},{text:"Use the CO₂ extinguisher, because it does not conduct electricity",correct:!1},{text:"Carry the fryer outside so the oil can burn out in the open air",correct:!1}],explanation:"A cooking-oil fire is Class K (EN F): use wet chemical as its label says, and turn off the heat if it is safe. Water throws burning oil into the air, CO₂ is not rated for cooking-oil fires, and moving a burning fryer spills the oil. If it does not go out at once, get out and call 115."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm first",detail:"Shout to warn others, press the fire alarm and make sure someone calls 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Do the go/no-go check",detail:"Use an extinguisher only if every answer is yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is right for it, your exit is behind you, the alarm is raised and 115 called, you are trained, and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If any answer is no, get out.",critical:!0},{title:"Read the label and take position",detail:"Check the class symbols and the operating instructions on the label; start from the distance the label gives, with your exit behind you.",critical:!0},{title:"Use PASS",detail:"Pull the pin, aim at the base of the fire, squeeze the handle and sweep across the base. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.",critical:!0},{title:"If it does not go out at once, get out",detail:"Stop, leave, close the door behind you and go to the assembly point to brief the 115 team.",critical:!0},{title:"After use",detail:"Keep watching the area for reignition, report the use and take the extinguisher out of service for recharge, even after a short discharge."}],warnings:["Never use water or CO₂ on burning cooking oil, and never put out a gas flame unless the gas can be stopped safely: water throws burning oil, and unburned gas can build up and explode.","Never fight a fire that is between you and your exit: if it grows, you are trapped.","Never hold the horn of a CO₂ extinguisher: it becomes extremely cold and causes cold burns."],tips:["Learn where the extinguishers in your area are and which classes their labels show.","In drills, say the go/no-go check aloud before you practice PASS.","Report a used, damaged or missing extinguisher at once so it is recharged or replaced.","Keep extinguishers visible and unblocked; never stack boxes or pallets in front of them."],activity:{prompt:"A small fire starts in a waste bin of paper and cardboard next to a welding bench. You are trained, the alarm has been raised and a coworker is calling 115. Near you are a CO₂ extinguisher and an ABC powder extinguisher, and your exit is behind you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Use the ABC powder extinguisher with PASS, aiming at the base, and leave if the fire does not go out at once.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: every go/no-go answer is yes, and ABC powder is rated for Class A; CO₂ is not for deep-seated paper fires."},{text:"Use the CO₂ extinguisher, because it leaves no residue.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: CO₂ is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires, and the fire can come back; ABC powder is rated for Class A."},{text:"Throw a bucket of water over the bin and the welding bench.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the welding equipment may be live, and water and electricity do not mix; use the extinguisher rated for the fire."},{text:"Aim the extinguisher at the top of the flames and the smoke.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: aim at the base, where the fuel is; aiming at the flames wastes the short discharge."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the paint store, a small fire starts in a tray of thinner. The door you came in by is on the far side of the fire, and a second exit is behind you. A foam extinguisher rated for Class B is next to you, and you are trained. The alarm has not been raised yet. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Raise the alarm and make sure 115 is called, then use the go/no-go check to decide about the foam extinguisher.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm comes first; the extinguisher is used only if every go/no-go answer is yes."},{text:"Fight the fire at once and raise the alarm only if it grows.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the alarm comes before firefighting; if the attempt fails, others lose the time they need to get out."},{text:"Pour water on the thinner to cool it down.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water is not for flammable liquid fires; it can spread the burning liquid."}]},{prompt:"The alarm is sounding and 115 has been called. You discharge the foam at the base of the fire, but the flames spread across the floor toward drums of solvent and smoke is building up. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Stop, leave by the exit behind you, close the door and report at the assembly point where the drums are.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the fire did not go out at once and is no longer small; closing the door slows the spread, and the 115 team needs to know about the drums."},{text:"Fetch a second extinguisher and keep fighting the fire.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the fire is growing and smoke is building up; the go/no-go answer is now no."},{text:"Roll the solvent drums away from the fire before you leave.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: staying near a growing fire puts your life at risk; leave and tell the 115 team where the drums are."}]}],realCase:"In a carpentry workshop, sawdust under a machine caught fire at the end of the day. A worker grabbed the nearest extinguisher, a CO₂ unit, without reading its label. The flames went down, then came back from the smoldering dust as soon as the gas cleared. A second worker had already raised the alarm and called 115; he brought an ABC powder extinguisher, checked that their exit was behind them and put the fire out. Both then left and waited for the 115 team outside. Afterward, the workshop marked each extinguisher point with the classes it covers and practiced the go/no-go check in drills."},{id:5,code:"AID-105",icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#db2777",title:"First aid and CPR",subtitle:"First aid and CPR — complete training",summary:"Complete training in first aid, CPR and emergency response in factories",blocks:[{body:"First aid is the initial help given to an injured or sick person before medical teams arrive. In factories the risk of injury is very high — from cuts and burns to electric shock and falls from height. Every employee must know how to call the ambulance (122), perform CPR, stop bleeding and protect the casualty until the ambulance arrives. This lesson follows the AHA 2025 guidelines and Stop the Bleed."},{heading:"1. Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) — in detail",body:"CPR is a life-saving procedure used when a person's heart stops and normal breathing is absent. Follow these steps:",points:["1. Scene safety: before anything else, make sure the area is safe (no live electricity, fire or gas), so you do not become a second casualty.",'2. Responsiveness: tap the casualty firmly on the shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you okay? Can you hear me?"',"3. Call: if there is no response and the person is not breathing or is only gasping, call 122 immediately (or have someone else call) and ask for an AED to be brought.","4. Chest compressions: place the heel of one hand on the center of the chest (the lower half of the breastbone) and the other hand on top. Give 30 hard, fast compressions: depth 5-6 cm, rate 100-120 compressions per minute and let the chest recoil fully.","5. Rescue breaths: tilt the head back and lift the chin, pinch the nose and give 2 breaths (each about 1 second, until the chest rises). If you cannot or are unwilling to give breaths, continue chest compressions only.","6. Continue (30:2): keep repeating 30 compressions and 2 breaths until the ambulance arrives, an AED is ready or the casualty starts breathing normally.","7. Using the AED: as soon as it arrives, switch it on, attach the pads to the bare chest and follow the device's voice prompts; do not touch the casualty while a shock is delivered."]},{heading:"2. Bleeding control (Stop the Bleed)",body:"Severe bleeding can kill a person within a short time, so call 122 and take these steps quickly:",points:["Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press hard with both hands. If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another one on top and keep pressing.","Wound packing: if the wound is deep (for example in the thigh, armpit or neck), pack clean cloth or hemostatic gauze into the wound and press hard. Do not elevate the injured limb and do not look for pressure points.","Tourniquet: if bleeding from an arm or leg is life-threatening, apply a tourniquet early, 5-7 cm above the wound (toward the body), tighten it firmly, write down the time and never loosen it.","Embedded object: if a piece of metal or glass is embedded in the wound, never pull it out! That would increase the bleeding. Apply pressure around it and stabilize it."]},{heading:"3. Treating burns",body:"Burns in factories are dangerous; treat them as follows, and call 122 for large burns or burns to the face or hands:",points:["Thermal burns: immediately cool the burn under cool running water (not ice water) for 20 minutes. Quickly remove rings and watches before the area swells.","Chemical burns: remove contaminated clothing and flush the skin with plenty of running water for 20 minutes or longer, and check the substance's SDS.","Electrical burns: first, cut off the power! Then cool the burn as you would a thermal burn and call 122, because electricity can cause damage to the heart and inside the body.","Caution: do not burst burn blisters, and do not use ice, butter or grease, toothpaste or ointments without medical advice."]},{heading:"4. Fractures and spinal injuries",body:"After a fall from height or when a fracture is suspected:",points:["Splinting: if a fracture is suspected, do not move the injured limb. Immobilize it in the position found with a piece of wood or cardboard to reduce pain.","Protecting the back and neck: if someone has fallen from height, do not move them unless the location is dangerous (for example, fire), because moving them can cause paralysis. Keep the head still and call 122.","Cooling: to reduce swelling, wrap ice in a piece of cloth and place it on the painful area for 20 minutes; do not put ice directly on the skin."]},{heading:"5. Electric shock and rescue",body:"Electricity can stop the heart; follow these procedures:",points:["Cut off the power immediately: do not touch the casualty until the power has been cut off, because the current will pass through you too.","Safe separation: if you cannot cut off the power and the voltage is low, use a dry piece of wood or plastic to move the wire away from the casualty while standing on something dry. Do not go near high-voltage lines; call 115.","Medical check: everyone who has received an electric shock, even if they feel fine, must go to the hospital for a heart check; call 122."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/cpr_factory.mp4",title:"CPR and first aid",description:"CPR training and helping injured workers in factories"}],quiz:[{question:"In CPR, what should the chest compression rate be per minute?",options:[{text:"50-70",correct:!1},{text:"80-90",correct:!1},{text:"100-120",correct:!0},{text:"140-160",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to AHA 2025, the compression rate is 100-120 compressions per minute, at a depth of 5-6 cm for adults, so that blood reaches the brain."},{question:"If a sharp object is embedded in a wound, what will you do?",options:[{text:"I pull it out",correct:!1},{text:"I leave it in place and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I keep my hands away from it and do nothing",correct:!1},{text:"I wash it with water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Pulling the object out will increase the bleeding. It must stay in place and be stabilized until a doctor removes it at the hospital."},{question:"If someone has received an electric shock, what is the first thing you do?",options:[{text:"I grab their hand",correct:!1},{text:"I start CPR",correct:!1},{text:"I cut off the power",correct:!0},{text:"I pour water on them",correct:!1}],explanation:"The power source must be cut off first so the current does not pass through you too; then call 122 and start CPR if needed."},{question:"A worker received an electric shock from a machine. The power has been cut. He does not respond and is not breathing normally. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 122, start CPR and send someone for the AED",correct:!0},{text:"Wait until he wakes up before calling anyone",correct:!1},{text:"Pour cold water on his face to wake him up",correct:!1},{text:"Place him in the recovery position and wait",correct:!1}],explanation:"With the power cut, the scene is safe. A person who does not respond and is not breathing normally is in cardiac arrest: call 122, start CPR and use the AED as soon as it arrives."},{question:"An untrained coworker is willing to help but does not want to give rescue breaths. What should he do?",options:[{text:"Give Hands-Only CPR: continuous compressions until help arrives",correct:!0},{text:"Do nothing, because CPR without breaths does not help at all",correct:!1},{text:"Give only rescue breaths, because they are easier to learn",correct:!1},{text:"Press gently on the chest so that he does not cause harm",correct:!1}],explanation:"Untrained or unwilling rescuers give Hands-Only CPR: hard, fast compressions without breaths (AHA 2025). Gentle presses do not move enough blood."},{question:"A worker fell from a scaffold. He is conscious, says his neck hurts and the area is safe. What is correct?",options:[{text:"Keep him still, support his head and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"Help him stand up to check whether he can walk",correct:!1},{text:"Put him in the recovery position to protect his airway",correct:!1},{text:"Carry him to the office so he can lie on a sofa",correct:!1}],explanation:"After a fall with neck pain, a spinal injury is suspected: do not move him unless the place is unsafe, keep the head still and call 122. The recovery position is not used with a suspected neck or back injury."}],steps:[{title:"Make the scene safe",detail:"Check for live electricity, fire, gas, moving machines or chemicals; stop the machine or cut the power only if it is safe. Do not become a second casualty.",critical:!0},{title:"Check and call",detail:"Tap and shout. If there is no response and no normal breathing, call 122 (and 115 if there is fire, electricity, entrapment or chemicals), and send someone for the AED and the first aid kit.",critical:!0},{title:"Start CPR",detail:"Push hard and fast in the center of the chest: 100–120 compressions per minute, 5–6 cm deep. If you are trained and willing, give 30 compressions and 2 breaths; otherwise give Hands-Only CPR.",critical:!0},{title:"Use the AED",detail:"Switch it on as soon as it arrives and follow its voice prompts; nobody touches the casualty during the shock.",critical:!0},{title:"Stop severe bleeding",detail:"Press hard on the wound, pack deep wounds and apply a tourniquet early for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg; note the time.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect and hand over",detail:"Do not move a person after a fall unless the place is unsafe; keep them warm, give nothing by mouth and tell the ambulance team what happened and what you did."}],warnings:["Never touch a casualty who may still be in contact with electricity: cut the power first, or the current will pass through you too.","Never move a casualty after a fall from height unless the place is dangerous or CPR is needed: moving can cause paralysis.","Never give food, drink or medicine to an unconscious or badly injured person: an unconscious person can choke, and treatment is decided by the medical team."],tips:["Know who the trained first aiders on your shift are and where the first aid kit and the AED are kept.","Keep the factory address and a nearby landmark written next to the phone so the 122 call is quick.","Practice CPR on a training manikin regularly; the skill fades without practice.","Restock the first aid kit after every use."],activity:{prompt:"A worker collapses on the production floor. He does not respond and is only gasping. A coworker has gone to call 122, and there is an AED in the corridor. You are trained in CPR. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Start chest compressions at once and ask another person to bring the AED.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: gasping is not normal breathing; start CPR immediately and use the AED as soon as it arrives."},{text:"Wait for the ambulance, since he is still breathing.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: gasping is a sign of cardiac arrest, not normal breathing; every minute without CPR lowers his chance of survival."},{text:"Put him in the recovery position and watch him.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the recovery position is for a person who is breathing normally; gasping needs CPR."},{text:"Leave him and fetch the AED yourself before doing anything else.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you are not alone; send someone else for the AED and start compressions at once."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the cutting area, a glass sheet breaks and deeply cuts a worker's thigh. Blood is spurting and pooling quickly on the floor. You have the first aid kit, which includes a tourniquet. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Have someone call 122 and apply the tourniquet above the wound at once, because the bleeding is life-threatening.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: spurting blood that pools quickly is life-threatening limb bleeding; apply the tourniquet early, 5–7 cm above the wound, and note the time."},{text:"Raise the leg and press on a pressure point in the groin.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: elevation and pressure points are no longer taught; use direct pressure, wound packing and a tourniquet."},{text:"Wash the wound with water first so you can see it clearly.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: washing wastes time while the casualty loses blood; control the bleeding first."}]},{prompt:"The tourniquet is on and the bleeding has stopped. The worker is pale and says the tourniquet hurts a lot. The ambulance has not arrived yet. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Leave the tourniquet tight, keep him lying down and warm, and tell the ambulance team the time it was applied.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a tourniquet hurts when it works; the first aider never loosens it, and the time helps the hospital team."},{text:"Loosen the tourniquet a little so he feels better.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: loosening can restart severe bleeding; only medical staff remove a tourniquet."},{text:"Walk him to the canteen so he can rest while you wait.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: walking can restart the bleeding and worsen shock; keep him lying down."}]}],realCase:"A maintenance worker collapsed next to a compressor. A coworker saw that he was not breathing normally, shouted for help and started chest compressions. A second worker called 122 and gave the factory address and the gate to use, while a third brought the AED from the corridor. The AED advised a shock, and the team continued CPR until the ambulance team took over. In the review afterward, the factory found that the quick start came from the CPR training given on every shift and from clear signs showing where the AED was. It added the gate number to the emergency call card."},{id:6,code:"CHEM-106",icon:"FlaskConical",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Chemicals (SDS)",subtitle:"Chemical safety and labeling",summary:"Handling chemicals and recognizing their hazards",blocks:[{body:"Chemicals in factories can cause skin burns, poisoning, fires or explosions."},{heading:"1. What is an SDS?",body:"The Safety Data Sheet (SDS) gives the information for each substance in 16 sections, including:",points:["The composition of the substance and its hazards.","Which PPE is required (respirator, gloves, eye protection).","First aid if the substance contacts the body, and firefighting measures."]},{heading:"2. GHS pictograms",body:"Some of the symbols found on containers and packaging:",points:["Flame: flammable substance.","Skull and crossbones: acutely toxic, potentially fatal substance.","Corrosion: an acidic or alkaline substance that eats away skin and metals."]},{heading:"3. Reading a container label",body:"Every container of a hazardous chemical must carry a label. Before use, read:",points:["The product name and the supplier, so you can find the right SDS.",'The signal word: "Danger" for the more severe hazards, "Warning" for the less severe ones.',"The pictograms and hazard statements: what the substance can do to you.","The precautionary statements: how to store and handle it, how to protect yourself and what first aid to give.","If a container has no label or the label cannot be read, do not use it; report it."]},{heading:"4. Safe storage and handling",body:"Many chemical accidents start in storage or while pouring from one container to another:",points:["Store incompatible chemicals apart as sections 7 and 10 of the SDS say, for example acids away from alkalis and oxidizers away from flammable materials.","Keep flammable liquids in closed, labeled containers away from heat and sparks.","Never put a chemical in a drink bottle or any unlabeled container.","Never mix chemicals unless the procedure says so: some mixtures release toxic gas.","Use the ventilation and PPE named in the SDS, and wash your hands before eating, drinking or smoking."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/ghs_pictograms.png",title:"SDS",description:"GHS hazard pictograms"}],quiz:[{question:"Where can we find out which respirator is required for a chemical?",options:[{text:"On the signs on the wall",correct:!1},{text:"In the SDS",correct:!0},{text:"Ask a coworker",correct:!1},{text:"No respirator is needed",correct:!1}],explanation:"The SDS provides all the protection information specific to that substance, including the type of PPE required (Section 8)."},{question:"What does the skull and crossbones pictogram on a container mean?",options:[{text:"A flammable substance that catches fire easily",correct:!1},{text:"An acutely toxic, potentially fatal substance",correct:!0},{text:"An acid or alkali that eats away skin and metals",correct:!1},{text:"A substance that harms only the environment",correct:!1}],explanation:"The skull and crossbones marks an acutely toxic, potentially fatal substance; the flame marks a flammable substance and the corrosion pictogram an acid or alkali."},{question:"Besides the hazards and the required PPE, what else does the SDS tell you?",options:[{text:"The price of the substance and its supplier",correct:!1},{text:"The cleaning schedule for the chemical store",correct:!1},{text:"The names of workers allowed to use it",correct:!1},{text:"First aid and firefighting measures",correct:!0}],explanation:"The SDS gives the information for each substance in 16 sections, including its hazards, the PPE required, first aid if the substance contacts the body and firefighting measures."},{question:"You will use a chemical for the first time. What do you do before opening it?",options:[{text:"Read the label and the SDS for its hazards and the PPE it needs",correct:!0},{text:"Smell the open container briefly to judge how strong it is",correct:!1},{text:"Ask the delivery driver how dangerous the substance is",correct:!1},{text:"Put a little on your skin to check whether it burns",correct:!1}],explanation:"The label and the SDS give the hazards, the PPE, first aid and storage for each substance. Smelling or touching a chemical can poison or burn you."},{question:"Which of these practices is wrong?",options:[{text:"Keeping a cleaning chemical in an empty drink bottle",correct:!0},{text:"Keeping acids and alkalis apart, as the SDS says",correct:!1},{text:"Labeling a new container before filling it",correct:!1},{text:"Closing flammable liquid containers after use",correct:!1}],explanation:"A chemical in a drink bottle can be drunk by mistake and has no hazard label. Chemicals stay in labeled containers, apart from incompatible substances, and flammable liquids are kept closed."},{question:"A large spill of an unknown chemical is giving off fumes in a warehouse. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Leave, keep others out and call 115 from a safe place",correct:!0},{text:"Cover it with sawdust and sweep it up quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Hose it into the drain with plenty of water",correct:!1},{text:"Find out what it is by smelling the vapor",correct:!1}],explanation:"A large, unknown or fuming spill is beyond a worker's spill kit: leave, keep others away and call 115 from a safe place. Sawdust can react with some chemicals, and a drain spreads the hazard."}],steps:[{title:"Warn and keep away",detail:"Warn the people nearby and keep everyone out of the spill and its vapors; move away into fresh air if there are fumes.",critical:!0},{title:"Help anyone contaminated",detail:"Remove contaminated clothing and flush the skin or eyes with plenty of running water; follow the first aid in the SDS and call 122.",critical:!0},{title:"Check the SDS before acting",detail:"Identify the substance and read its hazards, the PPE it needs and the spill measures (section 6 of the SDS)."},{title:"Small spill: contain it only if trained",detail:"With the right PPE and the spill kit, stop the source and contain the spill; keep it out of drains."},{title:"Large, unknown or fuming spill: evacuate",detail:"Leave the area, close the doors if it is safe, call 115 from a safe place and give the team the substance name and its SDS.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never mix chemicals or pour one into another's container: some mixtures produce toxic gas, or heat that can lead to fire or explosion.","Never smell a container to find out what is inside: the label and the SDS identify it, and breathing the vapor can poison you."],tips:["Keep the SDS file where workers can reach it quickly, in a language they understand.","Know where the nearest eyewash station, safety shower and spill kit are.","Before pouring a chemical into a new container, label it with the product name and its hazards."],activity:{prompt:"In the maintenance store, a worker finds an old plastic container of clear liquid with no label. He wants to use it to clean a part because it smells like a solvent. You are the store supervisor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop him, set the container aside safely, mark it as unknown and arrange for it to be identified or disposed of properly.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an unlabeled substance is unknown, and smell does not identify it; it is not used until it is identified."},{text:"Let him use it outdoors, where the vapors can spread.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the liquid could be corrosive, toxic or reactive; fresh air does not make an unknown substance safe."},{text:"Pour it into a labeled solvent can so it gets used up.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: mixing an unknown liquid with a solvent can cause a reaction, and the can would then carry a false label."},{text:"Pour it down the drain to get rid of the risk.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: chemicals in drains can react, start a fire or pollute water; disposal follows the SDS and the official rules."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During cleaning, a worker pours a chlorine bleach cleaner into a bucket that still contains an acid descaler. A sharp smell fills the small washroom and he starts coughing. You are nearby. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Get him and everyone else out into fresh air at once and stop others from going in.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: bleach mixed with acid releases chlorine gas; leaving the area comes first."},{text:"Add water to the bucket to dilute the mixture.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: staying next to the bucket keeps you in the gas; leave first."},{text:"Open the window and finish cleaning with a dust mask from the shelf.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a dust mask does not protect against chlorine gas; leave the area."}]},{prompt:"Outside, the worker is still coughing and says his chest feels tight. The washroom door is closed. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Call 122, keep him at rest in fresh air, tell the ambulance team which products were mixed and keep the washroom closed until it is cleared.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: breathing symptoms after a chemical exposure need medical care, and the team needs to know the products."},{text:"Give him milk to drink to neutralize the gas.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: no home remedies; nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor says so. Call 122."},{text:"Send him back to work once the coughing eases.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: breathing symptoms after a chemical exposure need a medical check, because they can get worse later."}]}],realCase:"A worker needed a small amount of acid for a cleaning job. He poured it into an empty water bottle and left it on a bench. Later, a coworker saw the bottle, thought it was water and almost drank from it; he stopped only because of the smell. The investigation found that the plant had no labeled small containers for decanting. Management supplied labeled containers, banned drink bottles in chemical areas and added label reading to the induction training. Supervisors now check at the start of each shift that every container in their area is labeled."},{id:7,code:"HGHT-107",icon:"Construction",accent:"#0284c7",title:"Working at height",subtitle:"Fall protection and ladders",summary:"Preventing falls from scaffolding, roofs and ladders",blocks:[{body:"Falls from height are one of the leading causes of death in factories and on construction sites."},{heading:"1. Fall protection",body:"Any work at height (OSHA: from 1.2 meters in general industry and from 1.8 meters in construction) requires the following:",points:["Full-body harness: worn correctly and fastened securely.","Lanyard: must be attached to a secure anchor point.","Guardrails: around scaffolding, roofs and open edges."]},{heading:"2. Ladder safety",body:"The 3-point contact rule:",points:["You must always have 3 limbs (two feet and one hand, or two hands and one foot) in contact with the ladder.","The ladder angle must be 4:1 (for every 4 meters of height, the base of the ladder is 1 meter away from the wall)."]},{heading:"3. Planning work at height",body:"Before any work at height, the supervisor plans it in this order:",points:["Avoid working at height where you can, for example by using long-handled tools from the ground.","Prevent falls with guardrails, a complete scaffold or a suitable work platform.","Where a fall cannot be prevented, use a fall arrest system: harness, lanyard and a secure anchor point.","Plan the rescue before the work starts: a worker hanging in a harness needs help quickly.","Only trained workers use harnesses, scaffolds and work platforms."]},{heading:"4. Ladders, scaffolds and openings",body:"Common causes of falls and how to stop them:",points:["Inspect the ladder before use: no cracked rails, missing rungs or broken feet.","Set the ladder on firm, level ground and secure it at the top, or have someone hold the base.","Do not stand on the top rungs and do not overreach; climb down and move the ladder instead.","Use only scaffolds that are complete, with guardrails and toe boards, and checked by a competent person.","Cover or guard floor openings and roof edges, and never step on fragile roof sheets or skylights."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fall_protection.mp4",title:"Working at height",description:"Using a harness"}],quiz:[{question:"What does the 3-point rule on a ladder mean?",options:[{text:"No more than 3 people on the ladder at the same time",correct:!1},{text:"Stay on it for 3 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Always keep 3 limbs in contact with the ladder",correct:!0},{text:"Tie the ladder with 3 ropes",correct:!1}],explanation:"The 3-point rule ensures that the worker keeps their balance and does not fall."},{question:"A ladder reaches 4 meters up a wall. How far from the wall should its base be?",options:[{text:"Half a meter",correct:!1},{text:"1 meter",correct:!0},{text:"2 meters",correct:!1},{text:"4 meters",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ladder angle must be 4:1: for every 4 meters of height, the base of the ladder is 1 meter away from the wall."},{question:"Where must the lanyard of a full-body harness be attached?",options:[{text:"To the worker's own tool belt",correct:!1},{text:"To the ladder the worker is standing on",correct:!1},{text:"To a secure anchor point",correct:!0},{text:"To any nearby pipe or cable",correct:!1}],explanation:"The lanyard must be attached to a secure anchor point, and the full-body harness must be worn correctly and fastened securely."},{question:"A harness and lanyard have stopped a worker's fall. He is unhurt. What happens to the equipment?",options:[{text:"It is taken out of service until a competent person checks it",correct:!0},{text:"It is used again at once, since it worked well",correct:!1},{text:"It is used again after a quick look for torn stitching",correct:!1},{text:"It is given to a worker who works at a lower height",correct:!1}],explanation:"Equipment that has stopped a fall has taken a heavy load and may be damaged inside. It is removed from service and is not used again until a competent person inspects it, following the manufacturer's instructions."},{question:"On a ladder, you need to reach a point a little to one side. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Climb down and move the ladder closer to the point",correct:!0},{text:"Lean out to the side while holding on with one hand",correct:!1},{text:"Step across onto a nearby shelf or machine",correct:!1},{text:"Stand on the top rung so you can reach further",correct:!1}],explanation:"Overreaching moves your weight outside the ladder and makes it slide or tip. Keep three points of contact and move the ladder instead."},{question:"Which is the right order of control when planning work at height?",options:[{text:"Avoid work at height, then prevent falls, then arrest falls",correct:!0},{text:"Use a harness first, then guardrails, then avoid the work",correct:!1},{text:"Use a ladder first, because it is the quickest solution",correct:!1},{text:"Rely on careful workers, then add equipment after a fall",correct:!1}],explanation:"First avoid working at height where possible; if not, prevent falls with guardrails or platforms; only where a fall cannot be prevented, use a harness and lanyard to arrest it, with a rescue plan."}],steps:[{title:"Check the plan",detail:"Know the task, the hazards and the rescue plan; follow the site's work-at-height permit if there is one."},{title:"Inspect your equipment",detail:"Check the harness webbing, stitching and buckles and the lanyard and its hooks; do not use damaged equipment or equipment that has already stopped a fall.",critical:!0},{title:"Put on and adjust the harness",detail:"Fasten every buckle and adjust the straps so the harness fits snugly.",critical:!0},{title:"Connect before you are exposed",detail:"Clip the lanyard to a secure anchor point before you reach the edge, and stay connected the whole time.",critical:!0},{title:"Keep the area below safe",detail:"Put a barrier around the area below, secure tools against falling and keep people out of the drop zone."}],warnings:["Never clip a lanyard to a guardrail, pipe or cable that was not chosen as an anchor point: it may not hold a falling person.","Never leave a fallen worker hanging in a harness while you wait: call 115 and start the rescue plan at once, because hanging in a harness quickly becomes dangerous."],tips:["Carry tools in a tool bag or on a tool lanyard when you climb, so both hands are free.","Stop work at height in strong wind, rain or poor light and tell the supervisor.","Face the ladder when you climb up or down."],activity:{prompt:"A worker must replace a lamp on a high ceiling. The scaffold is in another building, so his coworker suggests standing on a chair placed on a table. You are the supervisor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop the job until a suitable ladder or work platform is available and the work is planned.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: improvised platforms are unstable; the right equipment prevents the fall."},{text:"Allow it if the coworker holds the table.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: holding the table does not make a chair on a table stable; a fall from it can kill."},{text:"Allow it, because the job is short.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: falls happen in seconds, short jobs included."},{text:"Ask him to wear a hard hat and go ahead.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a hard hat does not prevent the fall; use the proper equipment."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Two workers are fixing a roof sheet. One wears a harness but has not clipped on his lanyard, because the anchor point is far from where he is working. You see him walking near the roof edge. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Tell him to move back from the edge at once and stop the work until he can stay connected to a secure anchor.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stop the unsafe act now; the work continues only when he can stay connected the whole time."},{text:"Wait until he finishes, then talk to him about it.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a fall can happen at any moment; stop the unsafe act now."},{text:"Tell him to clip the lanyard to the gutter at the edge.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a gutter is not a tested anchor point and can tear away under the load of a fall."}]},{prompt:"Later, while connected, the same worker slips over the edge. The harness stops his fall and he is hanging below the edge, conscious. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Call 115, start the site rescue plan at once and talk to him to keep him calm; do not go near the edge without fall protection yourself.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: hanging in a harness quickly becomes dangerous, so the rescue starts at once, using the planned method and equipment."},{text:"Climb down to him without a harness and pull him up.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you may fall too; the rescue uses the planned method and proper equipment."},{text:"Leave him hanging until the 115 team arrives, since the harness has stopped the fall.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: hanging in a harness quickly becomes dangerous; start the rescue plan while the team is on the way."}]}],realCase:"A worker climbed a ladder to fix a pipe. The ladder stood on an oily floor, and no one was holding it. As he reached out to one side, the ladder slid and he fell onto the concrete floor and hurt his back. His coworkers did not move him and called 122. The investigation found that the ladder had no anti-slip feet, the floor had not been cleaned and the job had not been planned. The factory replaced its old ladders, added ladder checks to the daily inspection and made supervisors approve every job at height before it starts."},{id:8,code:"CONF-108",icon:"Container",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Confined spaces",subtitle:"Confined space safety",summary:"Working inside tanks, pipes and manholes",blocks:[{body:"Confined spaces are dangerous because the air inside is not refreshed, oxygen levels can drop and deadly gases can build up."},{heading:"1. Main hazards",body:"Before you enter, be aware of:",points:["Oxygen deficiency (below 19.5%): causes asphyxiation.","Toxic gases such as CO (odorless) and H₂S (which knocks out the sense of smell at high concentrations).","Risk of fire and explosion if flammable gases or vapors are present."]},{heading:"2. Entry procedures",body:"These conditions must be in place:",points:["Air testing with a gas monitor before entry and during the work.","An attendant who stays outside, keeps watch on the entrant inside and never enters under any circumstances.","An entry permit, means of communication and a rescue plan must be in place."]},{heading:"3. Where are the confined spaces in a factory?",body:"A confined space is large enough to enter, has limited ways in and out and is not designed for people to stay in:",points:["Tanks, silos, large vessels and pits.","Sewers, manholes, air ducts and large pipes.","Pits under machines and the inside of boilers.","If you are not sure whether a space is confined, treat it as one and ask your supervisor."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/confined_entry.png",title:"Confined spaces",description:"Air testing"}],quiz:[{question:"Is it acceptable for the attendant outside to enter without equipment to rescue a coworker?",options:[{text:"Yes, out of humanity",correct:!1},{text:"No, because they will be overcome too",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, if they hold their breath",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if the coworker is very close to the entrance",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NIOSH studies, a large share of confined-space deaths are people who entered without equipment or training to attempt a rescue. The attendant must call the rescue team and 115."},{question:"When must the air in a confined space be tested with a gas monitor?",options:[{text:"Only once, on the first day of the job",correct:!1},{text:"Only if someone notices an unusual smell",correct:!1},{text:"After the work is finished",correct:!1},{text:"Before entry and during the work",correct:!0}],explanation:"The air is tested before entry and during the work, because oxygen levels can drop and deadly gases can build up; CO is odorless, so smelling the air is not a test."},{question:"Inside a tank, a worker suddenly can no longer smell H₂S. What could this mean?",options:[{text:"The gas has cleared and the air is safe to breathe",correct:!1},{text:"The gas may have knocked out the sense of smell",correct:!0},{text:"The ventilation fan has started to work properly",correct:!1},{text:"The gas has turned into harmless water vapor",correct:!1}],explanation:"At high concentrations H₂S knocks out the sense of smell, so losing the smell does not mean the gas is gone; only a gas monitor shows whether the air is safe."},{question:"You are the supervisor about to sign a confined-space entry permit. Which of these, if missing, means the work must not start?",options:[{text:"A spare pair of gloves for the worker inside",correct:!1},{text:"The rescue plan with its people and equipment",correct:!0},{text:"A written list of every tool that will be taken inside",correct:!1},{text:"A photo of the space taken before the work begins",correct:!1}],explanation:"The permit, means of communication and rescue plan must be ready before entry; an unplanned rescue leads untrained people to go in."},{question:"Which of these is wrong for the attendant to do?",options:[{text:"Staying in contact with the worker inside all the time",correct:!1},{text:"Ordering the worker out when the gas monitor alarms",correct:!1},{text:"Going in briefly to help a worker who feels unwell",correct:!0},{text:"Raising the alarm and calling 115 if the worker collapses",correct:!1}],explanation:"The attendant never goes in; the job is to watch, order the worker out, raise the alarm and call for rescue."},{question:"The air test before entry was good. Why must the air still be tested during the work?",options:[{text:"Because the first test only counts for the supervisor",correct:!1},{text:"Because the work itself can release gases or use up oxygen",correct:!0},{text:"Because the monitor must stay switched on to keep its battery charged",correct:!1},{text:"Because without a second test the permit is only paper",correct:!1}],explanation:"Conditions change during the work: cleaning, welding, paint or sludge can release gases and oxygen can drop, so testing continues."}],steps:[{title:"Before entry: permit and plan",detail:"Nobody enters until the entry permit is signed, the hazards of this space are known, the means of communication work and the rescue plan is ready. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the competent authority.",critical:!0},{title:"Isolate and ventilate",detail:"Lines that bring liquids, gases or steam into the space are shut and locked, moving parts are stopped with lockout/tagout (LOTO), and the space is ventilated before entry.",critical:!0},{title:"Test the air",detail:"Measure oxygen, flammable gases and toxic gases with a gas monitor before entry and keep measuring during the work. If the monitor alarms, everyone gets out at once.",critical:!0},{title:"The attendant stays outside",detail:"The attendant keeps the worker inside in sight and in contact the whole time and never goes in. If anything changes, the attendant orders the worker out.",critical:!0},{title:"If the worker inside collapses",detail:"The attendant does not go in. Raise the alarm, call the rescue team and 115, and use the retrieval line from outside only if the plan provides for it. Only a trained team with breathing apparatus (SCBA) goes in.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never go in to rescue a collapsed worker without the rescue plan, breathing apparatus and training: the untrained rescuer becomes the next victim.","Do not trust your nose: CO has no smell and H₂S can knock out the sense of smell.","Do not rely on an old test or yesterday's test: the air changes during the work."],tips:['Before the job, agree with the attendant on simple signals for "all right", "come out" and "help".',"Keep the entrance open and clear so the worker can get out fast and the rescue team can reach them.","If you feel dizzy, get a headache or feel short of breath inside, get out at once and tell the attendant.","Supervisors: before you sign the permit, check the permit, the gas monitor and the rescue equipment yourself."],activity:{prompt:"You are the attendant at a manhole where a colleague is cleaning a tank. Suddenly he stops answering your calls and you see him lying on the floor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I climb down quickly to pull him out",correct:!1,feedback:"The air that overcame him will overcome you too; a large share of confined-space deaths are people who went in without equipment to attempt a rescue."},{text:"I stay outside, raise the alarm, call the rescue team and 115, and use the retrieval line from outside if the plan provides for it",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this keeps you alive and gets a trained team with breathing apparatus to him quickly."},{text:"I hold my breath and go in for a few seconds",correct:!1,feedback:"You cannot judge the time you need; in air without oxygen or full of toxic gas, a single breath can make you lose consciousness."},{text:"I tie a wet cloth over my mouth and nose and go in",correct:!1,feedback:"A wet cloth does not protect against CO, H₂S or lack of oxygen; only breathing apparatus and training do."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:'A team will clean the inside of a water tank. The permit is ready, but the gas monitor is missing because it was taken to another site. The supervisor says: "It only held water, start now." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I start without a test, since it only held water",correct:!1,feedback:"Rust, decaying material and nearby lines can use up oxygen or release gases, even in a water tank."},{text:"I stop the job until a working gas monitor arrives and the air is tested",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: testing before entry is a condition of the permit; without a test nobody knows whether the air is safe."},{text:"I send the youngest worker in first to try the air",correct:!1,feedback:"A person is not a gas monitor; this puts a life at risk."},{text:"I open the hatch for a while, then go in without a test",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the hatch does not show that there is enough oxygen or that the gas has gone; only a test shows that."}]},{prompt:"The monitor arrives, the air test is good and a worker goes in. Some time later the monitor on his chest alarms and he says he feels dizzy. As the attendant, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell him to finish quickly and then come out",correct:!1,feedback:"An alarm means the air is no longer safe; every extra minute inside adds to the danger."},{text:"I order him out at once, help him from outside, stop all entry and report it so the cause is found",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm is the signal to get out; nobody goes back in until the cause is found and the air is safe again."},{text:"I go in to read his monitor myself",correct:!1,feedback:"The attendant never goes in; going in puts two people in danger."},{text:"I silence the alarm because the monitor may be faulty",correct:!1,feedback:"Treat every alarm as real; the monitor is checked outside after everyone is out."}]}],realCase:"During the cleaning of a tank, the attendant noticed that the worker inside was answering slowly and his words were unclear. She ordered him out at once and helped him from the entrance without going in. He recovered in the fresh air and was checked by a doctor. The gas monitor at the entrance showed that the oxygen inside had dropped. The investigation found that a nitrogen line into the tank had not been locked shut. The factory added a rule: every line into a confined space is shut, locked and checked before the permit is signed."},{id:9,code:"NOIS-109",icon:"Ear",accent:"#db2777",title:"Noise and hearing protection",subtitle:"Hearing conservation",summary:"Protection against the noise of heavy machinery",blocks:[{body:"Deafness or hearing loss caused by factory noise develops gradually and cannot be cured."},{heading:"1. Noise levels",body:"When does noise become a hazard?",points:["85 dB(A): the threshold at which the hazard begins, for an 8-hour workday.","100 dB(A): safe without protection for only 15 minutes a day (NIOSH).","Warning sign: if you notice ringing in your ears (tinnitus) after work, the noise was too loud."]},{heading:"2. Protective equipment",body:"Hearing protection devices (HPD):",points:["Earplugs: give good protection when inserted correctly.","Earmuffs: easy to put on and suitable for loud noise.","Wear both together in very noisy areas."]},{heading:"3. Noise control in the workplace",body:"Protective equipment is the last line of defense. The workplace first:",points:["Buys quieter machines and repairs loose, worn or unbalanced parts.","Encloses noisy machines or separates them from work areas.","Marks noisy areas with signs and provides hearing protection at their entrances.","Arranges hearing checks for workers in noisy areas and acts on the results."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/hearing_protection.mp4",title:"Noise",description:"How do you use hearing protection?"}],quiz:[{question:"If the noise level reaches 100 dB(A), how long can you stay without hearing protection according to NIOSH?",options:[{text:"45 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"15 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"4 hours",correct:!1},{text:"A full 8-hour shift",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NIOSH, every 3 dB increase halves the safe exposure time: 85 dB(A) for 8 hours, and 100 dB(A) for only 15 minutes."},{question:"After a shift, you notice ringing in your ears (tinnitus). What does this tell you?",options:[{text:"Your ears are getting used to the noise",correct:!1},{text:"The noise was too loud",correct:!0},{text:"Your earplugs were too tight",correct:!1},{text:"It is normal and means nothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"Ringing in the ears after work is a warning sign that the noise was too loud; hearing loss from noise develops gradually and cannot be cured, so use hearing protection."},{question:"In very noisy areas, how should hearing protection be worn?",options:[{text:"Cotton wool instead of earplugs",correct:!1},{text:"No protection, but with short breaks",correct:!1},{text:"Earplugs and earmuffs together",correct:!0},{text:"Hands over the ears when it gets loud",correct:!1}],explanation:"In very noisy areas, wear both together: earplugs give good protection when inserted correctly, and earmuffs are easy to put on and suitable for loud noise."},{question:"You have to raise your voice to be heard by a colleague an arm's length away. What does this suggest?",options:[{text:"The noise is likely loud enough to damage hearing",correct:!0},{text:"Your colleague probably already has a hearing problem",correct:!1},{text:"The machines need oil and will soon become quieter",correct:!1},{text:"This is normal factory noise and needs no action at all",correct:!1}],explanation:"Needing to raise your voice at arm's length is a simple sign that the noise is hazardous; have the noise measured and use protection."},{question:"What is the best way to protect workers from a loud machine?",options:[{text:"Cut the noise at the source, then use hearing protection for the rest",correct:!0},{text:"Give earplugs only to the workers who complain about the noise",correct:!1},{text:"Let workers play music in headphones so they stop noticing the noise",correct:!1},{text:"Ask workers to stand behind the machine instead of in front of it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cutting the noise at the source protects everyone; protective equipment is the last line of defense, and music adds more sound."},{question:"A worker takes off his earmuffs for a short time every hour to rest his ears. What is the problem?",options:[{text:"Earmuffs wear out faster when they are taken off often",correct:!1},{text:"Even short periods without protection in the noise cause damage",correct:!0},{text:"There is no problem, because short breaks in the noise do no harm at all",correct:!1},{text:"His ears will get used to the noise and grow stronger",correct:!1}],explanation:"Protection must be worn the whole time in the noise; to rest your ears, go to a quiet place and take it off there."}],steps:[{title:"Know the noisy areas",detail:"Look for the hearing-protection signs and ask which machines and areas are noisy. If you have to raise your voice to be heard by someone an arm's length away, the noise is likely to be hazardous."},{title:"Reduce the noise at the source first",detail:"Managers and maintenance teams: quieter machines, enclosures around noisy machines, repair of loose or worn parts and distance from the source come before protective equipment."},{title:"Fit your protection correctly",detail:"Insert earplugs fully, as shown in training. Earmuffs must seal around the ear with no hair, glasses arms or cap underneath."},{title:"Wear it the whole time in the noise",detail:"Protection only works if it is worn the whole time. Taking it off even briefly in the noise lets much of the damage reach your ears."},{title:"Report and get your hearing checked",detail:"Report ringing in the ears or muffled hearing after work, and take part in the hearing checks your workplace arranges."}],warnings:["Do not use cotton wool or tissue as earplugs: they do not give real protection.","Do not use music headphones to cover factory noise: they add sound instead of protecting.","Do not take off your protection in the noise to talk or answer the phone: move to a quieter place first."],tips:["Keep earplugs clean and replace them when dirty or damaged; wash your hands before inserting them.","Use hearing protection at home too when you work with loud tools.","Managers: put signs at the entrances of noisy areas and keep enough hearing protection there."],activity:{prompt:"In the press shop the noise is very loud. A new worker takes off his earmuffs because they feel hot and says the noise does not bother him. You are his supervisor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave him, because noise that does not bother him cannot harm him",correct:!1,feedback:"Noise damages hearing without pain; the damage builds up slowly and cannot be cured."},{text:"I explain the damage, have him put protection back on and find him a more comfortable type such as earplugs",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: protection only works when it is worn, and a comfortable type makes it more likely he will wear it."},{text:"I give him cotton wool to put in his ears instead",correct:!1,feedback:"Cotton wool gives no real protection and makes the worker think he is protected."},{text:"I let him work without protection, but only for half the shift",correct:!1,feedback:"The safe time depends on the measured noise level, not on how the worker feels; in a very loud area that time is very short."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Workers in the packing hall say that since a new compressor was installed they have to shout to hear each other. You are the supervisor. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Nothing, because nobody has complained of pain",correct:!1,feedback:"Noise harms without pain; having to shout to talk is a sign of hazardous noise."},{text:"I report it so the noise is measured, and give out hearing protection until it is controlled",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: measuring shows how big the risk is, and protection keeps workers safe until the noise is reduced."},{text:"I ask the workers to talk less",correct:!1,feedback:"Talking less does not lower the compressor noise; the damage continues."},{text:"I turn the radio up so the compressor is not noticed",correct:!1,feedback:"The radio adds more sound on top of the noise and makes the damage worse."}]},{prompt:"The measurement shows that the hall is noisy. Management asks what to do. Which plan is best?",options:[{text:"Only hand out earplugs and change nothing else",correct:!1,feedback:"Protective equipment is the last line of defense; reducing noise at the source protects everyone."},{text:"Enclose or move the compressor, keep protection and signs until the noise is down, and arrange hearing checks",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the noise is cut at the source, protection covers what remains and hearing checks find damage early."},{text:"Give workers longer breaks inside the same noisy hall",correct:!1,feedback:"Resting inside the same noise does not take the worker away from the noise."},{text:"Employ only workers who already have hearing loss",correct:!1,feedback:"This is unfair, and the hearing they still have would be damaged too."}]}],realCase:'A worker spent many years next to cutting machines and wore his earplugs only "when the noise was very loud". After shifts he noticed ringing in his ears but thought it was normal. Over time he could no longer follow a conversation in a group and could not hear his children clearly at home. A hearing test showed permanent hearing loss. The factory then measured the noise in every hall, put signs at the noisy areas, enclosed the loudest machines and trained all workers to wear protection the whole time they are in the noise.'},{id:10,code:"ERGO-110",icon:"Package",accent:"#0d9488",title:"Manual handling (ergonomics)",subtitle:"Safe lifting techniques",summary:"Preventing back and muscle injuries",blocks:[{body:"Back injuries are among the most common injuries in factories, caused by lifting loads incorrectly."},{heading:"1. The correct lifting technique",body:"Following these steps protects your back:",points:["Bend your knees: use the strength of your legs, not your back.","Keep the load close to your body: the farther the load is from you, the greater the strain on your back.","Keep your back straight: do not bend your back while lifting something heavy.","Do not twist your body: if you need to move the load to one side, turn your whole body using your feet."]},{heading:"2. Think before you lift",body:"Most lifting injuries can be prevented by planning:",points:["Do not lift by hand where a trolley, pallet truck or hoist can do the job.","Assess the load: weight, size, grip and whether its contents can shift.","Assess yourself and the place: space, floor, steps, lighting and heat.","If the load is heavy or awkward, get help or equipment; do not take the risk."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/safe_lifting.png",title:"Lifting a load",description:"The correct technique"}],quiz:[{question:"When lifting something heavy, where should the effort come from?",options:[{text:"The lower back muscles",correct:!1},{text:"The leg muscles",correct:!0},{text:"The shoulder muscles",correct:!1},{text:"The hands only",correct:!1}],explanation:"The leg muscles are stronger and protect the back from injury."},{question:"Where should a heavy load be while you lift it?",options:[{text:"At arm's length, away from your body",correct:!1},{text:"Above your head",correct:!1},{text:"To one side of your body",correct:!1},{text:"Close to your body",correct:!0}],explanation:"Keep the load close to your body: the farther the load is from you, the greater the strain on your back."},{question:"You need to move a load to one side. How do you turn?",options:[{text:"Twist your body and keep your feet still",correct:!1},{text:"Turn your whole body using your feet",correct:!0},{text:"Bend your back sideways toward the new spot",correct:!1},{text:"Throw the load across quickly to save effort",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not twist your body: to move the load to one side, turn your whole body using your feet and keep your back straight."},{question:"Before lifting a box you have never moved before, what do you do first?",options:[{text:"Rock it gently to judge its weight and check the grip",correct:!0},{text:"Lift it quickly so its weight does not tire you",correct:!1},{text:"Ask someone to put it on your back for you",correct:!1},{text:"Bend over it with straight legs to reach it more easily",correct:!1}],explanation:"Testing the weight and grip before lifting shows whether you can carry it alone or need help or equipment."},{question:"A load blocks your view as you carry it. What is the right decision?",options:[{text:"Carry it anyway and walk faster to arrive sooner",correct:!1},{text:"Use a trolley or a team lift, or split the load, so you can see",correct:!0},{text:"Carry it on your head so that your eyes are free",correct:!1},{text:"Ask a colleague to shout directions while you walk without seeing",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you cannot see, you may hit things or fall; lifting aids, a team lift or smaller loads give you back your view."},{question:"In a team lift, which of these is wrong?",options:[{text:"Agreeing on the route and where to put the load before starting",correct:!1},{text:"Lifting together on one clear command",correct:!1},{text:"Each person lifting at the moment he feels ready",correct:!0},{text:"Choosing partners of similar height where possible",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lifting out of step puts the whole weight on one person for a moment and can injure his back; the lift must be on one command."}],steps:[{title:"Plan the lift",detail:"Check the weight, size and grip of the load and the route. If the load is heavy or awkward, use a trolley, a hoist or a second person."},{title:"Clear the route",detail:"Remove obstacles from the way, open doors before lifting and know where you will put the load down."},{title:"Firm stance and good grip",detail:"Place your feet apart for balance, bend your knees, keep your back straight and grip the load firmly."},{title:"Lift smoothly with your legs",detail:"Keep the load close and lift with your legs, without rushing or jerking; keep your head up."},{title:"Move and put down",detail:"Turn with your feet, not by twisting, put the load down and then adjust its position. If you get tired, put the load down first and then rest."}],warnings:["Do not lift a load you are not sure you can carry: test it gently first, and ask for help or equipment.","Do not lift and twist at the same time, and do not carry a load that blocks your view of the way ahead."],tips:["Store heavy items at waist height, not on the floor or on high shelves.","Split a large load into smaller ones where you can.","In a team lift, one person gives the commands so everyone lifts and puts down together.","Report back pain early, before it becomes a serious injury."],activity:{prompt:"A delivery of heavy sacks arrives at the store and the trolley is broken. Your supervisor wants them moved quickly. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I carry two sacks at a time to finish faster",correct:!1,feedback:"A double load adds strain to the back and makes injury more likely."},{text:"I report the broken trolley, ask for other equipment or help and plan a team lift",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: lifting aids and a team lift reduce the strain on the back, and the report gets the trolley repaired."},{text:"I drag the sacks across the floor, bent at the waist",correct:!1,feedback:"Dragging with a bent back puts heavy strain on the lower back."},{text:"I throw the sacks up onto the shelf from below",correct:!1,feedback:"Throwing twists the body suddenly, and the load may fall on you or someone else."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You have to move a heavy motor from the floor to a workbench. It is hard to grip. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I lift it at once with straight legs and a bent back",correct:!1,feedback:"This puts the greatest strain on the back; it is the most dangerous way."},{text:"I assess the weight and grip and use a hoist or another person",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: planning and lifting aids come first; a heavy, awkward load is not lifted alone."},{text:"I pull it up by its cable",correct:!1,feedback:"The cable is not made for lifting and may break, dropping the load on your feet."},{text:"I ask the youngest worker to lift it alone",correct:!1,feedback:"Age does not lower the risk; a heavy, awkward load can injure anyone's back."}]},{prompt:"A colleague will help you lift the motor together. How do you do the team lift?",options:[{text:"Each of us lifts when he is ready",correct:!1,feedback:"Lifting out of step throws the whole weight onto one person."},{text:"One person gives the commands; we lift together, keep the load close and move and put it down together",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: one person's commands share the weight evenly, so nobody suddenly carries the load alone."},{text:"The stronger person takes most of the weight while the other only guides",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not a team lift; the stronger person still carries a heavy load alone."},{text:"We lift it fast to shorten the time under the load",correct:!1,feedback:"A fast, jerky pull injures the back; the lift must be smooth and controlled."}]}],realCase:"A storekeeper lifted heavy boxes from the floor to a high shelf every day, bending his back and twisting his body to place them. One morning he felt a sharp pain in his lower back during a lift and could not stand up straight. He was away from work for a long time. After this, the factory moved heavy items to shelves at waist height, provided a trolley and a small lifting table, and trained all store staff to plan each lift, ask for help with awkward loads and turn with their feet."},{id:11,code:"GAS-111",icon:"Wind",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Gases and ventilation",subtitle:"Ventilation and toxic gases",summary:"Controlling air quality and toxic gases",blocks:[{body:"Proper ventilation in a factory is important to prevent the buildup of dust and gases that cause respiratory diseases."},{heading:"1. Carbon monoxide (CO)",body:"The silent killer gas:",points:["It is colorless and odorless.","It is produced by burning fuel and by gasoline and diesel engines (such as generators and forklifts).","Symptoms: headache, dizziness, nausea and loss of consciousness. If you notice these symptoms, get outside immediately and call 122."]},{heading:"2. Types of ventilation",body:"How is the air exchanged?",points:["Natural ventilation: open doors and windows.","Mechanical ventilation: fans and local exhaust ventilation (LEV) systems.","Personal protection: filter respirators as specified in the SDS; however, filter respirators do not protect against CO or oxygen deficiency."]},{heading:"3. Other dangerous gases at work",body:"CO is not the only dangerous gas:",points:["H₂S comes from sewage, drains and rotting waste, and it can knock out the sense of smell.","Gases such as nitrogen and argon can push out oxygen with no color or smell.","Vapors from solvents, paints and fuels can be toxic and flammable too.","Read the SDS for each chemical: it gives the hazards, the ventilation and the protection needed."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/ventilation_safety.mp4",title:"Gas",description:"Carbon monoxide (CO)"}],quiz:[{question:"Why is CO dangerous?",options:[{text:"Because it smells bad",correct:!1},{text:"Because it cannot be seen or smelled",correct:!0},{text:"Because it is red",correct:!1},{text:"Because it is only produced at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"Because it cannot be sensed, workers are poisoned without realizing it. Install CO detectors wherever engines are running."},{question:"While working near a running generator, you get a headache, dizziness and nausea. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Take a painkiller and keep working",correct:!1},{text:"Sit down near the generator and rest",correct:!1},{text:"Put on a filter respirator and stay",correct:!1},{text:"Get outside at once and call 122",correct:!0}],explanation:"Headache, dizziness and nausea can be signs of CO poisoning; get outside immediately and call 122, because CO cannot be seen or smelled."},{question:"Does a filter respirator protect a worker against CO?",options:[{text:"No, it does not protect against CO",correct:!0},{text:"Yes, any filter respirator stops CO",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, if it is worn for less than an hour",correct:!1},{text:"Only if the filter is changed every day",correct:!1}],explanation:"Filter respirators are chosen as specified in the SDS, but they do not protect against CO or oxygen deficiency; where engines run, ventilation and CO detectors are needed."},{question:"A gas detector alarms in your area, but you smell nothing. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Leave the area and report it, because many gases have no smell",correct:!0},{text:"Stay, because an alarm without a smell is probably false",correct:!1},{text:"Switch the detector off and test it again after the shift",correct:!1},{text:"Open a window and carry on with the job you were doing",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO and many other gases have no smell, and H₂S can knock out the sense of smell; treat the alarm as real and get out."},{question:"What is the best way to remove vapors at a work point such as a solvent cleaning bench?",options:[{text:"An ordinary fan that blows air toward the workers",correct:!1},{text:"Local exhaust ventilation that captures vapors at the source",correct:!0},{text:"Spraying air freshener in the workshop regularly throughout the day",correct:!1},{text:"A dust mask for every worker at the bench",correct:!1}],explanation:"Local exhaust captures vapors before they reach the worker's breathing zone; an ordinary fan spreads them, and a dust mask does not stop vapors."},{question:"A worker has collapsed in a gas area. Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Raising the alarm and calling 115 and 122 at once",correct:!1},{text:"Going in with a wet cloth over your mouth to pull him out",correct:!0},{text:"Keeping other people out of the gas area until the team arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Telling the rescue team which gas may be present",correct:!1}],explanation:"A wet cloth does not protect against toxic gases or lack of oxygen; only a trained team with breathing apparatus goes in."}],steps:[{title:"Before work: ventilation and detectors",detail:"Make sure the ventilation and local exhaust are running and the gas detectors work. Do not run petrol or diesel engines in a closed area without ventilation designed for it."},{title:"Recognize the warning signs",detail:"A gas detector alarm, an unusual smell, or headache, dizziness and nausea in several workers at the same time are all signs of gas."},{title:"Get out and warn others",detail:"Stop work, get to fresh air and warn others. If the gas may be flammable, use no switch, flame or phone inside. Close a valve only if it is close, reachable without entering the gas area and you are trained for it; otherwise get out.",critical:!0},{title:"Call from outside",detail:"From fresh air, call 115 for the gas leak and 122 for anyone who is ill. Say which gas or chemical may be involved and where people are.",critical:!0},{title:"Do not go back in to rescue",detail:"If someone collapses in the gas area, do not go in without breathing apparatus (SCBA), a plan and training: the untrained rescuer becomes the next victim. Raise the alarm, call 115 and keep people away.",critical:!0},{title:"Return only when cleared",detail:"Nobody goes back in until the air has been tested and the safety officer or the 115 teams allow it.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never enter a gas area to rescue a collapsed worker without breathing apparatus and training: the untrained rescuer becomes the next victim; raise the alarm and call 115.","Never run a petrol or diesel engine in a closed area without ventilation designed for it: CO builds up without any warning.","Do not use a filter respirator to stay in a gas area: it does not protect against CO or lack of oxygen."],tips:["Know where the gas detectors, alarms and exits are in your work area.","Report at once if a ventilation fan or local exhaust stops, gets weak or makes an unusual noise.","If several workers in the same place get headaches at the same time, suspect gas and get everyone out.","Managers: keep the safety data sheets (SDS) for every chemical and train workers on them."],activity:{prompt:"In a workshop where a diesel forklift is working, the CO detector alarms and two workers say they have a headache. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I silence the alarm and open a window",correct:!1,feedback:"One window is not enough, and the workers' symptoms show that CO has reached a dangerous level."},{text:"I get everyone out to fresh air, call 122 for those with symptoms and let nobody back in until the air is tested",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: fresh air comes first with CO, symptoms need medical care, and nobody returns until the air is tested."},{text:"I give them painkillers and work goes on",correct:!1,feedback:"Painkillers do not remove CO from the body, and the workers stay in the gas and may pass out."},{text:"I hand out filter respirators and work goes on",correct:!1,feedback:"Filter respirators do not protect against CO; only getting out to fresh air does."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During a shift you see a worker lying in a pit where chemical vapors collect. Another worker runs toward the pit to pull him out. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I go in with him so we can get the worker out faster",correct:!1,feedback:"There would be three victims instead of one; the vapors that overcame the first worker will overcome you too."},{text:"I stop him from going in, raise the alarm and call 115 and 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stopping the untrained rescuer saves his life, and the 115 team goes in with breathing apparatus."},{text:"I hold a wet cloth over my mouth and climb into the pit",correct:!1,feedback:"A wet cloth does not protect against toxic vapors or lack of oxygen."},{text:"I wait to see whether the worker comes round by himself",correct:!1,feedback:"Every minute in the gas counts; the rescue teams must be called at once."}]},{prompt:"The 115 team is on its way. What do you do until it arrives?",options:[{text:"I keep people away from the gas area, clear the way for the team and prepare the facts: where the worker is, which chemical and the SDS",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this information lets the team go in quickly with the right protection."},{text:"I pour water into the pit to wash the gas away",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not remove all gases and may spread the chemical or put the collapsed worker at risk of drowning."},{text:"I switch on an electric fan near the pit to blow the gas out",correct:!1,feedback:"If the gas is flammable, an unsuitable fan can set off an explosion, and it may push the gas toward other people."},{text:"I send all the workers home so the area is quiet",correct:!1,feedback:"Workers must be counted and give information to the team when it arrives; sending them away makes the team's work harder."}]}],realCase:"In winter, a workshop's doors were kept shut while a petrol-engine compressor ran inside. Several workers got headaches and felt dizzy, and one of them passed out. A colleague recognized the signs of CO, got everyone out to fresh air, called 122 and did not let anyone go back in for their tools. All the workers were checked by medical staff. Afterwards the engine was moved outside the building, CO detectors were installed and the ventilation was checked."},{id:12,code:"EVAC-112",icon:"Megaphone",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Emergency evacuation",subtitle:"Emergency assembly points",summary:"The plan for getting out during a fire or disaster",blocks:[{body:"Knowing your exit route can make the difference between life and death."},{heading:"1. Evacuation rules",body:"When the alarm sounds:",points:["Walk calmly and quickly, do not run and do not use the elevator.","Close doors behind you so fire and smoke do not spread, but do not lock them.","Go to the assembly point so that employees can be counted.","Never go back inside until the safety officer or the 115 teams give permission."]},{heading:"2. Roles in the evacuation plan",body:"Every factory needs a written emergency plan that everyone knows:",points:["Wardens guide people out, check their area if it is safe and report to the person in charge.","The person in charge receives the head count and briefs the 115 team.","Everyone knows two exits, the alarm sound and the assembly point.","Visitors, contractors and colleagues with disabilities are included in the plan. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/evacuation_drill.mp4",title:"Evacuation drill",description:"Evacuation training"}],quiz:[{question:"When the alarm sounds, what will you do?",options:[{text:"Gather my belongings, then leave",correct:!1},{text:"Walk calmly toward the exit",correct:!0},{text:"Hide under a desk until it stops",correct:!1},{text:"Shout",correct:!1}],explanation:"Evacuating calmly and quickly is the best way to protect life."},{question:"What do you do with the doors as you leave during an evacuation?",options:[{text:"Lock them so no one can go back inside",correct:!1},{text:"Leave them wide open to let the smoke out",correct:!1},{text:"Close them, but do not lock them",correct:!0},{text:"Prop them open for the teams arriving later",correct:!1}],explanation:"Close doors behind you so fire and smoke do not spread, but do not lock them."},{question:"You are at the assembly point. When may you go back inside?",options:[{text:"As soon as the alarm bell stops ringing in the building",correct:!1},{text:"When the safety officer or the 115 teams allow it",correct:!0},{text:"When no more smoke comes out of the windows",correct:!1},{text:"After 10 minutes, if no fire can be seen",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never go back inside until the safety officer or the 115 teams give permission; the assembly point is also where employees are counted."},{question:"At the assembly point you notice that a colleague is missing. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Tell the warden or the 115 team at once where you last saw him",correct:!0},{text:"Go back inside quickly to look for him where he works",correct:!1},{text:"Wait until the end of the day to see whether he turns up",correct:!1},{text:"Call his family to ask whether he has already gone home",correct:!1}],explanation:"The 115 teams, with protective equipment, can search safely if they know where to look; going back yourself or waiting costs time and lives."},{question:"You find pallets stored in front of an exit door. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Leave them, because there is another exit at the far end",correct:!1},{text:"Remove them or report them at once, because exits must stay clear",correct:!0},{text:"Lock the door so nobody uses this blocked exit by mistake",correct:!1},{text:"Put a sign on the pallets asking people to climb over them",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a fire, smoke may block the other exit; every exit must stay clear and open from the inside without a key."},{question:"Which of these is wrong during an evacuation?",options:[{text:"Helping a visitor who does not know the way out",correct:!1},{text:"Taking the elevator because the stairs are crowded",correct:!0},{text:"Closing doors behind you without locking them",correct:!1},{text:"Reporting to the warden at the assembly point",correct:!1}],explanation:"An elevator can stop in a fire and trap the people inside; all the other actions are part of a correct evacuation."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm",detail:"If you see a fire or another danger, raise the alarm and call 115 from a safe place.",critical:!0},{title:"Stop work safely",detail:"Make your machine safe only if it takes a moment and you are trained to do it. Leave your belongings and get out at once.",critical:!0},{title:"Leave by the nearest safe exit",detail:"Walk calmly, do not use the elevator and close doors behind you. If there is smoke, stay low and use the other route.",critical:!0},{title:"Help others",detail:"Help visitors and colleagues who need help, as the plan says. Wardens check their area if it is safe to do so."},{title:"Report at the assembly point",detail:"Give your name to the warden. If someone is missing or trapped, say where they were; do not go back yourself to look for them.",critical:!0},{title:"Wait for permission",detail:"Stay at the assembly point, away from the building and the emergency vehicle routes, until the safety officer or the 115 teams allow you to leave or go back.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never go back inside for belongings or to search for a missing person: tell the warden or the 115 team where that person may be.","Never block, lock or store goods in exit routes and exit doors: an exit door must open from the inside without a key."],tips:["Know two ways out of your work area.","Take evacuation drills seriously; they reveal blocked exits and missing people.","Managers: keep an up-to-date list of everyone on site, including visitors and contractors.","Plan help in advance for colleagues who cannot use the stairs."],activity:{prompt:'The fire alarm sounds while you are in the middle of an urgent order. Your supervisor says: "It is probably a drill, finish first." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I finish the order, since it is probably a drill",correct:!1,feedback:"Treat every alarm as real; while you wait, smoke may block the exit routes."},{text:"I make the machine safe if it takes a moment, then leave by the nearest exit for the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm is an order for everyone to leave; the work can be finished later."},{text:"I go to the office to find out whether it is real",correct:!1,feedback:"Going to the office wastes time and may take you toward the fire."},{text:"I stay at my machine until someone tells me to go",correct:!1,feedback:"The alarm itself is the order; waiting may let fire or smoke cut off your way out."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At the assembly point, the warden finds that a worker from the paint store is missing. A colleague says he may be in the toilet. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I run back inside to check the toilet",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back puts your life at risk and may leave the 115 team searching for two people instead of one."},{text:"I tell the warden and the 115 team at once where he may be",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the 115 teams, with protective equipment, can reach him quickly if they know where to look."},{text:"I phone him and wait without telling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"He may not answer; time is lost and the team does not know someone is inside."},{text:"I assume he left by another exit and go home",correct:!1,feedback:"An assumption is not certainty; every missing person must be reported at once."}]},{prompt:"The 115 team arrives. The fire is in the paint store. Some workers want to go back to move their cars from the yard next to the building. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I let them move their cars quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"They would be close to the fire and may block the way for emergency vehicles."},{text:"I keep everyone at the assembly point, away from the fire and the team's routes, and follow the team's instructions",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cars can be moved later; lives and the team's work come first."},{text:"I go back in to fetch the attendance book",correct:!1,feedback:"Never go back inside; the count is done with an outside list or by name."},{text:"I move the assembly point closer so we can see the fire better",correct:!1,feedback:"Getting closer to the fire raises the risk from smoke and from paint materials exploding."}]}],realCase:"When the fire alarm sounded in a factory, most workers left at once, but a group in the packing area kept working because they thought it was a false alarm. One exit was blocked by pallets, so they had to go back through a smoky corridor to reach the other exit. At the assembly point a worker was missing; the warden told the 115 team where he had been working, and the team found him in a storeroom. Afterwards the factory cleared all exit routes, held regular drills and made the wardens responsible for checking each area."},{id:13,code:"WELD-113",icon:"Sparkles",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Welding safety",subtitle:"Welding and cutting hazards",summary:"Protection from welding radiation, heat and fumes",blocks:[{body:"Welding work involves the risk of ultraviolet (UV) radiation, severe burns, and toxic gases and fumes."},{heading:"1. Eye and skin protection",body:"What welders need:",points:["A welding helmet with the correct filter shade: to protect the eyes from UV radiation and the intense light of the welding arc.","Long leather gauntlets: for protection from heat and spatter.","A leather apron: to protect the body."]},{heading:"2. Fire hazard (hot work)",body:"Before starting, obtain a hot-work permit and:",points:["Clear combustible materials from around the work area (NFPA 51B: within 11 meters / 35 feet), or cover them with fire-resistant blankets.","Have a suitable fire extinguisher ready close by.","Fire watch: stays at the location during the work and for at least 1 hour after the work is finished; the area is then monitored for up to 3 more hours, as decided by the permit authorizing individual (NFPA 51B)."]},{heading:"3. Fumes, gases and cylinders",body:"Welding makes fumes and uses gases that need control:",points:["Fumes from metals and coatings can harm the lungs; use local exhaust or good ventilation.","In a closed or confined space, shielding gases such as argon can push out oxygen; follow the confined-space rules.","Gas cylinders are stored upright, secured against falling and kept away from heat; valves are closed when work stops.","Damaged cables and hoses are taken out of use and reported."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/welding_safety.png",title:"Welding",description:"Wearing protective gear correctly"}],quiz:[{question:"Why does the fire watch stay at the location for at least 1 hour after welding?",options:[{text:"To clean up the leftover metal pieces",correct:!1},{text:"To prevent a hidden fire from developing",correct:!0},{text:"To wait until the welded metal has cooled down",correct:!1},{text:"It is not necessary",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sparks often fall into cracks and materials and start a fire some time later. Under NFPA 51B (2019 edition onward), the fire watch continues for at least 1 hour after the work."},{question:"Before hot work starts, what must be done with combustible materials around the work area?",options:[{text:"Leave them in place, since a fire watch will be present",correct:!1},{text:"Remove them or cover them with fire-resistant blankets",correct:!0},{text:"Spray them with water once and start welding right away",correct:!1},{text:"Move them just behind the welder, away from direct sparks",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before starting, obtain a hot-work permit and clear combustible materials from around the work area (NFPA 51B: within 11 meters / 35 feet), or cover them with fire-resistant blankets."},{question:"What protects a welder's eyes from UV radiation and the intense light of the arc?",options:[{text:"Ordinary clear safety glasses, as used for dust",correct:!1},{text:"Dark sunglasses worn under a hard hat",correct:!1},{text:"A welding helmet with the correct filter shade",correct:!0},{text:"Looking at the arc for a few seconds only",correct:!1}],explanation:"A welding helmet with the correct filter shade protects the eyes from UV radiation and the intense light of the welding arc; long leather gauntlets and a leather apron protect the hands and body."},{question:"You are asked to cut open an old drum that held fuel. What is the right decision?",options:[{text:"Do not cut until a competent person has cleaned it and confirmed it is safe",correct:!0},{text:"Rinse it once with water and cut it while it is still wet",correct:!1},{text:"Cut it outdoors, because vapors cannot build up and explode there",correct:!1},{text:"Cut it quickly so the heat does not reach the inside",correct:!1}],explanation:"Vapor left inside the drum can explode from a single spark, even outdoors; only cleaning and testing by a competent person remove the danger."},{question:"While you work, welding fumes rise into your face. What is the best action?",options:[{text:"Hold your breath while you weld each seam",correct:!1},{text:"Use local exhaust and keep your head out of the fumes",correct:!0},{text:"Lift the helmet a little so more air reaches you",correct:!1},{text:"Wear a dust mask and carry on in the same position",correct:!1}],explanation:"Welding fumes contain metal particles and gases; local exhaust removes them at the source and moving your head protects your breathing. Lifting the helmet exposes your eyes to the arc."},{question:"Which of these is wrong for a fire watch?",options:[{text:"Keeping a suitable extinguisher ready near the work",correct:!1},{text:"Checking hidden places such as gaps and the floor below",correct:!1},{text:"Leaving as soon as the welder stops working",correct:!0},{text:"Knowing how to raise the alarm and call 115",correct:!1}],explanation:"The watch continues after the work too (at least 1 hour under NFPA 51B), because hidden sparks can start a fire later."}],steps:[{title:"Permit and area check",detail:"Get the hot-work permit, remove or cover combustible materials, and close openings in floors and walls so sparks cannot pass through.",critical:!0},{title:"Check the equipment",detail:"Check cables, hoses, connections and gas cylinders for damage. Cylinders stay upright and secured so they cannot fall."},{title:"Protect yourself and others",detail:"Wear the welding helmet with the correct filter shade, leather gloves and apron. Put up screens so people nearby are protected from the arc light."},{title:"Control the fumes",detail:"Work with local exhaust ventilation or good general ventilation, and keep your head out of the rising fume plume.",critical:!0},{title:"Fire watch during and after the work",detail:"The fire watch has a suitable extinguisher ready, knows how to raise the alarm and call 115, and stays at the site for as long as the permit requires.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never weld or cut a drum, tank or pipe that has held flammable material until a competent person has cleaned it and confirmed it is safe: it can explode.","Never look at the welding arc without a filter: its light burns the eyes even in a short time.","Do not weld inside a confined space unless the confined-space rules (permit, air testing and attendant) are followed."],tips:["Wear clean cotton or wool clothes free of oil; synthetic fabric can melt onto the skin.","Close the gas cylinder valves when work stops.","Warn the people around you before striking the arc so they can look away.","The fire watch also checks hidden places: behind walls, below floors and inside ducts."],activity:{prompt:"You are about to weld a bracket in the workshop. Cardboard boxes and a can of solvent are near the work. The permit is signed, but nobody has been named as fire watch. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I start welding and keep an eye on the boxes myself",correct:!1,feedback:"With the welding helmet down you cannot see around you; the fire watch must be another person."},{text:"I move or cover the combustibles and do not start until a fire watch with an extinguisher is in place",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the permit is not just paper; removing combustibles and having a fire watch are conditions of hot work."},{text:"I weld quickly so the sparks have no time to start a fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Sparks reach the cardboard and the solvent vapor in a second; speed does not reduce the risk."},{text:"I spray the boxes with water once and start",correct:!1,feedback:"Wet cardboard dries fast and the solvent can is still there; the materials must be removed or covered."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A welder finishes a job near a wooden wall at the end of the shift. The supervisor wants everyone to go home now. You are the fire watch. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave with everyone because the welding has stopped",correct:!1,feedback:"Sparks can smolder in cracks and start a fire later; that is why the watch continues after the work."},{text:"I stay and watch the area for as long as the permit says, checking hidden places",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: watching after the work finds hidden fires before they spread."},{text:"I pour water on the wall and leave",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not reach every crack or the space behind the wall, and nobody stays to see a fire."},{text:"I lock the workshop so nobody goes in",correct:!1,feedback:"Locking up does not stop a fire; a hidden fire would grow without anyone knowing."}]},{prompt:"Some time later you see smoke coming from a gap behind the wall. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I raise the alarm and call 115; I use the extinguisher only if the fire is small, the exit is behind me and I am trained",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: alarm and 115 first, then the go/no-go check before using the extinguisher; if the fire does not go out at once, get out."},{text:"I pull the wall panel open with bare hands to find the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening it with bare hands can burn you and feed air to the fire so it suddenly grows."},{text:"I wait to see whether it goes out by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke means a hidden fire that can spread fast; waiting loses precious time."},{text:"I go home and report it in the morning",correct:!1,feedback:"An unwatched fire can burn down the whole building overnight; it must be reported at once."}]}],realCase:"A welder repaired a steel frame near a storage area at the end of a working day. The combustible materials nearby had not been removed, and no fire watch stayed after the work. Late at night a fire started from sparks that had fallen into packaging, and it spread through the storage area before anyone noticed. Nobody was inside, but the stock and part of the building were lost. The factory then made the hot-work permit, the removal or covering of combustibles and a fire watch during and after every job a fixed rule."},{id:14,code:"FORK-114",icon:"Forklift",accent:"#475569",title:"Material handling equipment",subtitle:"Forklift and vehicle safety",summary:"Forklift safety and driving inside the factory",blocks:[{body:"A forklift is a heavy machine with a risk of crushing and tip-over if it is not operated correctly."},{heading:"1. Operating rules",body:"For forklift operators:",points:["Only trained and authorized persons may operate a forklift (OSHA 1910.178).","Never carry anyone on the forks of a forklift or beside you.","Do not exceed the rated capacity of the machine, so it does not tip over, and fasten your seat belt.","Slow down and sound the horn at every corner and doorway."]},{heading:"2. Pedestrian safety",body:"If you are walking near a forklift:",points:["Make eye contact with the operator and keep a safe distance.","Walk in the marked pedestrian walkways."]},{heading:"3. Load and stability",body:"Most tip-overs are caused by a wrong load or wrong driving:",points:["Read the capacity plate: the safe load depends on the weight and how far the load's center is from the mast.","Put the forks fully under the load and tilt it back slightly before moving.","On a ramp with a load, keep the load facing uphill, and never turn on a ramp.","Never park in front of exits, firefighting equipment or emergency routes."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/forklift_safety.mp4",title:"Forklift",description:"Recognizing blind spots"}],quiz:[{question:"If a forklift operator is carrying a tall load and cannot see ahead, what should they do?",options:[{text:"Drive fast",correct:!1},{text:"Drive in reverse",correct:!0},{text:"Sound the horn and close their eyes",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"When the load blocks the operator's view, they must drive in reverse so they can see where they are going (OSHA 1910.178)."},{question:"A coworker asks for a ride on the forks of your forklift. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Let them ride if you drive slowly",correct:!1},{text:"Let them stand on the forks and hold on",correct:!1},{text:"Let them sit beside you instead of on the forks",correct:!1},{text:"Refuse, because no one may ride on the forks",correct:!0}],explanation:"Never carry anyone on the forks of a forklift or beside you; a forklift is a heavy machine with a risk of crushing and tip-over."},{question:"You are walking near a moving forklift. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Make eye contact and keep a safe distance",correct:!0},{text:"Walk close to it so the driver sees you better",correct:!1},{text:"Cross in front of it quickly before it reaches you",correct:!1},{text:"Wave and walk on, since the driver must stop",correct:!1}],explanation:"Near a forklift, make eye contact with the operator, keep a safe distance and walk in the marked pedestrian walkways; the operator may not see you in the blind spots."},{question:"When a forklift is moving, where should the forks be?",options:[{text:"Low, close to the floor, while the truck is moving",correct:!0},{text:"At eye level so the driver can see them well",correct:!1},{text:"As high as possible to pass over obstacles",correct:!1},{text:"At any height, as long as the driver goes slowly",correct:!1}],explanation:"Low forks and a low load keep the truck stable and reduce the chance of tipping; a raised load while moving makes it unstable."},{question:"The forklift starts to tip onto its side. What should a driver wearing the seat belt do?",options:[{text:"Jump out on the low side as fast as possible",correct:!1},{text:"Stay in the seat, hold on firmly and lean away from the fall",correct:!0},{text:"Jump out on the high side and run away from the machine",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the steering hard toward the other side",correct:!1}],explanation:"Most serious injuries happen when the driver jumps and is caught under the machine; the belt and seat keep him inside the protective frame."},{question:"The daily check finds a hydraulic oil leak. What is the right decision?",options:[{text:"Use it until the end of the shift and then report it",correct:!1},{text:"Take it out of use and report it for repair",correct:!0},{text:"Put sand under it and keep using it as usual",correct:!1},{text:"Use it only for light loads until it is repaired",correct:!1}],explanation:"A hydraulic leak can make the load drop or the lift fail, and it makes the floor slippery; a faulty forklift is not used until it is repaired."}],steps:[{title:"Check the forklift before use",detail:"Before each shift, check the brakes, steering, horn, lights, forks, chains, tires and any leaks. Report a faulty forklift and do not use it.",critical:!0},{title:"Check the load",detail:"The load must not exceed the capacity on the forklift's data plate, must be stable and centered, and the forks must be fully under it."},{title:"Drive safely",detail:"Wear the seat belt, keep the forks low while moving, slow down at corners and sound the horn at doorways. If the load blocks your view, drive in reverse.",critical:!0},{title:"Watch for people",detail:"If a pedestrian comes close, stop until they move away. Nobody may stand or walk under raised forks or a raised load.",critical:!0},{title:"Park safely",detail:"Lower the forks to the floor, put the controls in neutral, set the parking brake, switch off and remove the key. Never park in front of exits or firefighting equipment."}],warnings:["Never lift a person on the forks or on a pallet: they can fall or be crushed.","If the forklift starts to tip over, do not jump out: stay in the seat with the belt on, hold on firmly and lean away from the direction of the fall.","Never lift a load heavier than the forklift's capacity, even low down: steering and brakes may not work properly and the truck can tip."],tips:["Pedestrians: do not use your phone while crossing forklift routes.","Managers: separate pedestrian walkways from forklift routes with markings or barriers.","Mirrors at blind corners help, but they do not replace slowing down and sounding the horn.","Charge batteries and refuel only in the places set aside for it."],activity:{prompt:`A driver in a hurry wants to lift a pallet heavier than the capacity on the forklift's data plate and says: "I have done it before." You are the shift supervisor. What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I allow it this time if he drives slowly",correct:!1,feedback:"Driving slowly does not make an overload safe; the truck can tip at a corner or when braking."},{text:"I stop him; the load is split or a forklift with enough capacity is used",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the capacity on the plate is the limit for every load on that truck; having done it before does not prove it is safe."},{text:"I ask two workers to sit on the back of the forklift as a counterweight",correct:!1,feedback:"This means carrying people on the forklift and could kill them; adding extra counterweight is not allowed either."},{text:"I let him lift it only slightly off the floor",correct:!1,feedback:"An overload is not allowed at any height; the steering and brakes may not work properly and the truck can tip when turning."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During the morning check, a driver finds that the forklift horn does not work and the brakes feel soft. Production needs the forklift now. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I use it carefully today and report it at the end of the shift",correct:!1,feedback:"Soft brakes may fail when they are needed; being careful does not replace brakes."},{text:'I take it out of use, tag it "do not use" and report it for repair',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a faulty forklift is not used until it is repaired; the tag stops others from using it."},{text:"I use it only in areas where there are no people",correct:!1,feedback:"Without brakes the truck can hit racks or columns and drop its load; the danger is not only to pedestrians."},{text:"I ask another driver to use it because he has more experience",correct:!1,feedback:"Experience does not fix faulty brakes; the truck is still unsafe."}]},{prompt:"A replacement forklift arrives. The driver is carrying a tall load through a busy aisle, cannot see ahead and workers are walking nearby. What should he do?",options:[{text:"Drive forward fast to get out of the aisle quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Driving fast without seeing, close to people, makes hitting someone far more likely."},{text:"Drive slowly in reverse, looking in the direction of travel, sound the horn and stop if anyone comes close",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: driving in reverse restores the view, and stopping when people come close protects pedestrians."},{text:"Raise the load higher so he can see under it",correct:!1,feedback:"A raised load while moving reduces stability; the truck can tip or the load can fall."},{text:"Ask a worker to stand on the forks and guide him",correct:!1,feedback:"Nobody rides on the forks; they can fall and be crushed."}]}],realCase:"In a warehouse, a forklift driver took a corner at speed with a raised load. The forklift tipped over. The driver had not fastened his seat belt and jumped out; he was caught under the machine and badly injured. The investigation found that the load was carried too high, the speed at the corner was too fast and the belt was not worn. Afterwards the company made seat belts mandatory, reduced speed at corners, required loads to be carried low and separated pedestrian walkways from forklift routes."},{id:15,code:"SIGN-115",icon:"AlertTriangle",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Safety signs",subtitle:"Safety signs and symbols",summary:"Understanding safety colors and symbols (ISO 7010)",blocks:[{body:"Safety signs warn us about hazards in a universal language."},{heading:"1. The meaning of colors (ISO 7010)",body:"Each color conveys a specific message:",points:["Red: prohibition or firefighting equipment (example: no smoking).","Yellow: warning and danger (example: electrical hazard).","Blue: mandatory (example: hard hat must be worn).","Green: safe condition and exits (example: emergency exit door)."]},{heading:"2. Shapes and what they mean (ISO 7010)",body:"Shape, color and symbol together give the meaning of a sign:",points:["Circle with a red border and a diagonal bar: prohibition (do not do this).","Yellow triangle with a black border: warning of a hazard.","Blue circle: mandatory action (you must do this).","Green square or rectangle: safe condition — exits, first aid and the assembly point.","Red square or rectangle: firefighting equipment — extinguisher, fire hose reel and fire alarm call point."]},{heading:"3. Signs at work: duties of workers and managers",body:"Signs protect people only when they are seen and understood:",points:["Read the signs at the door of each area before you start work.","Never cover, move or remove a sign; report broken, faded or missing signs to your supervisor.","Exit and firefighting equipment signs must be visible from the work area; stacked goods must not hide them.","A sign does not remove the hazard: it reminds you of the control you need (PPE, a procedure or a work permit).","Chemical containers carry GHS hazard pictograms (a diamond with a red border); read the label and the SDS.","Which signs are required at your workplace: follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/safety_signs.png",title:"Safety signs",description:"Standard symbols"}],quiz:[{question:"What does a yellow sign mean?",options:[{text:"Prohibition of an action",correct:!1},{text:"Warning and danger",correct:!0},{text:"Safe place and escape route",correct:!1},{text:"Use of water",correct:!1}],explanation:'Yellow always means "be careful" or "there is a hazard".'},{question:"A blue sign at a workshop door shows a hard hat. What does it mean?",options:[{text:"Hard hats are not allowed here",correct:!1},{text:"Warning: objects may fall here",correct:!1},{text:"A hard hat must be worn here",correct:!0},{text:"Hard hats are stored behind this door",correct:!1}],explanation:"Blue means mandatory, for example a hard hat must be worn; red means prohibition or firefighting equipment, yellow warning and green safe condition and exits."},{question:"Which color is used for safe conditions and exits, such as the emergency exit door?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1},{text:"Blue",correct:!1},{text:"Green",correct:!0}],explanation:"Green marks safe conditions and exits, such as the emergency exit door; red is for prohibition or firefighting equipment."},{question:"A door has a circle with a red border and a diagonal bar over a flame symbol. What does it mean?",options:[{text:"Open flames are prohibited here",correct:!0},{text:"A fire extinguisher is behind this door",correct:!1},{text:"Warning: hot surfaces inside the room",correct:!1},{text:"Flame-resistant clothing must be worn here",correct:!1}],explanation:"A circle with a red border and a diagonal bar always means prohibition; a warning is a yellow triangle, a mandatory action a blue circle and fire equipment a red square."},{question:"Which sign shows where a fire extinguisher is?",options:[{text:"A red square with an extinguisher symbol",correct:!0},{text:"A green square with an arrow and a running person",correct:!1},{text:"A blue circle with an extinguisher symbol",correct:!1},{text:"A yellow triangle with a flame symbol",correct:!1}],explanation:"Firefighting equipment is shown by a red square or rectangle; green is the escape route, blue a mandatory action and a yellow triangle a warning."},{question:"A coworker has hung his jacket over the green exit sign. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I ask him to remove it now so the sign stays visible",correct:!0},{text:"I leave it, because everyone knows where the exit is",correct:!1},{text:"I move the sign to a spot where nobody hangs things",correct:!1},{text:"I leave it until the next safety inspection finds it",correct:!1}],explanation:"An exit sign must be visible at all times; in smoke or darkness new workers and visitors depend on it. Moving a sign is not a worker's job."}],steps:[{title:"Look before you enter",detail:"Before you enter any area, read the signs at its door: which PPE is mandatory, what is prohibited and which hazards are inside.",critical:!0},{title:"Read shape, color and symbol together",detail:"The shape tells you the type of sign (circle, triangle, square), the color confirms the message and the symbol shows what it is about. A text panel below the sign adds detail."},{title:"Do what the sign requires",detail:"Blue sign: use the PPE or take the mandatory action. Prohibition sign: do not do that act. Yellow sign: take care and follow the control for that hazard.",critical:!0},{title:"Know where the green signs lead",detail:"During induction, walk the exit route along the green signs to the assembly point, and use the red signs to locate the extinguisher and the fire alarm call point.",critical:!0},{title:"Report damaged or hidden signs",detail:"Report a broken, faded, missing or hidden sign to your supervisor at once. Never remove or move a sign yourself."}],warnings:["Never cover or hide an exit sign or a firefighting equipment sign, even for a short time: in smoke, people depend on them to find the way out and the equipment.","Do not rely on color alone, and a missing sign does not mean there is no hazard: if a sign is faded or missing, ask before you start work."],tips:["Learn the five shapes; a sign can be understood without words.","If a sign is unclear to you, ask your supervisor before you start work.","Managers: check signs during routine workplace inspections and after any change to the layout.","Explain new signs to new workers during induction, not only with writing on the wall."],activity:{prompt:'You are a new worker. At a workshop door there is a blue hearing-protection sign, and inside there is a yellow electrical-hazard sign on a panel. Your supervisor says: "Just come in for a minute, no need for hearing protection." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I go in without hearing protection because it is only a minute",correct:!1,feedback:"A blue sign is mandatory every time you enter, not only for long jobs; loud noise can damage hearing quickly."},{text:"I wear hearing protection, keep clear of the electrical panel and politely ask the supervisor about the rule",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the blue sign is mandatory and the yellow sign tells you to keep away from the hazard; asking is the right way to clear up the rule."},{text:"I take the blue sign down because it confuses people",correct:!1,feedback:"Workers do not remove signs; if a sign seems wrong, report it so the person in charge decides."},{text:"I open the electrical panel door to see whether the hazard is real",correct:!1,feedback:"The yellow sign means the hazard is there; only a qualified, authorized electrician opens the panel."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the night-shift supervisor in a warehouse. A forklift driver has stacked pallets in front of the green exit sign and the red extinguisher sign in an aisle. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I have the pallets moved now so the exit and the signs are clear, then I record it",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the exit route and its signs must be clear at all times; recording it helps fix the cause."},{text:"I leave them until the morning shift because nobody works there now",correct:!1,feedback:"A fire can start at any time; night-shift staff need the exit route too."},{text:'I tape a paper "exit" note onto the pallets',correct:!1,feedback:"A paper note does not replace a proper sign and an open route; the exit is still blocked."},{text:"I take the sign down because the route is blocked anyway",correct:!1,feedback:"This doubles the danger: the route is blocked and nobody knows where it is; the route must be opened."}]},{prompt:"A week later, the fire alarm sounds in the same warehouse and light smoke spreads under the roof. A new worker does not know which way to go. What do you tell him?",options:[{text:"Follow the green exit signs, stay low under the smoke and go to the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: green signs show the escape route, and cleaner air is near the floor."},{text:"Follow the red signs, because they show the way out",correct:!1,feedback:"Red signs show prohibitions or firefighting equipment, not the escape route."},{text:"Stay at your machine until a supervisor comes to take you",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting wastes precious time; everyone must leave at once by the marked route."},{text:"Use the goods lift, because it is faster than the stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"Lifts are not used during a fire: they may stop and trap people inside."}]}],realCase:"In a packaging plant, workers stored cartons in a corridor and hid the exit sign at a junction. During a drill, several new workers turned the wrong way and reached a locked storeroom. The safety officer noted it in the drill report. The plant manager had the corridor cleared, added the signs to the routine inspection checklist and showed all new workers the exit routes during induction. At the next drill, everyone followed the green signs to the assembly point. The lesson: a sign only protects people when it can be seen and when people know what it means."},{id:16,code:"HOUS-116",icon:"ClipboardCheck",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Housekeeping",subtitle:"Order and maintenance",summary:"Organizing the workplace to reduce hazards",blocks:[{body:"A clean workplace is a safe workplace. Many accidents happen because of slipping and tripping over scattered materials."},{heading:"1. General rules",body:"For all workers:",points:["Clean up spilled oil or water as soon as possible.","Collect waste in designated bins.","Keep walkways and exits open and free of obstructions.","Return every tool to its place when the work is finished."]},{heading:"2. Housekeeping is a fire and safety duty",body:"Clutter and waste give a fire its fuel and block the way out:",points:["Waste, oily rags, cardboard and dust are fuel: a small spark can start a fire that spreads through clutter.","Rags soaked with oil or solvent can heat up by themselves and ignite; keep them in a closed metal container and empty it regularly.","Do not let combustible dust build up on floors, beams and machines; it can cause a dust explosion (see the lesson on fire classification).","Keep clear space around electrical panels, extinguishers, alarm call points, hose reels and sprinkler heads.","Fire doors stay closed and are never wedged open.","Exit doors open from the inside without a key and are never locked or blocked during working hours."]},{heading:"3. Spills, cables and storage",body:"Rules for daily work:",points:["Spills: warn others, put out a sign, then clean up; for chemicals, read the SDS first.","If you cannot clean a spill safely (unknown chemical, large amount or fumes), keep people away and report it at once.","Route cables and hoses away from walkways or cover them; never leave them across the path.","Do not overload shelving or stack higher than the height set for the site.","Managers: give each area a clearly responsible person and include housekeeping in routine inspections; follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/clean_factory.png",title:"Housekeeping",description:"An example of a clean workplace"}],quiz:[{question:"If you see oil spilled on the floor, what will you do?",options:[{text:"Leave it as it is until the cleaner comes on the next shift",correct:!1},{text:"Clean it up, or put a sign on it and report it",correct:!0},{text:"Jump over it",correct:!1},{text:"Ignore it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every worker is responsible for keeping their own work area clean and safe, so that no one falls."},{question:"How should walkways and exits be kept?",options:[{text:"Open and free of obstructions",correct:!0},{text:"Used to store boxes when the store is full",correct:!1},{text:"Blocked only for short periods during work",correct:!1},{text:"Cleared only before an inspection visit",correct:!1}],explanation:"Keep walkways and exits open and free of obstructions; many accidents happen because of slipping and tripping over scattered materials."},{question:"You have finished a job. What do you do with the tools you used?",options:[{text:"Leave them on the floor for the next shift",correct:!1},{text:"Return each tool to its place",correct:!0},{text:"Put them in the waste bin with the scraps",correct:!1},{text:"Pile them in the walkway near the exit",correct:!1}],explanation:"Return every tool to its place when the work is finished and collect waste in designated bins; scattered materials cause slips and trips."},{question:"At the end of the shift, where do oily rags go?",options:[{text:"In a closed metal container made for them",correct:!0},{text:"In the general waste bin together with the paper",correct:!1},{text:"In a pile in a corner to wash later",correct:!1},{text:"In a cardboard box under the workbench",correct:!1}],explanation:"Oily rags can heat up by themselves and ignite; a closed metal container limits air and keeps any fire inside. Cardboard and the general bin add fuel."},{question:"You find a fire door wedged open with a block of wood so trolleys can pass. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I remove the wedge and report it so a safe solution is found",correct:!0},{text:"I leave it, because it makes moving the trolleys much faster",correct:!1},{text:"I tie it open firmly with a rope so it cannot swing shut",correct:!1},{text:"I put a warning sign on the door and leave it open as it is",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fire door only stops smoke and fire when it is closed; if it must stay open, the person in charge fits a proper device that closes it automatically when the alarm sounds."},{question:"Which of these practices is unsafe?",options:[{text:"Storing boxes in front of the electrical panel",correct:!0},{text:"Running a cable along the wall away from the walkway",correct:!1},{text:"Putting a sign on a wet floor until it dries",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping a spill kit near the oil store",correct:!1}],explanation:"The space in front of an electrical panel must stay clear so it can be switched off fast in an emergency and an electrician can work safely. The other three practices are safe."}],steps:[{title:"Check your area before work",detail:"Walk through your work area: walkways, exits, extinguishers, electrical panels and alarm call points must be open and free of obstructions.",critical:!0},{title:"Deal with spills at once",detail:"Warn others and put out a warning sign, then clean up with the right material. For chemicals, read the label and the SDS first, and use PPE and the spill kit.",critical:!0},{title:"Put waste in the right container",detail:"Cardboard and paper go in their own bins, and rags soaked with oil or solvent go in a closed metal container made for them."},{title:"Store materials safely",detail:"Heavy items on low shelves, stacks stable and no higher than the height set for the site; nothing in front of extinguishers or electrical panels, or right under sprinkler heads."},{title:"Leave the area ready at the end of the shift",detail:"Return tools to their places, take out the waste and report any remaining problem to the supervisor and the next shift."}],warnings:["Never wedge a fire door open, and never lock an exit door or store goods in front of it: in a fire these are the way out and the barrier against smoke.","Do not wipe up an unknown chemical with rags or sawdust: read the label and the SDS first; some substances react with them or give off toxic fumes."],tips:["Mark storage areas and walkways with lines on the floor.","Clean as you go, not only at the end of the shift.","Keep a spill kit near where liquids are used, and learn how to use it.","Use a short housekeeping checklist at shift handover."],activity:{prompt:"At the end of a busy shift there is a pile of cardboard and oily rags next to the grinding bench, where sparks fly. You are about to go home. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave it for the night cleaner",correct:!1,feedback:"Cardboard and oily rags are fuel next to sparks; a fire can start before the cleaner arrives."},{text:"I put the cardboard in its bin and the rags in the closed metal container, then tell the supervisor",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the fuel is removed from the sparks, and oily rags stay in a closed container because they can heat up by themselves and ignite."},{text:"I pour a bucket of water over the pile",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not remove the fuel, makes the floor slippery and may reach electrical equipment."},{text:"I cover the pile with a plastic sheet",correct:!1,feedback:"Plastic burns too, and the fuel is still next to the sparks."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a supervisor. A worker reports that an unlabeled drum is leaking a liquid onto a walkway and there is a strong smell. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I keep people away, allow no sparks or flames, alert the safety officer or ERT and look for the SDS",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the substance is unknown and the smell may mean flammable or toxic vapor; protect people first, then identify the substance."},{text:"I clean it up at once with sawdust so nobody slips",correct:!1,feedback:"Without knowing the substance, sawdust may react with it or become fuel."},{text:"I tell the workers to step over it and carry on",correct:!1,feedback:"People stay in the vapor and may fall; the danger is not over."},{text:"I carry the drum outside with my bare hands",correct:!1,feedback:"Without PPE or knowing the substance, you expose yourself to an unknown chemical and may make the spill worse."}]},{prompt:"The safety officer has identified the liquid: a flammable solvent. The leak is small and has stopped. What happens next?",options:[{text:"Trained staff clean it with the spill kit and PPE as the SDS says, with no sparks, and put the waste in a labeled container; then they fix why the drum was in the walkway",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: clean-up as the SDS says, then fix the cause (wrong storage, no label) so it does not happen again."},{text:"I hose it into the floor drain",correct:!1,feedback:"A flammable solvent collects as vapor in drains and pollutes the environment."},{text:"We restart work at once because the leak has stopped",correct:!1,feedback:"The spilled liquid is still on the floor and its vapor may reach a spark."},{text:"We leave it to evaporate by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Evaporation means vapor in the air; that raises the risk of fire and of breathing it in."}]}],realCase:"In a furniture workshop, sawdust and offcuts were cleaned up only at the end of the week. One evening a spark from a grinder fell into a pile of dust and wood shavings under a bench and it began to smolder. A worker noticed the smell and raised the alarm, and 115 was called. A trained colleague checked that the fire was small and his exit was behind him, then put it out with the right extinguisher. No one was hurt. The manager changed the routine: clean as you go, check the dust collection system and check housekeeping at every shift handover."},{id:17,code:"QUAK-117",icon:"Activity",accent:"#d97706",title:"Earthquakes in factories",subtitle:"Earthquake safety in factories",summary:"Worker safety during and after an earthquake",blocks:[{body:"An earthquake creates a double hazard in a factory: the shaking itself, plus falling heavy machinery, chemical spills, broken pipes and gas leaks. Every factory must have an earthquake plan."},{heading:"1. Drop, Cover, Hold On",body:"During an earthquake:",points:["Drop to the ground (Drop) and get under a sturdy table or workbench — away from heavy machinery, shelving and glass.","If there is no table, kneel next to an interior wall and cover your head and neck with your hands (Cover).","Hold on to the table (Hold On) until the shaking stops completely.","Do not run, and do not go under machinery or next to shelving! Heavy machinery and shelving can shift or fall on you."]},{heading:"2. After the earthquake",body:"Once the shaking stops:",points:["Inspect machinery for damage, and do not switch on damaged machinery until it has been inspected.","Check whether any chemicals have spilled, and keep away from them.","If you smell gas — get out quickly, evacuate the factory and call 115 from outside.","Only trained, designated staff shut off the gas, and only if there is a smell of gas or a leaking sound and the gas valve can be reached safely, without entering the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark; shut off the electricity if wiring or electrical panels have been damaged.","Before going back inside, an engineer must inspect the factory building for cracks."]},{heading:"3. Preparedness",body:"Planning ahead:",points:["Prepare an earthquake plan for every department.","Secure heavy machinery with chains or anchor bolts.","Store drums and chemical containers on low, stable shelving.","Keep exit routes open at all times.","Hold a drill once every 6 months."]},{heading:"4. Wherever you are when the shaking starts",body:"Act according to where you are working:",points:["At a machine: move away from it and get under a sturdy table; never try to hold the machine.","On a forklift or in a vehicle: stop in an open spot away from racking, buildings and power lines, set the brake and stay where you are until the shaking stops.","Outdoors: if you can do so safely, move away from buildings, outdoor racks, stacked materials and power lines, then drop and cover your head and neck.","In chemical areas: the plan names who safely stops the processes after the shaking; nobody runs to valves or panels while it shakes."]},{heading:"5. Duties of supervisors and managers after the shaking",body:"Until the 115 teams and the engineer arrive:",points:["Count all staff, contractors and visitors at the assembly point.","Report trapped or missing people to 115 and injured people to 122; do not enter damaged buildings to search.","Restart work only after qualified people have checked the building, gas, electricity and machines.","Building inspection and permission to restart: follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/factory_earthquake.png",title:"Earthquake",description:"An earthquake in a factory"}],quiz:[{question:"During an earthquake in a factory, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Run outside through the nearest door at once",correct:!1},{text:"Drop to the ground and get under a sturdy table",correct:!0},{text:"Switch off all the machines before doing anything else",correct:!1},{text:"Go near the windows",correct:!1}],explanation:"Drop, Cover, Hold On is the first action inside buildings, away from machinery, shelving and glass."},{question:"After an earthquake in a chemical plant, what is the first thing to check?",options:[{text:"The electricity meters and panels",correct:!1},{text:"Chemical spills",correct:!0},{text:"The water pipes",correct:!1},{text:"The telephone lines",correct:!1}],explanation:"Chemical spills are a major hazard after an earthquake and must be checked quickly."},{question:"After the shaking stops, you smell gas in the factory. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Go to the gas valve and shut it off yourself",correct:!1},{text:"Switch on the extractor fans to clear the gas",correct:!1},{text:"Get out quickly and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Call 115 on your phone first, then leave",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you smell gas, get out quickly, evacuate the factory and call 115 from outside; only trained, designated staff shut off the gas, and only if the valve can be reached safely without entering the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark."},{question:"You are driving a forklift in the warehouse when the shaking starts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop away from the racking, set the brake and stay seated",correct:!0},{text:"I drive fast toward the nearest exit door",correct:!1},{text:"I jump off and run under the nearest storage rack",correct:!1},{text:"I raise the forks to hold up the rack beside me",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stopping in an open spot and staying seated protects you from falling objects; driving or running while it shakes and going near racking are dangerous."},{question:"After an earthquake a coworker says the building looks fine and wants to restart the production line. What is right?",options:[{text:"Wait for the engineer and qualified staff to inspect",correct:!0},{text:"Restart at once so the orders are not delayed",correct:!1},{text:"Restart only the machines that still have power",correct:!1},{text:"Restart after a quick look by the newest worker",correct:!1}],explanation:"Damage to the building, gas pipes and wiring is not always visible, and aftershocks can bring down weak parts; only qualified people give permission."},{question:"Which of these is a mistake during an earthquake at work?",options:[{text:"Running for the exit while the building still shakes",correct:!0},{text:"Getting under a sturdy workbench away from machines",correct:!1},{text:"Kneeling by an interior wall and covering your head",correct:!1},{text:"Holding on to the table until the shaking stops",correct:!1}],explanation:"Running while it shakes exposes you to glass and falling objects; the other three actions are part of Drop, Cover, Hold On."}],steps:[{title:"Drop, Cover, Hold On",detail:"At once drop down and get under a sturdy workbench, away from heavy machines, tall racking and glass; if there is no table, cover your head and neck beside an interior wall.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay put until the shaking stops",detail:"Do not run or try to leave the building while it shakes. If you are on a forklift, stop in an open spot away from racking, set the brake and stay seated.",critical:!0},{title:"After the shaking: check yourself and others",detail:"Look for injured or trapped people, give first aid if you are trained and call 122 and 115. Expect aftershocks, and each time Drop, Cover and Hold On again."},{title:"Leave carefully by the planned route",detail:"Use the stairs, not the lift, and keep away from spills, fallen objects and damaged wiring. If you smell gas, use no switches or phones inside, get out and call 115 from outside; only trained, designated staff shut off the gas, and only if the valve can be reached safely.",critical:!0},{title:"Account for everyone at the assembly point",detail:"The supervisor counts the staff and tells the 115 teams the name and last known location of anyone missing. Nobody goes back in until an engineer has checked the building.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Do not run outside while the ground is shaking: glass, wall cladding and stacked goods can fall on you on the way.","Do not go back into a damaged building or restart damaged machines until they are inspected: aftershocks can bring down weakened structures.","Never use a lift after an earthquake: it may stop or may have been damaged."],tips:["Know the nearest sturdy cover in every area where you work.","In drills, practise Drop, Cover, Hold On at your own workstation.","Keep heavy items low on the shelves and fix tall racking to the wall or floor.","Keep a flashlight and a whistle in each department's emergency kit."],activity:{prompt:"You are working at a lathe when strong shaking begins. Tall racking is behind you and the exit door is at the far end of the hall. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I run fast to the exit door",correct:!1,feedback:"Running while it shakes puts you under falling objects and you may fall; the first action is to drop."},{text:"I drop, get under a sturdy workbench away from the machine and the racking, and hold on",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Drop, Cover, Hold On, away from things that can shift or fall."},{text:"I stand in the nearest doorway",correct:!1,feedback:"A doorway is not stronger than other places and the door can swing into you; this is not taught."},{text:"I stay next to the machine to stop it from falling",correct:!1,feedback:"No one can hold a heavy machine; it can shift and fall on you."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"The shaking has stopped in the store of a chemical plant. You are the supervisor. A drum has fallen and liquid is spreading, and there is a smell of gas in one corner. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I get everyone out by the planned route away from the spill and the gas, use no switches and call 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: there are two hazards at once; people come first, and the call is made outside so no spark is made."},{text:"I switch off the lights and machines on my way out",correct:!1,feedback:"Switches can make a spark in the gas; no switch is used where gas can be smelled."},{text:"We clean up the spill first so it does not spread",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying inside with gas and an unknown liquid puts people at risk; the spill is handled later by specialist teams."},{text:"I go to the gas valve in that corner to close it",correct:!1,feedback:"The valve is inside the area where the gas has collected; going there is not allowed. Get out and tell 115."}]},{prompt:"At the assembly point you find that two workers are missing. A coworker wants to go back into the store to look for them. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop him and tell the 115 teams where the two workers were last seen",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the building is damaged and contains gas; searching is the rescue teams' job, and they need your information."},{text:"I let him go if he is quick",correct:!1,feedback:"Aftershocks and gas can make him a victim too; then three people need rescue."},{text:"I go back in myself with a flashlight",correct:!1,feedback:"The supervisor must stay at the assembly point and run the count; going in carries the same danger."},{text:"I wait without reporting, they may have gone home",correct:!1,feedback:"Every missing person must be reported at once; time matters for rescue."}]}],realCase:"A moderate earthquake struck during the day shift at a metal fabrication plant. Workers who had practised dropped under workbenches, away from machines and tall racking. One heavy rack that was not anchored fell into an aisle. When the shaking stopped, the supervisors led everyone out by the planned routes and counted staff at the assembly point. A maintenance worker reported a gas smell near the boiler room; no one went back in, and 115 was called from outside. An engineer checked the building before work resumed. Afterward the plant anchored all its racking."},{id:18,code:"FLOOD-118",icon:"Waves",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Floods and water inundation",subtitle:"Flood emergency in industrial areas",summary:"Protecting the factory and its workers during a flood",blocks:[{body:"A flood can completely destroy a factory: it can ruin machinery, spread chemicals and turn the electrical system into a hazard. Factories near rivers and valleys are at greater risk."},{heading:"1. Before the flood",body:"Preparedness:",points:["Follow warnings from the meteorological service.","Move important equipment and chemicals to a higher location.","Prepare the electrical system for automatic shutoff.","Place sandbags in front of the doors and along the foundation of the factory."]},{heading:"2. During the flood",body:"Emergency procedures:",points:["If you can do so safely and the water has not reached the electrical panels, shut off the electricity and gas; never touch anything electrical while standing in water!","Do not enter flood water — 15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away.","Move to higher ground — the second floor or the roof.","Call 115 and stay where you are until the rescue teams arrive."]},{heading:"3. After the flood",body:"Returning and cleaning up:",points:["Do not enter the factory until an engineer confirms that the building is safe.","Do not touch chemicals that have been in contact with flood water — request a hazardous materials (HazMat) team through 115.","Do not switch the power back on until the system has been inspected by a qualified electrician.","Photograph all damage for insurance and reporting."]},{heading:"4. The flood plan: duties of managers",body:"Before a flood comes, the plan must answer these questions:",points:["Can the site flood? (Low ground, near a river or valley, or poor drainage.)","Who decides to stop work and send staff home or upstairs, and when?","A list of chemicals and fuels and where they are; store them above the expected water level where possible.","An emergency kit (flashlight, first-aid kit, radio and drinking water) on an upper floor.","Warnings and evacuation orders: follow Civil Defense and official announcements; the competent authority sets the warning channels."]},{heading:"5. Flood water and health",body:"Flood water is not just water:",points:["It can hide open holes, sharp objects and live electricity.","It may be mixed with chemicals, fuel and sewage.","After contact with flood water, wash your hands and skin with clean water and soap, and report any cuts to first aid.","Generators and petrol pumps run outdoors only, at least 6 m (20 ft) from windows, doors and vents."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/factory_flood.png",title:"Flood",description:"A flood in a factory"}],quiz:[{question:"During a flood in a factory, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Protect the machinery by covering it with large plastic sheets",correct:!1},{text:"Shut off the electricity and gas, if you can do so safely",correct:!0},{text:"Go home",correct:!1},{text:"Start pumping out the water before anything else",correct:!1}],explanation:"Electricity and gas are the primary hazards during a flood; however, if the water has reached the electrical panels, do not approach them and move to higher ground."},{question:"How much fast-moving water can be enough to knock a person down?",options:[{text:"50 cm",correct:!1},{text:"75 cm",correct:!1},{text:"15 cm",correct:!0},{text:"1 meter",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not enter flood water: 15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away. Move to higher ground and call 115."},{question:"After the flood, you find chemical drums that were in the flood water. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Rinse them with clean water and put them back on the shelf",correct:!1},{text:"Do not touch them; ask 115 for a HazMat team",correct:!0},{text:"Carry them outside by hand before the water rises again",correct:!1},{text:"Open them to check whether water got inside",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not touch chemicals that have been in contact with flood water; request a hazardous materials (HazMat) team through 115."},{question:"A flood warning is issued while you are at work and your car is parked in a low spot. Which choice is safest?",options:[{text:"I follow the plan and official advice and leave only by a dry route",correct:!0},{text:"I drive quickly through the water before it rises",correct:!1},{text:"I wade to the car to move it to higher ground",correct:!1},{text:"I stay on the ground floor to keep an eye on my car",correct:!1}],explanation:"A car can be replaced, a life cannot; 30 cm of water can carry a car away and 15 cm of fast water can knock a person down."},{question:"After the flood, someone suggests running a petrol pump inside the hall to remove the water. What is the main danger?",options:[{text:"Its exhaust is carbon monoxide, which can kill people inside",correct:!0},{text:"It uses too much fuel and takes a long time to finish",correct:!1},{text:"Its noise will disturb the workers on the other shift",correct:!1},{text:"The water will quickly damage the pump's paint",correct:!1}],explanation:"Petrol engines produce carbon monoxide (CO), which has no color or smell and can kill quickly indoors. Outdoors only, away from windows and doors."},{question:"The water has reached the electrical panels. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep away from the panels and move to high ground",correct:!0},{text:"I stand on a dry box and switch off the main breaker",correct:!1},{text:"I unplug the machines one by one with dry gloves",correct:!1},{text:"I pile sand around the panel to stop the water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Once water has reached the panels, touching them risks a fatal shock; a box or gloves do not fully protect you. Keep away and tell 115."}],steps:[{title:"When a flood warning comes: follow the plan",detail:"Follow the instructions of Civil Defense and official announcements. People first: stop work and send staff who are not needed to high ground early; then, if time allows, raise chemicals and important equipment.",critical:!0},{title:"Power and gas only if safe",detail:"Shut off power and gas only if you can do it safely and the water has not reached the panels. If you are standing in water or the water has reached the panel, keep away.",critical:!0},{title:"Go to high ground, never into the water",detail:"Go up to the second floor or the roof, and never walk or drive into flood water: 15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down and 30 cm can carry a car away.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 and account for everyone",detail:"Tell 115 your location, how many people are with you and whether anyone is hurt; stay where you are until the rescue teams arrive. The supervisor knows who is where."},{title:"After the flood: nothing before inspection",detail:"Do not enter the factory until an engineer confirms the building is safe, do not switch the power back on until a qualified electrician has checked it, and do not touch chemicals that were in the flood water — ask 115 for a HazMat team.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never walk or drive into flood water: it can knock you down, carry vehicles away and hide open drains and live electricity.","Never touch switches or electrical equipment while standing in water or with wet hands.","Never run a generator or a petrol pump inside the building to dry it out: carbon monoxide (CO) can kill; outdoors only."],tips:["Keep copies of the site map, the chemical list and staff phone numbers in a high place that is easy to reach.","Clear drains and gutters before the rainy season.","Tie down drums and small tanks so the water cannot float them away.","During drills, walk the route to the upper floor or high ground."],activity:{prompt:"Heavy rain is falling and water is entering the ground floor of the factory, where the main electrical panel is. The water has reached the bottom of the panel and you are standing in it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I go to the panel and switch off the power by hand",correct:!1,feedback:"The water has reached the panel and you are in water; an electric shock could kill you."},{text:"I keep away from the panel, go upstairs with the others and tell 115 that the panel is under water",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: power is shut off only when it is safe; now is the time to go up and inform the 115 teams."},{text:"I start an electric pump on the ground floor to remove the water",correct:!1,feedback:"An electrical device in the water increases the shock risk and will not stop a flood."},{text:"I drive the company car through the water to get help",correct:!1,feedback:"30 cm of water can carry a car away; call 115 and stay on high ground."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"One afternoon an official flood warning is issued for your area. The factory is in a valley and drums of solvent are on the ground floor. You are the shift manager. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I follow the warning and the plan: stop work, send staff who are not needed out early by a high route and, if time allows, have trained staff raise or tie down the drums",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: people first, and leaving early before water covers the roads is safer; then protect the chemicals so the water does not spread them."},{text:"We keep working until we can see water",correct:!1,feedback:"A flood can arrive very fast; by then the roads are cut and there is no time to leave safely."},{text:"I send everyone home by the valley road, even if there is water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"The valley road is the first place to flood; driving through water is the most dangerous choice."},{text:"I let each worker decide for himself",correct:!1,feedback:"In an emergency people need a clear decision and one plan, so that nobody is left in danger."}]},{prompt:"The water has reached the ground floor. A few staff are with you on the second floor. One worker wants to wade through the water to his car. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop him, we stay upstairs and tell 115 how many we are and where",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: staying high and waiting for the rescue teams is safer than entering the water."},{text:"I let him go if he ties a rope around himself",correct:!1,feedback:"A rope does not protect against fast water, debris or electricity, and it may pull others in."},{text:"We all wade out together holding hands",correct:!1,feedback:"This puts everyone in danger; flood water hides holes and live electricity."},{text:"I go down to switch off the main panel while standing in water",correct:!1,feedback:"Touching the panel while standing in water can kill; tell 115 the panel is flooded."}]}],realCase:"After heavy rain, a small food-processing plant near a seasonal stream received an official flood warning in the afternoon. The supervisor followed the site plan: work stopped, most staff left early by a route on high ground and drums of cleaning chemicals were moved to a high shelf. Two maintenance workers stayed on the second floor with the emergency kit and called 115 when water surrounded the building. No one walked into the water. The next day nobody went back in until an engineer and an electrician had checked the building and the wiring."},{id:19,code:"FACT-119",icon:"Siren",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Civil Defense response in factories",subtitle:"Industrial Civil Defense response and multi-agency coordination",summary:"Organizing the factory response team during incidents and coordinating with 115",blocks:[{body:"Large facilities (oil, gas, chemicals, steel) must have their own emergency response teams that act before the 115 teams arrive. The NFPA 600 standard (Facility Fire Brigades) sets out the organization, training and equipment of facility fire brigades. The first minutes of an incident are critical, so the facility team must be ready and trained."},{heading:"1. Organizing the facility emergency response team (ERT)",body:"Key principles of the emergency response team:",points:["Team size: determined by the facility hazard assessment and the team's assigned duties; NFPA 600 does not set a fixed ratio of members to the number of employees.","Training: based on each member's duties, with refresher training at least once a year.","Incident Commander (IC): only one person gives orders, so that instructions are not mixed up.","Main duties: firefighting, rescue, first aid and building evacuation.","Communications: two-way radio on a dedicated channel, with cell phones as a backup.","Response time: define it in the plan, measure it during drills and improve it.","Recommended: the ICS-100 and ICS-200 courses (FEMA) to learn the Incident Command System."]},{heading:"2. The emergency response plan (ERP)",body:"Requirements for the emergency response plan under the standards:",points:["The emergency action plan must be in writing and available at the workplace; employers with 10 or fewer employees may communicate it orally (OSHA 29 CFR 1910.38).","Main sections: roles, procedures, exit routes, communications, resources, drills and post-incident review.","Assembly points: one primary and one alternate, in a safe location away from the building and from the emergency vehicle access route.","Exit routes: in general, at least 2 exits from every floor (NFPA 101, with some exceptions).","Fire alarm: must be audible or visible in all areas of the building (NFPA 72).","First aid team: every shift must have employees trained in first aid and CPR.","Drills: held regularly, with the results of every drill recorded.","Post-incident review (Post-Response Critique): as soon as possible after every incident."]},{heading:"3. Coordination with Civil Defense (115)",body:"When an incident occurs and the 115 teams arrive, the ERT must take these steps:",points:["1. Call 115 immediately, and 122 if there are casualties.","2. Give the key information: location, type of incident, number of casualties, hazardous materials involved and the access route.","3. Have the site map ready at the gate, showing hazard locations and the electricity and gas shutoff points.","4. Liaison officer: only one person communicates with 115.","5. Have the SDS ready for every chemical on site.","6. Record the names and statements of witnesses.","7. Transfer of command: when 115 arrives, command passes to the 115 commander and the ERT provides support.","8. Documentation: photos, video and the incident log for investigation and insurance."]},{heading:"4. Working with the hazardous materials (HazMat) team",body:"For chemical incidents, a HazMat team is required:",points:["HazMat training levels (OSHA 1910.120 and NFPA 470, which consolidates NFPA 472):","• Awareness level: recognizing the hazard, staying away and raising the alarm — for all employees.","• Operations level: protecting people and the surroundings with defensive actions from a distance.","• Technician level: offensive actions to stop the leak at close range, with specialized PPE.","• Specialist level: in-depth knowledge of specific substances and technical support for technicians.","Three zones: the hot zone, the warm zone and the cold zone.","HazMat PPE levels: A, B, C, D (A gives the highest protection).","Decontamination is carried out in the warm zone, before entering the cold zone.","Untrained employees do not enter the hot zone; they wait for the 115 teams.","The facility plan must state how specialized hazardous materials teams are requested through 115."]},{heading:"5. Multi-agency response",body:"Major incidents require several agencies:",points:["115 Civil Defense: firefighting, rescue and hazardous materials.","122 Ambulance: medical treatment and transport of casualties.","104 Police: cordoning off the area, traffic control and legal investigation.","Security agencies: when explosives or terrorism are suspected — notified through 104.","Ministry of Health: preparing nearby hospitals.","Electricity directorate: cutting off power in the surrounding area.","Water and gas authorities: shutting off water and gas lines.","Environmental authority: if chemicals have been released into the environment.","Emergency Operations Center (EOC): for major incidents involving many agencies."]},{heading:"6. Phases of response (5 phases)",body:"Every incident goes through these 5 phases:",points:["1. Detection: sensors, the fire alarm or a witness.","2. Notification: calling 115, and alerting the ERT and the liaison officer.","3. Response: the ERT acts until the 115 teams arrive.","4. Mitigation: firefighting, rescue and decontamination.","5. Recovery: cleanup, analysis and post-incident review.","Every phase must be documented in an Incident Action Plan (IAP).","The Golden Hour: rapid treatment after a serious injury increases the chance of survival — which is why calling 122 quickly matters."]},{heading:"7. Drills and training",body:"Drills are the foundation of preparedness (recommended schedule):",points:["Tabletop exercise: once every 3 months — a scenario worked through around a table.","Functional drill: once every 6 months — the ERT only, not the whole building.","Full-scale drill: once a year — the entire facility together with 115.","Evaluation: an after-action report as soon as possible.","ERT training: ongoing and based on assigned duties, at least once a year.","Competency standards: NFPA 1081 (Facility Fire Brigade Member), OSHA 1910 Subpart L and ICS-100/200.","Joint drill with 115: once a year for coordination."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fact_ert_response.mp4",title:"ERT response drill",description:"A full-scale drill at an oil facility"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/fact_ics_chart.png",title:"Incident Command System (ICS) structure",description:"Incident Command System (ICS) structure"}],quiz:[{question:"How is the size of a facility emergency response team (ERT) determined?",options:[{text:"By a fixed ratio: 1 trained member for every 100 employees on each shift",correct:!1},{text:"Based on the facility hazard assessment and the team's assigned duties",correct:!0},{text:"By a fixed ratio: 1 per 200 employees",correct:!1},{text:"By a fixed ratio: 1 per 25 employees",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA 600 does not set a fixed ratio of members to the number of employees; each facility determines the team size according to its own hazards and duties."},{question:"What does the Golden Hour mean?",options:[{text:"The first hour of each work shift, when all the safety checks must be carried out",correct:!1},{text:"The initial period after a serious injury, when rapid treatment is critical",correct:!0},{text:"Midday",correct:!1},{text:"The heat of the day",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Golden Hour is the period after a serious injury during which getting the casualty to treatment quickly increases the chance of survival; so call 122 immediately."},{question:"In the ICS, what does the liaison officer do?",options:[{text:"Puts out the fire",correct:!1},{text:"Communicates with outside agencies",correct:!0},{text:"Performs CPR",correct:!1},{text:"Guards the gate and controls who enters",correct:!1}],explanation:"The liaison officer communicates with 115, the police (104), the ambulance service (122) and other agencies."},{question:"What does the HazMat Operations level do?",options:[{text:"Raises the alarm only",correct:!1},{text:"Defensive — preventing the spread from a distance",correct:!0},{text:"Offensive — entering the hot zone to stop the leak at close range",correct:!1},{text:"Specialist",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Operations level is defensive: preventing the spread of the substance from a distance, not offensive work at close range."},{question:"What is the hot zone in a HazMat incident?",options:[{text:"A cool, safe place where the command post and support teams stay",correct:!1},{text:"The zone where people and equipment are decontaminated before they leave",correct:!1},{text:"The most dangerous zone — HazMat technicians only, with appropriate PPE",correct:!0},{text:"The assembly area",correct:!1}],explanation:"The hot zone is where the hazardous material is; only HazMat technicians in Level A or B PPE enter it."},{question:"When the 115 teams arrive at the incident scene, who takes command?",options:[{text:"The ERT remains in command",correct:!1},{text:"The 115 commander",correct:!0},{text:"The factory manager",correct:!1},{text:"The police",correct:!1}],explanation:"Transfer of command (ICS Transfer): when 115 arrives, command passes to the 115 commander and the ERT provides support."},{question:"The ERT has put out a small fire completely. Should 115 have been called at the start?",options:[{text:"Yes — 115 is called at once, whatever the ERT expects",correct:!0},{text:"No — only if the ERT cannot control it",correct:!1},{text:"No — only the manager decides later whether to call",correct:!1},{text:"Yes — but only after it spreads to a second area",correct:!1}],explanation:"An incident can grow within minutes and the 115 teams need time to arrive; an early call costs nothing, a late one can cost lives."},{question:"When the 115 teams arrive, which action by a supervisor is wrong?",options:[{text:"Giving his own separate orders to the 115 teams",correct:!0},{text:"Handing the site map and SDS to the 115 commander",correct:!1},{text:"Reporting who is missing and where they were last seen",correct:!1},{text:"Showing the commander the gas and power shutoffs",correct:!1}],explanation:"Command passes to a single 115 commander; parallel orders cause confusion. The other three actions are proper support."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm and call 115 at once",detail:"Call 115 (and 122 if anyone is hurt) even if the ERT expects to control the incident: give the location, type of incident, number of casualties, hazardous materials and the access route.",critical:!0},{title:"One commander, and evacuation",detail:"There is only one incident commander (IC). Evacuation starts, and the ERT does only what it is trained for; if the incident grows, the ERT withdraws too.",critical:!0},{title:"Account for everyone at the assembly point",detail:"Staff, contractors and visitors are counted; the name and last known location of anyone missing is reported to the commander.",critical:!0},{title:"Prepare for the 115 teams",detail:"Open the gate and the access route; the liaison officer waits for the 115 commander at the gate with the site map, the power and gas shutoff points and the SDS."},{title:"Hand over command to 115 and support",detail:"After a short briefing (situation, hazards, missing people, actions taken), command passes to the 115 commander. The ERT works only as tasked; untrained staff never enter the hot zone.",critical:!0},{title:"After the incident: record and review",detail:"Collect the incident log, photos and witness statements, hold the post-incident review and correct the plan."}],warnings:['Do not delay the call to 115 so the ERT can "try first": fires and chemical releases can grow within minutes.',"Untrained staff never enter the hot zone, and nobody enters a confined space (such as a tank or pit) to rescue a collapsed person: the air inside can kill the rescuer too.","Never give orders in parallel with the incident commander: one commander, one channel."],tips:["Keep a grab box at the gate: site map, SDS, list of staff on shift and keys.","Practise the 115 call with complete information during drills.","Keep radios charged and set to the agreed channel.","With Civil Defense agreement, invite the 115 teams to learn the site and its hazards before any incident."],activity:{prompt:"There is a fire in the solvent store of a chemical plant. The ERT is limiting the spread by cooling. The 115 teams have arrived. You are the ERT leader. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep giving orders and tell 115 to wait until we finish",correct:!1,feedback:"When 115 arrives, command passes to its commander; two commanders cause confusion and danger."},{text:"I brief the 115 commander (situation, hazards, missing people, SDS, shutoffs) and hand over command; the ERT works as he tasks it",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a handover of command with full information is the basis of coordination."},{text:"I pull the whole ERT out and leave without a briefing",correct:!1,feedback:"Without your information, the 115 teams do not know which chemicals are there or whether anyone is missing."},{text:"I send ERT members into the store to remove drums before the handover",correct:!1,feedback:"Entering a burning store full of solvent drums risks an explosion; that decision belongs to the 115 commander."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Emergency alarm: a worker has collapsed in a pump pit and there is a chemical smell. Another worker wants to climb down to pull him out. You are the first ERT member on the scene. What do you do?",options:[{text:'I stop him going in, move everyone away upwind, call 115 and 122 saying "possible toxic air" and alert the ERT leader',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: many would-be rescuers have died in confined spaces; rescue needs breathing apparatus and a trained team."},{text:"I hold my breath and climb down quickly to pull him out",correct:!1,feedback:"Toxic or oxygen-poor air can knock you out within a few breaths; then there are two victims."},{text:"I lower a rope and ask him to tie it around himself",correct:!1,feedback:"An unconscious person cannot tie a rope; this wastes time without alerting 115."},{text:"I put a wet cloth over my mouth and nose and go in",correct:!1,feedback:"A wet cloth does not protect against toxic gases or a lack of oxygen."}]},{prompt:"The 115 teams are on the way. The ERT leader asks you to act as liaison officer. What do you prepare?",options:[{text:"I wait for the 115 commander at the gate with the site map, the chemical's SDS, details of the pit, the number of casualties and the shutoff points",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the liaison officer is the site's single voice to 115, and ready information saves time."},{text:"I go back to the pit to see whether the worker is moving",correct:!1,feedback:"That is not your role and it brings you near dangerous air; 115 needs you at the gate."},{text:"I phone the worker's family before anything else",correct:!1,feedback:"The family is informed later through the proper channel; your task now is to prepare information for 115."},{text:"I stand at the gate with no information; the commander will find out himself",correct:!1,feedback:"Without the map and SDS, the 115 teams lose precious time searching."}]}],realCase:"At night, a hose burst in the pump area of a fuel depot and fuel sprayed onto the ground. A worker raised the alarm; the ERT leader called 115 at once and gave the location, the type of fuel and the access gate. The pumps were stopped from the control room, sparks and flames were kept away and everyone not needed went to the assembly point and was counted. The liaison officer met the 115 commander at the gate with the site map and the SDS. Command passed to 115, and the ERT supported with foam supplies. A review afterward updated the hose inspection plan."},{id:20,code:"FACT-120",icon:"FlameKindling",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire classification in factories",subtitle:"Industrial fire classes — NFPA 30/484/654",summary:"Classification specific to industry: flammable liquids, gases, metals and combustible dust",blocks:[{body:"This module uses the NFPA system: A ordinary solids, B flammable liquids and gases, C electrical equipment, D metals, K cooking oil. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Industry-specific standards: flammable liquids (NFPA 30), combustible metals (NFPA 484) and combustible dust (NFPA 654). Since the 2025 edition, NFPA 484 and NFPA 654 have been consolidated into the NFPA 660 standard."},{heading:"1. Class A — combustible solids in the factory",body:"Common combustible solids in factories:",points:["Fuels: paper, cardboard, fabric, wood, rubber, plastic and pallets.","These materials leave embers and hidden fire behind; after extinguishing, cool the area with water and keep it under watch.","In factories: paper and cardboard storage areas and wooden pallets spread fire quickly.","Extinguisher: water, foam, ABC powder.","Stacks of empty pallets are a major hazard: do not stack them high, and store them outdoors or in a sprinklered area.","Standard: NFPA 13 (sprinkler systems), which also covers storage protection.","Examples in factories: warehouses and packaging lines."]},{heading:"2. Class B — flammable liquids in the factory",body:"One of the most dangerous classes in factories:",points:["Fuels: gasoline, kerosene, diesel, acetone, toluene, xylene.","Flash point: gasoline about -43°C, acetone -20°C, diesel 52°C or higher.","Explosive limits (LEL/UEL): the minimum and maximum vapor concentrations in air that can ignite:","• Gasoline: 1.4-7.6%.","• Acetone: 2.5-12.8%.","• Methanol: 6-36%.","Extinguisher: foam (AFFF or fluorine-free foam), CO₂, ABC powder. For acetone, methanol, alcohols and other polar solvents: CO₂, dry chemical or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them.","Do not direct a water jet onto a burning liquid — it spreads the fire. A BLEVE (Boiling Liquid Expanding Vapor Explosion) occurs when a pressurized tank is heated by fire.","Liquid classes under NFPA 30 (based on flash point and boiling point):","• Class I (Flammable): flash point below 37.8°C — IA (flash point < 22.8°C and boiling point < 37.8°C), IB (flash point < 22.8°C and boiling point 37.8°C or higher), IC (flash point 22.8 to < 37.8°C).","• Class II (Combustible): flash point 37.8 to < 60°C.","• Class III (Combustible): IIIA from 60 to < 93°C, and IIIB 93°C or higher.","Secondary containment (diking): under NFPA 30, the volume of the containment area must be no less than the liquid that can be released from the largest full tank (100%); a 110% capacity is required by some other regulations and guidelines (to allow for rainwater as well).","Vapor density: gasoline vapor is heavier than air (about 3-4 times) and collects in low-lying areas."]},{heading:"3. Flammable gases — NFPA: Class B, EN 2: Class C",body:"Industrial gases: acetylene, propane, hydrogen:",points:["Sources: acetylene for welding, propane for some forklifts, hydrogen from charging lead-acid batteries.","These substances are gases at normal temperatures and ignite easily. Explosive limits in air: hydrogen 4-75%, propane 2.1-9.5%, acetylene from 2.5% upward. (-253°C is the boiling point of hydrogen, not its flash point.)","The temperature of an oxy-acetylene flame exceeds 3000°C.","Acetylene forms an explosive compound (copper acetylide) with copper; do not use unsuitable copper pipes or fittings.","Extinguishing: first, if it is safe to do so, close the gas valve. Do not extinguish a gas fire if you cannot stop the gas flow — the leaking gas can cause an explosion.","Civil Defense teams (115) use water spray from a distance to cool cylinders and tanks, not to extinguish the gas flame.","Gas detectors in gas storage areas: the alarm must sound well before the LEL is reached (typically at 10-20% of the LEL).","In factories: welding areas, forklift charging areas and battery rooms."]},{heading:"4. Class D — combustible metals (NFPA 484)",body:"Combustible metals — one of the most dangerous types:",points:["Fuels: magnesium, titanium, aluminum powder, sodium and lithium metal.","These metals burn at extremely high temperatures and give off very intense light; do not look directly at the flame.","Water is prohibited! Mg + H₂O → MgO + H₂ (hydrogen is produced and can explode).","CO₂ is prohibited! 2Mg + CO₂ → 2MgO + C (magnesium even burns in CO₂).","Class D extinguishers only, and only one whose label lists that metal: the agent differs from metal to metal (for example sodium chloride (NaCl)-based powder or graphite; copper powder for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam. Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's SDS names it and it is completely dry.","Examples in factories: aluminum smelting, magnesium machining and lithium-metal batteries.","NFPA 484: storage, handling and protection of combustible metals (now part of NFPA 660).","Aluminum powder: also carries a risk of dust explosion (NFPA 654 / 660).","Response: call 115 immediately and use only a Class D extinguisher listed for that metal. Lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries contain no metallic lithium and are cooled with water."]},{heading:"5. Combustible dust — dust explosions (NFPA 654)",body:"A deadly hazard in factories:",points:["The dust explosion pentagon: fuel (dust) + oxygen + heat (ignition) + dispersion in air + confinement.","Sources: flour, sugar, wood, coal, metal dust and plastics.","The minimum ignition temperature (MIT) varies with the type of dust; refer to the SDS or the dust test results.","The minimum explosible concentration (MEC) also varies with the type of dust; dust dispersed in the air is always dangerous.","Primary explosion: inside a piece of equipment (such as a filter or a mill).","Secondary explosion: the primary explosion lifts dust from floors, shelves and pipes, causing a larger and more deadly explosion.","Risk reduction: dust collection systems, explosion vents and suppression systems.","Housekeeping: a dust layer 0.8 mm (1/32 inch) thick over more than 5% of the floor area can create a hazard (NFPA 654 / OSHA).","Example: in 2008 at the Imperial Sugar refinery (USA), a sugar dust explosion killed 14 workers (CSB).","Standards: NFPA 654 (now NFPA 660) and OSHA 29 CFR 1910.272 (for grain handling facilities)."]},{heading:"6. Stages of fire development in a factory",body:"The 4 standard stages — in factories a fire can develop faster:",points:["Incipient: a small fire — a trained person may put it out with the right portable extinguisher, after the alarm is raised and 115 called, with the exit route behind them; if it does not go out at once, get out.","Growth: fire and smoke increase rapidly and sprinklers activate — get out and call 115.","Fully developed: after flashover — Civil Defense teams (115) only.","Decay: the fuel decreases, but the risk of backdraft and collapse remains.","In factories, high-rack storage, pallets and liquids can bring a fire to the fully developed stage faster than in homes.","Pressurized gas and liquid tanks heated by fire can explode as a BLEVE — evacuate the surrounding area quickly.","The factory plan must keep the access route for 115 teams and the fire hydrant locations ready and unobstructed.","Fire watch after hot work: during the work and for at least 1 hour after it is finished, then monitoring for up to 3 more hours as decided by the permit authorizing individual (NFPA 51B)."]},{heading:"7. Fire load and risk assessment",body:"Assessing the hazard in the factory:",points:["Fire load density (MJ/m²): the amount of energy that combustible materials can release per square meter. Average values from EN 1991-1-2 (Eurocode 1):","• Dwelling: about 780 MJ/m².","• Office: about 420 MJ/m².","• Library: about 1500 MJ/m².","• Warehouses and factories: calculated from the materials stored, and often higher than these values.","Risk assessment: risk = likelihood of occurrence × severity of the consequences.","• Low: controlled by normal procedures.","• Medium: requires additional controls and monitoring.","• High: requires urgent action before work continues.","• Extreme: work stops until the risk is reduced.","The fire resistance rating of walls and roofs is set by building codes according to the type and use of the building (such as NFPA 101 and NFPA 5000).","Update the facility risk assessment (such as an HVA) at least once a year and after every major change."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fact_dust_explosion.mp4",title:"Dust explosion",description:"A dust explosion demonstration"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/fact_fire_classes.png",title:"Fire class chart",description:"Fire classification in factories"}],quiz:[{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable for a Class D fire?",options:[{text:"The nearest ABC dry powder extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"A Class D extinguisher listed for that metal",correct:!0},{text:"A CO₂ extinguisher aimed at the base of the fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"Only a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists the burning metal is suitable; the agent differs from metal to metal. Do not use water, CO₂ or ABC powder."},{question:"What does BLEVE stand for?",options:[{text:"Burning Liquid Emergency Venting Equipment",correct:!1},{text:"Boiling Liquid Expanding Vapor Explosion",correct:!0},{text:"A sprinkler system",correct:!1},{text:"A class of gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"A BLEVE is the explosion of a pressurized liquid tank when it is heated by fire and the vapor expands rapidly."},{question:"How many elements does the dust explosion pentagon have?",options:[{text:"3",correct:!1},{text:"4",correct:!1},{text:"5",correct:!0},{text:"6",correct:!1}],explanation:"Five elements: fuel + oxygen + heat + dispersion + confinement."},{question:"What does acetylene form with copper?",options:[{text:"Nothing — copper is safe for acetylene",correct:!1},{text:"Copper acetylide — an explosive compound",correct:!0},{text:"Cleaning",correct:!1},{text:"A protective green layer on the copper pipe",correct:!1}],explanation:"Acetylene forms a sensitive explosive compound with copper (copper acetylide) that detonates on impact or heating."},{question:"What hazard does aluminum powder present?",options:[{text:"Class A, like wood and paper",correct:!1},{text:"Class D and dust explosion",correct:!0},{text:"Gas only",correct:!1},{text:"No hazard",correct:!1}],explanation:"Aluminum powder is both a combustible metal (Class D) and a combustible dust (NFPA 484 / 654, now NFPA 660)."},{question:"According to EN 1991-1-2, what is the approximate average fire load in dwellings?",options:[{text:"100 MJ/m²",correct:!1},{text:"230 MJ/m²",correct:!1},{text:"420 MJ/m²",correct:!1},{text:"780 MJ/m²",correct:!0}],explanation:"The average fire load in dwellings is about 780 MJ/m², and in offices about 420 MJ/m² (EN 1991-1-2, Table E.4)."},{question:"A deep fryer in the staff canteen catches fire. Which extinguisher is right?",options:[{text:"A wet chemical extinguisher (NFPA K, EN F)",correct:!0},{text:"A CO₂ extinguisher, because it leaves no residue",correct:!1},{text:"A water extinguisher, because it cools the oil",correct:!1},{text:"An ABC powder extinguisher, because it covers every class",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cooking oil is NFPA Class K, which is Class F in EN 2; a wet chemical extinguisher forms a soap-like layer and cools the oil. Water spreads the fire and ABC does not cover K/F. Turn off the heat too if it is safe."},{question:"Two extinguishers stand by a panel: one has a black band, the other a blue band. How do you choose?",options:[{text:"I read the label: the classes and agent are printed on it",correct:!0},{text:"I take the black one, because black always means electrical",correct:!1},{text:"I take the blue one, because blue means Class C fires",correct:!1},{text:"I take either, because band colors mean the same thing",correct:!1}],explanation:"Color is never the fire class: colored bands (such as BS 7863) only show the agent and are not the same in every country. The classes and instructions are on the label."}],steps:[{title:"Know the fuels in your area",detail:"Identify: ordinary solids (A), flammable liquids and gases (B), live electrical equipment (C), metals (D) and cooking oil (K) — NFPA letters as the project reference. If labels use EN classes: NFPA B (gases) = EN C and NFPA K = EN F."},{title:"Read the extinguisher label",detail:"Make sure the nearby extinguishers are listed for those classes. The color of the body or band is not the class; always read the label. ABC powder covers A, B and C only, not D or K."},{title:"The go/no-go check before PASS",detail:"Before you use an extinguisher, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If any answer is no, or the fire does not go out at once, get out.",critical:!0},{title:"Gas fire: stop the gas only if it is safe",detail:"A qualified person closes the gas valve only if it can be reached safely. Never put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped: unburned gas builds up and can explode. Otherwise get everyone out, raise the alarm and call 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Metal fire: only a listed Class D agent",detail:"Use only a Class D extinguisher whose label lists that metal; never water, CO₂, ordinary powder or foam. Call 115; if the fire is not small, get out.",critical:!0},{title:"Report and update the assessment",detail:"Report every fire, even a small one, send the used extinguisher for recharge and update the risk assessment when a new material or area is added."}],warnings:["Never use water on a cooking-oil fire (K / F) or a burning flammable liquid: it spreads the fire. CO₂ and ABC powder are not the right choice for cooking oil; use a wet chemical extinguisher.","Never put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped: unburned gas builds up and can explode.","Never use water, CO₂, ordinary powder or foam on a burning metal: they make the fire worse or cause an explosion."],tips:["Write the main fire classes of each area in the site fire plan (the project's NFPA letters, with the EN equivalent if the equipment uses EN).","When a new material arrives, read section 5 of its SDS (firefighting measures).","During routine visual checks, read the label of each extinguisher and keep it in its place.","Remember: color is never the class; the label decides."],activity:{prompt:"In the welding area, the acetylene hose has caught fire at the regulator and a long flame is coming out. The cylinder valve is on top of the cylinder. You are a trained welder. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I put out the flame with a CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"If the gas keeps flowing, putting out the flame lets gas build up and it can explode; CO₂ is not the right answer for a gas fire."},{text:"If I can reach the cylinder valve safely, I close it to stop the gas; if not, I get everyone out, raise the alarm and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a gas fire ends when the gas is stopped, if that is safe; otherwise evacuate and call 115."},{text:"I pour water on the flame to cool it",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not stop a gas flame; cooling cylinders from a distance is the 115 teams' job."},{text:"I carry the burning cylinder outside",correct:!1,feedback:"A heated cylinder can explode; going near it and moving it puts your life at risk."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the magnesium machining area, chips in a collection bin have caught fire with a very bright white light. You are a trained operator. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I put it out with a water hose",correct:!1,feedback:"Water and magnesium produce hydrogen, which can explode."},{text:"I raise the alarm and call 115, and only if the fire is small I use the Class D extinguisher listed for magnesium as its label says; otherwise I get out",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a metal fire needs a Class D agent listed for that metal, and the go/no-go check still applies."},{text:"I use the ABC powder extinguisher because it is for all fires",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC covers A, B and C only; it does not work on metals and may spread the fire."},{text:"I use the CO₂ extinguisher to cut off the oxygen",correct:!1,feedback:"Magnesium burns even in CO₂; CO₂ is not used on metals."}]},{prompt:"The fire is spreading to the dust on a nearby machine and the smoke is getting thicker. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep applying the Class D agent until it is out",correct:!1,feedback:"The fire is no longer small and the dust can cause a bigger explosion; the go/no-go answer is now no."},{text:"I stop, get everyone out, close the doors and give the 115 teams the information (type of metal, location, SDS)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: when a fire grows, the job becomes evacuation; your information protects the 115 teams."},{text:"I blow the dust off the machine with compressed air",correct:!1,feedback:"Compressed air disperses the dust in the air, which is one of the conditions for a dust explosion."},{text:"I use the foam extinguisher on the dust",correct:!1,feedback:"Foam contains water and is not used on burning metals."}]}],realCase:"In a plastics factory, a worker saw smoke rising from a pile of cartons next to an electrical panel. Before acting she checked: the fire was small, the alarm had been raised and 115 called, her exit was behind her and she was trained. She read the label and chose an extinguisher listed for ordinary solids and live electrical equipment, aimed at the base and the fire went out. She kept watching the area and reported it. The review found the cartons had been stored against the panel; storage was moved and the area in front of the panel was marked to stay clear."},{id:21,code:"FACT-121",icon:"ShowerHead",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Fire suppression systems in factories",subtitle:"Portable extinguishers + fixed systems (sprinkler/foam/CO₂)",summary:"Portable extinguishers and fixed fire suppression systems in factories",blocks:[{body:"Factories need two levels of fire suppression: 1) portable extinguishers for small fires in their early stage, 2) fixed suppression systems for continuous protection. Depending on the type of hazard: sprinklers (NFPA 13), foam (NFPA 11 / 16), CO₂ (NFPA 12), dry chemical (NFPA 17) and clean agent (NFPA 2001). This module explains these systems and the inspection schedule."},{heading:"1. Portable extinguishers — selection for the factory",body:"The 7 main types and a selection guide:",points:["ABC powder (multipurpose): the most common type for corridors and general areas — A, B, C (not D or K).","CO₂: for electrical panels and server rooms — B, C.","Foam (AFFF): for flammable liquid storage and diked areas — A, B.","Wet chemical: for the factory kitchen and cafeteria — K.","Clean agent: for server and control rooms.","Class D (agent listed on the label for the metal in use): for metal machining and aluminum smelting.","Water mist (fine deionized water): for A, and some models for C — according to the device listing.","Hazard levels under NFPA 10 (minimum Class A extinguisher rating):","• Light hazard (offices): 2-A.","• Ordinary hazard (typical factory): 2-A.","• Extra hazard (factory with large amounts of combustibles): 4-A.","Travel distance to an extinguisher:","• Class A: up to 23 meters (75 feet) at all hazard levels.","• Class B: 9 or 15 meters (30 / 50 feet) depending on the extinguisher rating and the hazard level."]},{heading:"2. Sprinkler systems — NFPA 13",body:"The most important fixed system in many factories:",points:["The 4 types of sprinkler system:","• Wet pipe: the pipes are always full of water — the fastest response and the most common type.","• Dry pipe: the pipes are filled with pressurized air or nitrogen — for areas where the temperature drops below 4°C.","• Pre-action: the valve opens only after detectors sense a fire, then the sprinkler head opens from the heat — for areas with sensitive equipment.","• Deluge: open heads that all discharge water at once — for high-hazard areas.","Activation temperature and glass bulb color:","• 57°C (orange): ordinary temperature rating — areas with low ambient temperatures.","• 68°C (red): ordinary temperature rating — the most common in offices and factories.","• 79°C (yellow) and 93°C (green): intermediate temperature rating — warmer areas.","• 141°C (blue): high temperature rating — boiler rooms and near hot equipment.","Coverage area per standard spray head: up to about 21 m² (light hazard), 12 m² (ordinary hazard) and 9 m² (extra hazard).","Water density: 0.10-0.40 gpm/ft² (about 4-16 mm/min) depending on the hazard level.","NFPA 25: inspection and testing on weekly, monthly, annual and 5-year schedules.","Sprinklers open only where the fire is; the heads do not all open at once (except in a deluge system)."]},{heading:"3. Foam systems — NFPA 11 / 16",body:"For large flammable liquid storage facilities:",points:["The 3 types of foam by expansion ratio:","• Low expansion (up to 20:1): tank fires and liquid spills.","• Medium expansion (20:1 to 200:1): diked areas.","• High expansion (200:1 to 1000:1): aircraft hangars and large enclosed spaces.","Types of foam concentrate:","• AFFF: for hydrocarbons (gasoline, diesel).","• AR-AFFF (Alcohol-Resistant): for polar solvents (alcohol, acetone).","• Fluoroprotein: for storage tanks.","• Fluorine-free foam (F3): the replacement for PFAS foams.","Application rate:","• Hydrocarbon spills: a minimum of about 4.1 L/min/m² (0.10 gpm/ft²).","• Polar solvents: according to the manufacturer's listing, generally higher.","PFAS hazard: AFFF contains fluorinated substances (PFAS) that accumulate in the body and the environment; many countries are moving to fluorine-free foam (F3).","In industry: tank farms, tanker loading racks and refineries."]},{heading:"4. CO₂ and clean agent systems — NFPA 12 / 2001",body:"For equipment rooms and total flooding:",points:["Total flooding with CO₂:","• Design concentration: a minimum of 34% (NFPA 12), and higher for some materials.","• Hazard: the extinguishing concentration is lethal to people — no one may remain in the room.","• Pre-discharge alarm and time delay: must be long enough to evacuate the room before the gas is released (NFPA 12).","• Applications: generator rooms, engine test cells and printing presses.","Clean agent (FK-5-1-12 / HFC-227ea):","• NOAEL: in occupied spaces, the design concentration does not exceed the no-observed-adverse-effect level (NOAEL) (NFPA 2001); even so, evacuate the room.","• Design concentration: generally about 4-9% depending on the agent.","• Discharge: halocarbon agents within 10 seconds (NFPA 2001).","• Applications: server rooms, control rooms and switchgear rooms.","• It costs much more than water-based systems.","Halon 1301: under the Montreal Protocol, its production has been halted in developed countries since 1994 because it depletes the ozone layer; new systems are not installed with it.","Hydrostatic testing of portable extinguishers (NFPA 10): CO₂ every 5 years, clean agent every 12 years; fixed-system cylinders according to NFPA 12 / 2001 and cylinder regulations."]},{heading:"5. Dry chemical systems — NFPA 17",body:"For special hazards:",points:["Pre-engineered systems:","• Commercial kitchens: wet chemical systems (NFPA 17A) are now generally used, not dry chemical.","• Paint spray booths.","• Protection of heavy equipment and vehicles.","• Dip tanks.","Agents:","• Sodium bicarbonate: B, C.","• Potassium bicarbonate (Purple-K): B, C — more effective than sodium bicarbonate on Class B fires.","• Monoammonium phosphate: A, B, C.","• Potassium chloride: B, C.","Activation: a heat detector or a manual pull station.","After discharge: the powder spreads everywhere and can damage electronic equipment; cleanup is difficult.","The coverage area of each nozzle is set by the system listing."]},{heading:"6. Fire detection — NFPA 72",body:"The detection system — the first line of warning:",points:["Types of detectors:","• Smoke: photoelectric for smoldering fires, ionization for flaming fires.","• Heat: fixed temperature (for example 57°C) or rate-of-rise.","• Flame: UV/IR detectors, which detect flames quickly.","• Gas: LEL detectors and CO sensors.","• Air sampling: for server rooms — the most sensitive type.","Spacing and coverage:","• Smoke: spacing between detectors of about 9 meters (30 ft) on a smooth ceiling (NFPA 72).","• Heat: according to the detector's listed spacing.","• Flame: according to the device's field of view and listed distance.","Alarm notification:","• Audible: generally at least 15 dB above the average ambient sound level (NFPA 72).","• Visual (strobe): for noisy areas and for people with hearing impairments.","• Voice / mass notification: for large buildings and facilities.","Inspection and testing: according to the NFPA 72 schedules (many devices are tested once a year)."]},{heading:"7. Inspection and maintenance schedule",body:"According to NFPA standards:",points:["Daily / weekly:","• Visual inspection (gauge pressure, damage).","• Check that nothing is obstructing the sprinklers.","• Running the fire pump without water flow (no-flow test): diesel pumps every week, electric pumps generally every month (NFPA 25).","Monthly:","• Visual inspection of portable extinguishers (NFPA 10).","• Inspection of the fire alarm panel and trouble signals.","• Inspection of manual pull stations — visible and unobstructed.","Annually:","• Annual maintenance of portable extinguishers by a qualified person (not hydrostatic testing).","• Inspection of sprinklers from floor level (NFPA 25).","• A sample of foam concentrate sent for testing.","• Testing of detectors and alarms (NFPA 72).","Every few years:","• Hydrostatic test of extinguishers (pressure test of the cylinder, NFPA 10): every 5 years for water, foam, wet chemical, CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical units; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical units with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells, and for clean agent units. Separately, every 6 years, stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need the 12-year test are emptied and checked inside by a qualified person. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.","• Internal inspection of sprinkler piping: every 5 years (NFPA 25).","• Replacement or sample testing of sprinkler heads after 50 years (standard heads — NFPA 25).","Recordkeeping: document every inspection and test, and keep the records as required by the standards and the relevant authorities."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/fact_sprinkler_demo.mp4",title:"Sprinkler activation",description:"A sprinkler demonstration"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/fact_systems_chart.png",title:"Suppression systems chart",description:"Fixed systems"}],quiz:[{question:"For most factories, which is the most important fixed system?",options:[{text:"Portable extinguishers",correct:!1},{text:"Sprinklers (NFPA 13)",correct:!0},{text:"CO₂ only",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"Sprinklers operate automatically and control a fire in its early stage, even when no one is on site; portable extinguishers are only for small fires."},{question:"Where is a wet pipe system not suitable?",options:[{text:"Equipment rooms (ICT)",correct:!1},{text:"Offices with a normal room temperature",correct:!1},{text:"Areas where the temperature drops below 4°C",correct:!0},{text:"Heated warehouses that stay above 10°C all year",correct:!1}],explanation:"In cold areas the water in the pipes freezes; a dry pipe system is suitable."},{question:"Are new systems installed with Halon 1301?",options:[{text:"Yes",correct:!1},{text:"No — its production has been halted under the Montreal Protocol",correct:!0},{text:"Yes — it is still the standard agent for new ICT rooms",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, but only in new server rooms where clean agents cannot be used",correct:!1}],explanation:"Halon 1301 depletes the ozone layer; its production in developed countries has been halted since 1994."},{question:"Why must everyone leave the room before a total flooding CO₂ system discharges?",options:[{text:"It is not necessary; CO₂ is harmless at any concentration in the room",correct:!1},{text:"Because the extinguishing concentration (34% and above) is lethal to people",correct:!0},{text:"Because CO₂ sprays water",correct:!1},{text:"Because the CO₂ discharge is so loud that it can permanently damage people's hearing",correct:!1}],explanation:"The CO₂ design concentration in total flooding systems is at least 34% (NFPA 12), and this concentration causes unconsciousness and death within a short time; that is why there is a pre-discharge alarm and a time delay."},{question:"What is the approximate coverage area per standard spray sprinkler head?",options:[{text:"2-5 m²",correct:!1},{text:"12-21 m²",correct:!0},{text:"30-40 m²",correct:!1},{text:"80-100 m²",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NFPA 13: about 21 m² for light hazard and 12 m² for ordinary hazard; for extra hazard it is smaller (about 9 m²)."},{question:"What does the weekly test of a diesel fire pump involve?",options:[{text:"A visual inspection only, without starting the pump",correct:!1},{text:"Starting the pump without water flow (no-flow test)",correct:!0},{text:"A hydrostatic test",correct:!1},{text:"Running the pump at full water flow for a whole day",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA 25: the diesel pump is started and tested without water flow every week, and the electric pump generally every month."},{question:"You are working in a generator room protected by CO₂ and the pre-discharge alarm sounds. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave at once and close the door behind me",correct:!0},{text:"I find the fire and use the portable extinguisher first",correct:!1},{text:"I stay low in the room until the gas has been released",correct:!1},{text:"I open the windows and wait inside for it to clear",correct:!1}],explanation:"The extinguishing concentration of CO₂ can kill; the pre-discharge delay is there for you to get out. Staying low does not help, because CO₂ is heavier than air and collects near the floor."},{question:"Which practice is wrong in a sprinklered warehouse?",options:[{text:"Stacking boxes right up to the sprinkler heads",correct:!0},{text:"Keeping the space under the heads clear of stock",correct:!1},{text:"Reporting a head damaged by a forklift",correct:!1},{text:"Checking that the control valve is open and secured",correct:!1}],explanation:"A sprinkler needs clear space below it so the water reaches the fire; goods stacked close block it. The other three practices keep the system working."}],steps:[{title:"Know which system protects your area",detail:"Sprinklers, foam, CO₂, clean agent or chemical: know what happens when the system operates, what its warning sounds like and where the manual release stations are."},{title:"Pre-discharge alarm: leave at once",detail:"In a room protected by CO₂ or clean agent, when the pre-discharge alarm sounds, leave at once and close the door behind you; do not stay to fight the fire.",critical:!0},{title:"Never obstruct the system",detail:"Keep sprinkler heads, detectors, nozzles, valves and release stations clear; do not stack goods right under sprinkler heads."},{title:"Report any impairment",detail:"Report a closed valve, a trouble signal on the panel or a damaged sprinkler head at once. The person in charge follows the impairment procedure."},{title:"Portable extinguisher: check before PASS",detail:"Only if every answer is yes: small fire, right extinguisher, exit behind you, alarm raised and 115 called, and you are trained. Start from the distance on the label; PASS for most units, the others as their label says.",critical:!0},{title:"After a discharge: stay out and restore",detail:"Do not enter a room where CO₂ or clean agent has been released until it is ventilated and the responsible person clears it. A used extinguisher is recharged after any use, and qualified people put the system back in service.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never enter a room after a CO₂ discharge until it is ventilated and cleared: the extinguishing concentration can kill.","Never close a sprinkler valve or silence the fire alarm without authorization and the impairment procedure: the plant is left unprotected and nobody knows.","Never stack goods so close to sprinkler heads that they block the spray: the sprinkler cannot reach the fire."],tips:["Know where the main sprinkler control valve and the manual release stations are, and who may operate them.","Teach staff what the pre-discharge alarm means and what the signs on room doors say.","Put an impairment tag on any valve taken out of service and write it in the log.","Keep inspection records at hand; follow the inspection intervals set by Civil Defense and the competent authority."],activity:{prompt:"You are working in a control room protected by a clean agent system. Smoke is coming from a cabinet and the pre-discharge alarm sounds. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay and fight the cabinet fire with a portable extinguisher before the agent is released",correct:!1,feedback:"The pre-discharge alarm means leave the room; the system itself will fight the fire."},{text:"I leave at once, close the door, raise the alarm and tell the person in charge; I do not go back in until cleared",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a closed door keeps the agent concentration in the room so it can put out the fire, and you stay away from the agent and smoke."},{text:"I press the abort switch so I can finish my work",correct:!1,feedback:"The abort switch is for false alarms and the responsible person's decision, not for finishing work while there is smoke."},{text:"I hold the door open so the agent can escape and I can watch",correct:!1,feedback:"An open door lowers the agent concentration so the fire is not put out, and smoke spreads."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At the weekend a contractor wants to close the sprinkler control valve to repair a pipe in the warehouse. The warehouse still holds stock and some workers. You are the plant manager. What do you decide?",options:[{text:"Only under the impairment procedure: authorization, notifying those the plan names, a short time, a fire watch and no hot work",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the repair is needed, but while the system is out the plant needs extra protection and everyone concerned must know."},{text:"I let him close it quietly so the alarm does not ring",correct:!1,feedback:"A closed system that nobody knows about is the most dangerous; in a fire nobody expects the water not to come."},{text:"I refuse any repair at all, ever",correct:!1,feedback:"The system needs maintenance; refusing leads to bigger failures. The answer is a safe procedure."},{text:"I let him close it and leave it closed until Monday's check",correct:!1,feedback:"Any impairment must be short and watched; leaving it for days leaves the plant unprotected."}]},{prompt:"The repair is finished. What must happen before the contractor leaves?",options:[{text:"The valve is fully opened, a qualified person confirms the system is back in service, the tag is removed, the log is written and everyone notified is told",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a return to service is proven by a check and a record, not by word of mouth."},{text:"We leave the valve as it is; the next inspection will find it",correct:!1,feedback:"This is the very failure that leaves plants unprotected; open and check before leaving."},{text:"We open the valve halfway so no water leaks",correct:!1,feedback:"A half-open valve may not deliver the water a fire needs; it must be fully open and secured."},{text:"We only inform the contractor's boss",correct:!1,feedback:"Those notified when it was closed must know it is back in service, and a record is required."}]}],realCase:"In a paper warehouse, a sprinkler valve had been closed for a repair and was never reopened. A routine visual check found the valve closed and the system out of service. The manager stopped hot work, set up a fire watch, informed those named in the site plan and had a qualified technician reopen the valve and test the system the same day. The investigation found there had been no impairment tag or log. The site introduced valve tags and an impairment log, and trained the maintenance staff on them."},{id:22,code:"BUS-122",icon:"Building2",accent:"#0f766e",title:"Fire safety in shops, markets and malls",subtitle:"Exits, storage, electricity, LPG and staff roles in an emergency",summary:"Shops, markets and malls are full of customers who do not know the building, stock that burns easily, electrical equipment and often LPG cylinders. This lesson is for owners, managers and staff: how to keep exits and fire equipment clear, how to prevent electrical and gas fires, and what each member of staff does when the alarm sounds. Local licensing and inspection rules are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority; follow their requirements.",blocks:[{heading:"1. Exits, escape routes and storage",body:"Customers usually try to leave the way they came in, so every way out must be ready at all times:",points:["Keep exits, corridors and stairs completely clear: no stock, boxes, carts or stalls in the way, not even for a short time.","Exit doors must open from the inside at once, without a key or code, whenever people are inside; remove chains, bars and extra locks before opening time.","Keep exit signs visible: goods, posters and decorations must not hide an exit door or its sign.","Keep extra stock in a storeroom, not in corridors, under stairs or in areas where customers walk.","Do not pile goods, cardboard or packaging against electrical panels, lights, heaters or cooking equipment.","In a market or mall, agree with the management on the shared exits, the alarm and the assembly point, and take part in joint drills."]},{heading:"2. Electricity: overloads and extension leads",body:"Overloaded sockets and cables that overheat are common causes of fire in shops:",points:["Do not overload sockets or power strips, and use power strips with built-in overload protection.","Extension leads are for temporary use only and never replace fixed wiring; if you need more sockets, have a qualified electrician install them.","Plug refrigerators, freezers, heaters, ovens and other large appliances directly into a wall socket, never into an extension lead.","Do not run cables under carpets or mats, through doorways or across walkways, where they can be crushed or make people trip.","Replace damaged cables and plugs at once. A burning smell, a hot plug, sparks or a breaker that keeps tripping means: switch off and call a qualified electrician."]},{heading:"3. LPG cylinders in shops and markets",body:"LPG is heavier than air: leaking gas collects near the floor and in low places such as drains and basements.",points:["Keep in the shop only the smallest number of cylinders the business needs; keep the rest in a separate, ventilated store, preferably in the open air.","Never keep cylinders below ground level, in a basement or next to drains.","Keep cylinders upright with the valve protected, where customers cannot touch them, away from heat, flames and things that burn.","Never keep cylinders on stairs, beside exit doors or anywhere they block the way out.","Only a qualified technician connects regulators, hoses and appliances. Never look for a leak with a flame.","How many cylinders a shop may keep, and where, is set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]},{heading:"4. If you smell gas",body:`1. Do not light any flame and do not switch lights, appliances or machines on or off. Do not use a phone inside.
2. Stop sales and get customers and staff out calmly through the nearest exit.
3. Close the gas valve only if it is close to you and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark; otherwise do not go near it.
4. Keep people away from the entrance and warn the neighboring shops.
5. Call 115 from outside, and do not let anyone back in until the specialists say it is safe.`,critical:!0},{heading:"5. Staff roles when the alarm sounds",body:"Every member of staff knows their role before an emergency. The emergency plan names a person for each task on every shift:",points:['Raise the alarm: shout "Fire!", press the nearest fire alarm call point if there is one, and make sure someone calls 115 from a safe place.',"Guide customers: stop sales, tell customers calmly to leave and point them to the nearest exit, not only to the main entrance.","Check: look into toilets, fitting rooms, storerooms and offices on your way out, only if it is safe, and close doors behind you.","Help: assist those who need it, such as older people, children who are alone and people with disabilities. Never use the elevators.","At the assembly point: count the staff, report anyone missing, and tell the firefighters where people may still be and about gas cylinders and other dangers.","Nobody goes back inside until Civil Defense says it is safe."]},{heading:"6. Getting a crowd out safely",body:"In a busy shop or mall, the way staff guide people decides how quickly everyone gets out:",points:['Speak calmly and give short, clear instructions, such as: "Leave by this exit. Walk, do not run."',"Use every available exit, and send people to another exit if one becomes crowded or smoke blocks it.","Do not let people stop to pay, collect goods or go back inside.","Keep the number of people inside within the limit set for the building, especially on holidays and sale days.","In a mall, follow the instructions of the mall's emergency team and the announcements, and move well away from the building to the assembly point."]},{heading:"7. Fire equipment, first-aid kit and AED",body:"Equipment saves lives only if people can see it, reach it and use it:",points:["Keep fire extinguishers, fire alarm call points and fire blankets visible, in their place and unobstructed; check every day that nothing is in front of them.","Use an extinguisher only if you are trained, the fire is small and just starting, the label shows that it suits this fire, others have been warned, 115 has been called and your exit is behind you. Otherwise get out and close the door.","Each extinguisher needs a visual check every month (in its place, safety pin and tamper seal intact, pressure gauge in the green, no damage) and servicing by a qualified person; the local schedule is set by Civil Defense.","Keep a complete first-aid kit where staff can reach it quickly, and know who on each shift is trained in first aid.","If the shop or mall has an AED, every member of staff knows where it is. If someone collapses and is not breathing normally: call 122, start CPR and send someone to bring the AED at once."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_shop_evacuation.webp",title:"Evacuating a shop",description:"A staff member guides customers to the nearest exit while a colleague checks the storeroom"}],quiz:[{question:"The storeroom is full and a delivery arrives. Where may the extra boxes go?",options:[{text:"In the corridor to the fire exit, until the end of the day",correct:!1},{text:"Under the stairs, as long as customers cannot see them",correct:!1},{text:"In another storeroom or a place away from exits and walkways",correct:!0},{text:"In front of the fire extinguisher, where there is floor space",correct:!1}],explanation:"Exits, corridors and stairs must stay clear at all times, even for a short time, and nothing may block fire equipment. Extra stock goes in a storeroom away from exits and walkways."},{question:"A new display refrigerator needs power. What is the safe way to connect it?",options:[{text:"Plug it into the power strip that already feeds the lights and the cash register",correct:!1},{text:"Plug it into a wall socket; an electrician adds more sockets if needed",correct:!0},{text:"Run an extension lead to it under the floor mat so nobody trips over it",correct:!1},{text:"Join two extension leads together to reach the socket by the door",correct:!1}],explanation:"Large appliances go directly into a wall socket. Extension leads are temporary and overload easily, and a cable under a mat can be crushed and overheat."},{question:"Staff smell gas strongly near the shop's LPG cylinders, and the valve is in the middle of the smell. What is right?",options:[{text:"Walk to the valve, close it and switch on the fan to clear the gas",correct:!1},{text:"Switch on the lights to find the leak and test the hose with a lighter",correct:!1},{text:"Phone the gas supplier from inside the shop while looking for the leak",correct:!1},{text:"Stay away from the valve, get everyone out and call 115 from outside",correct:!0}],explanation:"The valve is closed only if it can be reached without going into the gas and without any spark. Here it cannot, so nobody goes near it: no switches, no phones inside, everyone out, and 115 is called from outside."},{question:"The fire alarm sounds in a busy shop. What does the staff member responsible for the toilets and storerooms do?",options:[{text:"Checks them on the way out if safe, then reports at the assembly point",correct:!0},{text:"Stays at the cash register to finish the sales before leaving the building",correct:!1},{text:"Locks the storerooms so that nobody can take any goods during the fire",correct:!1},{text:"Goes back inside after the count to search for anyone who is missing",correct:!1}],explanation:"Checking toilets and storerooms on the way out, only if it is safe, makes sure nobody is left behind. The result is reported at the assembly point; nobody goes back inside."},{question:"During an evacuation, customers crowd at the main entrance while another exit is free. What do staff do?",options:[{text:"Tell everyone to wait in the shop until the main entrance is clear",correct:!1},{text:"Calmly direct people to the free exit with short, clear instructions",correct:!0},{text:"Open the elevators so that more people can leave the floor at once",correct:!1},{text:"Ask customers to pay quickly so that the line at the register moves faster",correct:!1}],explanation:"People tend to leave the way they came in. Staff use every available exit and calmly guide people to a free one; elevators are never used in a fire."},{question:"A manager wants to stop thefts through the back exit during opening hours. Which solution keeps the exit safe?",options:[{text:"A lock that opens from the inside at once, without a key or code",correct:!0},{text:"A padlock and chain, with the key kept at the cash desk",correct:!1},{text:"Boxes stacked against the door so that it cannot be pushed open",correct:!1},{text:"A code lock that only the manager knows how to open",correct:!1}],explanation:"Exit doors must open from the inside at once, without a key or code, whenever people are inside. A chain, a key kept elsewhere, boxes or a code all delay the escape in a fire."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm",detail:'Shout "Fire!", press the nearest fire alarm call point if there is one and make sure someone calls 115 from a safe place.',critical:!0},{title:"Stop sales and guide customers out",detail:"Tell customers calmly to leave by the nearest exit, not only by the main entrance. Use every available exit and do not let anyone stop to pay or collect goods.",critical:!0},{title:"Check on the way out",detail:"Look into toilets, fitting rooms, storerooms and offices only if it is safe, and close doors behind you to slow the smoke."},{title:"Help those who need it",detail:"Help older people, children who are alone and people with disabilities to the nearest exit. Never use the elevators.",critical:!0},{title:"Count and report at the assembly point",detail:"Count the staff, report anyone missing and tell the firefighters where people may still be and about gas cylinders and other dangers. Nobody goes back inside until Civil Defense says it is safe.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never lock or chain an exit door while people are inside: in a fire, a locked exit can trap customers and staff.","Never leave stock in corridors, on stairs or in front of fire equipment, not even for a short time: blocked routes slow the escape and hide the equipment.","Never use a flame to look for a gas leak, and never switch anything on or off when you smell gas: a spark can ignite it."],tips:["Walk the exit routes every day before opening: check that the doors open from the inside and the signs can be seen.","Name a person for each emergency task on every shift, and write the names in the emergency plan.","On their first day, show every new member of staff the exits, the call points, the extinguishers and the assembly point."],activity:{prompt:"It is the morning before opening. You find the back exit door chained shut after a theft last week, and boxes from a delivery are stacked in the corridor to it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I remove the chain and the boxes before opening, tell the manager and ask for a way to stop theft that still lets the door open from the inside at once",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: exit doors must open from the inside without a key whenever people are inside, and the way to them stays clear at all times."},{text:"I leave the chain on; the main entrance is enough for customers",correct:!1,feedback:"In a fire or in smoke, customers may not be able to reach the main entrance; every exit must be ready to use."},{text:"I move the boxes but keep the chain and leave the key at the cash desk",correct:!1,feedback:"In a fire nobody has time to fetch a key; exit doors open at once from the inside without a key or code."},{text:"I unchain the door but leave the boxes, because they will only be there until the evening",correct:!1,feedback:"Corridors to exits must stay clear at all times, even for a short time: boxes slow people down and can catch fire."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a busy sale day, a customer tells you that smoke is coming from under the door of the storeroom at the back of the shop. You are the shift supervisor. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I raise the alarm, make sure 115 is called, stop sales and start getting everyone out by the nearest exits",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm and the call come first, then everyone out. The storeroom door stays closed."},{text:"I open the storeroom door wide to see how big the fire is",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the door lets air in and smoke out, and the fire can grow at once. Keep it closed and raise the alarm."},{text:"I finish the sales in progress so that customers do not leave without paying",correct:!1,feedback:"Lives come before goods and money. Stop sales at once and get people out."},{text:"I send a member of staff into the storeroom with buckets of water",correct:!1,feedback:"Nobody goes into smoke, and the storeroom may hold electrical equipment or aerosols. An extinguisher is used only after the go/no-go check."}]},{prompt:"Customers are leaving. Many crowd at the main entrance, smoke is now in the corridor near the storeroom and an older man is walking slowly near the fitting rooms. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I calmly send people to the other free exit, ask a colleague to help the older man out and we close doors behind us",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: use every exit, help those who need it and close doors to slow the smoke; then count everyone at the assembly point."},{text:"I send everyone through the main entrance, because customers know that way best",correct:!1,feedback:"The main entrance is already crowded. Staff guide people to every available exit."},{text:"I go into the smoky corridor to check the storeroom once more",correct:!1,feedback:"Never go into smoke. Tell the firefighters where the fire is and what is stored there."},{text:"I tell the older man to wait in a fitting room until the firefighters arrive",correct:!1,feedback:"He can get out with help, and the smoke is spreading. Staff help people who need it to get out."}]}],realCase:'In a clothing shop, an overloaded power strip behind the counter began to smoke. A member of staff noticed the burning smell, shouted "Fire!" and pressed the alarm call point, and a colleague called 115 from outside. The supervisor stopped sales and sent customers to both exits, not only to the main door. Another colleague checked the fitting rooms and the toilet on her way out and closed the doors behind her. At the assembly point the supervisor counted the staff and told the firefighters about the gas cylinder in the back room. Everyone got out because the exits were clear and each person knew their role.'},{id:23,code:"BUS-123",icon:"CookingPot",accent:"#c2410c",title:"Fire safety in restaurants and commercial kitchens",subtitle:"Oil and grease fires, hood systems, gas and staff drills",summary:"A commercial kitchen brings heat, oil, grease and gas together in a small space, and a fire in a pan or fryer can spread into the hood and its duct within moments. This lesson is for owners, managers, cooks and service staff: how to deal with an oil fire, what the kitchen hood fire suppression system does, how to handle gas safely, why grease must be cleaned and how to get customers out. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority.",blocks:[{heading:"1. Oil and grease fires: never water",body:"Burning oil and fat need special handling:",points:["Never pour water on burning oil or fat: the water turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil out in a fireball.","Do not carry or move a burning pan or fryer.","A small fire in a pan: turn off the heat or gas at the appliance only if you can reach it safely, then slide a metal lid over the pan or cover it with a fire blanket, and leave it covered until it has cooled.","An ABC powder or foam extinguisher is not suitable for a deep fryer: the oil stays hot and can flare up again.","If the fire is not small, reaches the hood or does not go out at once: get out, close the door and call 115."],critical:!0},{heading:"2. The wet chemical extinguisher (Class K / Class F)",body:"The project uses NFPA 10 letters as its reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. So Class K (NFPA) and Class F (EN 2) are the same class: cooking oils and fats.",points:["A wet chemical extinguisher is made for cooking oil and fat: it forms a soap-like layer on the oil that cuts off the air and cools it.","If the kitchen has a hood suppression system, activate the system first (automatically or with the manual pull station), and only then use the extinguisher. A sign next to the extinguisher says this.","Read the label before an emergency: the class, the operating range and how to use it. Some wet chemical units are operated differently from PASS; follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.","Use it only if you are trained, the fire is small, the alarm has been raised and your exit is behind you.","The color of an extinguisher is not its class: always read the label."]},{heading:"3. The kitchen hood fire suppression system",body:"Many commercial kitchens have a fixed fire suppression system in the hood over the fryers, grills and ranges. Staff do not service it, but everyone must know how it works:",points:["When a fire under the hood heats the system, it starts by itself and sprays an extinguishing agent from nozzles onto the cooking surfaces and into the hood and its duct.","When it is activated, it also shuts off the gas or electricity to the cooking equipment under the hood. Only a qualified person restores it afterward.","If it does not start by itself, use the manual pull station. It is on the way out of the kitchen, every cook must know where it is, and nothing may block it.","Keep the hood filters clean and in place. Keep the nozzles clean, and never cover, bend or move them.","Do not move, add or swap cooking appliances under the hood without the system installer checking it: the nozzles are aimed at specific appliances.","A qualified company inspects and services the system regularly and attaches a service tag; the interval is set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]},{heading:"4. Gas cylinders, manifolds and pipework",body:"Gas in a kitchen is safe only when it is installed and handled correctly:",points:["Keep gas cylinders and their manifold (the pipe that joins several cylinders) outside the kitchen, in a ventilated place, upright and protected from heat and tampering; never in a basement or below ground.","Only a qualified gas technician installs, connects, repairs and services pipework, regulators and appliances. Staff change cylinders only if they have been trained to do it.","Train staff to look every day for obvious faults, such as a damaged hose or connection, a flame that goes out and does not stay lit or a smell of gas, and to report them and stop using the appliance.","Where the gas is interlocked with the extract fan, the gas stops when the fan stops. Never bypass or disable this interlock.","Every cook knows where the kitchen gas valve is, and uses it only as the gas-leak rule below allows.","Never look for a gas leak with a flame."]},{heading:"5. If you smell gas in the kitchen",body:`1. Do not light any flame and do not switch lights, fans or appliances on or off. Do not use a phone inside.
2. Close the gas valve only if it is close to you and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making any spark; otherwise do not go near it.
3. Get staff and customers out calmly and warn the neighbors.
4. Call 115 from outside, and do not go back in until the specialists say it is safe.`,critical:!0},{heading:"6. Grease: clean the hood, filters and duct",body:"Grease that builds up in the hood, the filters and the extract duct is fuel waiting for a flame:",points:["Flames from frying, grilling or flambéing can set built-up grease alight, and a fire in the duct can spread through the building.","Clean filters, hood surfaces, walls, floors and appliances on a fixed schedule, so that grease does not build up.","Have the extract duct cleaned regularly by a qualified company and keep the record. How often depends on the type and amount of cooking; the requirement is set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority.","Empty and clean a fryer only after the oil has cooled, and never move a fryer while the oil is hot.","Keep cardboard, cloths, paper and oil containers away from cooking equipment and heat."]},{heading:"7. Staff drills and getting customers out",body:"Everyone in the restaurant knows the plan from their first day at work:",points:["The emergency plan names who raises the alarm, who calls 115, who turns off the gas if it is safe, who guides customers and who counts staff at the assembly point.","Service staff calmly tell customers to leave by the nearest exit and do not let them stop to pay or collect belongings.","Check toilets, dining rooms and storerooms on the way out only if it is safe, and close doors behind you.","Keep the back door and the kitchen exit clear, and never lock exit doors while staff or customers are inside.","On their first day, show every new member of staff the exits, the pull station, the extinguisher, the fire blanket and the gas valve, and test the plan with drills.","At the assembly point, tell the firefighters whether the hood system has discharged, whether the gas is off and whether anyone is missing."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_kitchen_grease_fire.webp",title:"An oil fire in a commercial kitchen",description:"A cook slides a metal lid over a burning pan; the manual pull station and the wet chemical extinguisher are visible near the exit"}],quiz:[{question:"A small fire starts in a frying pan on the stove. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Pour a glass of water on the pan to cool the oil down quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Carry the pan outside so that the fire is out of the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"If safe, turn off the heat and slide a metal lid over the pan",correct:!0},{text:"Spray the pan with the ABC powder extinguisher from the office",correct:!1}],explanation:"Covering the pan cuts off the air, and turning off the heat stops the oil from getting hotter. Water makes burning oil erupt in a fireball, moving the pan spreads the fire, and ABC powder is not made for cooking oil."},{question:"A fire under the hood is not going out. The kitchen has a hood suppression system and a wet chemical extinguisher. What comes first?",options:[{text:"Activate the hood system with the pull station, then the extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"Use the extinguisher first and keep the hood system for a bigger fire",correct:!1},{text:"Switch off the extract fan and remove the filters to let the smoke out",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for the hood system to start by itself before doing anything",correct:!1}],explanation:"The hood system cuts off the gas or electricity and covers the cooking surfaces, the hood and the duct with extinguishing agent. The wet chemical extinguisher is designed for use after the system has been activated, as the sign next to it says."},{question:"Why must cooking appliances under the hood not be moved or swapped without the system installer?",options:[{text:"Heavier appliances can crack the floor tiles under the hood",correct:!1},{text:"The nozzles are aimed at specific appliances and may miss a new one",correct:!0},{text:"Moving them makes the kitchen much noisier for the cooks and the customers",correct:!1},{text:"New appliances always come with their own built-in fire system",correct:!1}],explanation:"The system protects the appliances it was designed for. If an appliance is moved or replaced, the installer must check that the nozzles still cover it."},{question:"Where should a restaurant's LPG cylinders and manifold be?",options:[{text:"In the basement, where they stay cool and out of the way of staff",correct:!1},{text:"Inside the kitchen under the range, close to the burners they feed",correct:!1},{text:"In the dining-room storeroom, behind the chairs that are stacked there",correct:!1},{text:"Outside in a ventilated place, upright and protected from heat",correct:!0}],explanation:"LPG is heavier than air and collects in low places such as basements. Cylinders are kept outside in a ventilated place, upright and away from heat, and a qualified technician makes the connections."},{question:"Why is cleaning grease from the hood, filters and duct a fire-safety task?",options:[{text:"Built-up grease can catch fire from cooking flames and spread in the duct",correct:!0},{text:"Grease only changes the smell of the food; it does not add to the fire risk",correct:!1},{text:"Clean ducts make the hood system go off by mistake far more often",correct:!1},{text:"A layer of grease protects the metal of the duct from the heat of cooking",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flames from frying, grilling or flambéing can ignite grease in the hood and duct, and a fire in the duct can spread through the building. Regular cleaning removes this fuel."},{question:"A new wet chemical extinguisher for the kitchen is marked Class F. A cook says the kitchen needs a Class K unit instead. What is right?",options:[{text:"Class F (EN 2) and Class K (NFPA) are the same class: cooking oil",correct:!0},{text:"Class F is for flammable gases, so it is wrong for a kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"Class F is a lower grade, so a Class K unit must be added",correct:!1},{text:"The letter does not matter, as long as the body is yellow",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA K = EN F: both letters mean cooking oils and fats. A color is never the class; read the label for the class, the range and how to use it."}],steps:[{title:"Never water, never move the pan",detail:"Do not pour water on burning oil or fat: it turns to steam and throws the burning oil out in a fireball. Do not carry or move a burning pan or fryer.",critical:!0},{title:"Switch off the heat if it is safe",detail:"Turn off the heat or the gas at the appliance only if you can reach the control safely, without leaning over the flames.",critical:!0},{title:"Smother a small pan fire",detail:"Slide a metal lid over the pan or lay a fire blanket over it gently, and leave it covered until it has cooled completely.",critical:!0},{title:"Under the hood: the system first",detail:"If the fire is in a fryer or reaches the hood, activate the hood system with the manual pull station on your way out. Only then, if you are trained, the fire is small and your exit is behind you, use the wet chemical extinguisher (NFPA K = EN F) as its label says."},{title:"If it does not go out at once, get out",detail:"Get staff and customers out, close the door and call 115. At the assembly point, tell the firefighters whether the hood system has discharged, whether the gas is off and whether anyone is missing.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never pour water on burning oil or fat: the water turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil out in a fireball.","Do not use an ABC powder or foam extinguisher on a deep fryer: the oil stays hot and can flare up again.","Never lift the lid or the fire blanket to look before the pan has cooled: fresh air can make the hot oil burst into flame again."],tips:["Keep the fire blanket and the wet chemical extinguisher on the way out of the kitchen, and check every day that nothing blocks them or the pull station.","While frying, keep a metal lid that fits the pan within reach of every cook.","Practice the pan-fire steps in drills with a cold pan and a lid, without a real fire."],activity:{prompt:"A new cook is frying when the oil in a deep pan catches fire. The flames are small and only in the pan. A colleague runs to the sink to fetch water. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop my colleague, turn off the heat if I can reach it safely and slide a metal lid or lay the fire blanket over the pan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: covering cuts off the air and turning off the heat stops the oil getting hotter. Leave it covered until it has cooled."},{text:"I let my colleague throw the water quickly, before the flames grow",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on burning oil turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil out in a fireball."},{text:"I carry the pan out through the back door and put it down outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving a burning pan spills burning oil, causes burns and spreads the fire."},{text:"I spray the pan with the ABC powder extinguisher from the dining room",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC powder is not made for cooking oil: the oil stays hot and can flare up again. Cover the pan instead."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During a busy dinner service, a fire in a fryer spreads into the hood above it. The hood system has not started by itself. You are the head cook. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I activate the hood system with the manual pull station on my way out and raise the alarm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the system covers the cooking surfaces, the hood and the duct and cuts off the gas or electricity. The extinguisher comes after it."},{text:"I use the wet chemical extinguisher first and keep the hood system for a bigger fire",correct:!1,feedback:"The order is the other way round: the hood system first, then the extinguisher, as the sign next to it says."},{text:"I pull out the hood filters so that the smoke can escape",correct:!1,feedback:"Reaching into the hood puts you in the flames and feeds the fire with air. Use the pull station and get out."},{text:"I pour a bucket of water into the fryer to cool the oil",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on burning oil turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil out in a fireball."}]},{prompt:"The hood system discharges and the gas to the cooking line shuts off. The flames in the fryer are lower but still burning, and smoke is reaching the dining room. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"Service staff get the customers out while I, if I am trained and my exit is behind me, use the wet chemical extinguisher as its label says; if the fire does not go out at once, I get out and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: customers leave as soon as smoke reaches them, and the wet chemical extinguisher is used after the system and only after the go/no-go check."},{text:"I turn the gas back on so that the other cooks can finish the orders",correct:!1,feedback:"After the system discharges, only a qualified person restores the gas or electricity, and the fire is still burning."},{text:"I keep the customers inside so that they do not panic in the street",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke is already in the dining room. Service staff calmly get customers out by the nearest exit."},{text:"I climb up to the hood to check whether the duct is burning",correct:!1,feedback:"A fire in the duct is for the 115 teams. Tell the firefighters that the system has discharged and where the fire is."}]}],realCase:"In a busy restaurant kitchen, oil in a frying pan caught fire. A new cook grabbed a jug of water, but the head cook stopped him. She turned off the gas at the appliance, which she could reach safely, slid a metal lid over the pan and left it covered. The flames went out and no oil was spilled. The manager called 115 to have the kitchen checked and later used the event in a staff drill: never water on burning oil, never move the pan and know where the lid, the fire blanket and the pull station are."},{id:24,code:"BUS-124",icon:"Building",accent:"#6d28d9",title:"Fire safety in hotels and guest accommodation",subtitle:"Night alarms, guest information, staff roles and guests who need help",summary:"In a hotel, guests sleep in a building they do not know, and some of them cannot leave without help. That makes a fire at night the greatest danger. This lesson is for owners, managers, reception and night staff: what guests must be told, the room-door rule, what each member of staff does at night, how to help guests with disabilities, and how smoke alarms and fire doors protect everyone. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority.",blocks:[{heading:"1. Why the night is the greatest danger",body:"A hotel fire plan must work at the worst possible time:",points:["Sleeping guests are slow to wake and may be confused, and they do not know the building or its exits.","Once the alarm sounds, there may be only a few minutes to get out safely.","Smoke spreads along corridors and up stairs; closed doors slow the spread of smoke, heat and fire.","At night the fewest staff are on duty, so the plan must work with the night team alone, and there must be enough staff to help guests who cannot leave by themselves."]},{heading:"2. Alarms, smoke detectors and fire doors",body:"These protect guests while they sleep, as long as they are kept working:",points:["Every guest room has smoke detection and an alarm loud enough to wake a sleeping guest.","Offer guests who are deaf or hard of hearing a room with a flashing-light or vibrating-pad alarm, where available.","Fire doors on corridors and stairs close by themselves. Never wedge or tie them open, and report at once a door that does not close fully.","A sprinkler system in each guest room gives extra protection where it is installed.","Check every day that escape routes are clear, exit doors open and the emergency lighting works; testing and service intervals are set by Civil Defense."]},{heading:"3. What every guest must know",body:"Give each guest the fire information at check-in:",points:["An escape plan on the inside of the room door shows where the room is and the routes to the nearest exits.","Tell guests what the fire alarm sounds like and where the two nearest exits are, and suggest that they count the doors from their room to the exit; this helps in the dark or in smoke.","Keep the room key by the bed and take it when you leave.","When the alarm sounds: leave at once, close the doors behind you and use the stairs, never the elevator.","Exit doors on escape routes open from the inside without a key at all times."]},{heading:"4. The room-door rule",body:"Teach it to staff and print it on the fire notice in every room:",points:["Before opening the room door, feel the door and the handle with the back of your hand. If it is hot, or smoke is coming in around it, keep it closed.","If the door is cool, open it slowly and be ready to shut it at once if there is smoke or fire outside.","If the corridor is clear, leave, close the door behind you and go to the nearest exit; if there is smoke, keep low.","If every way out is blocked: stay in the room with the door closed, block the gaps around the door and the air vents with towels or bedding, switch off fans and air conditioning, call 115 and give the room number, and signal at the window.","Do not jump from an upper floor; wait for the firefighters."]},{heading:"5. Staff roles at night",body:"The night plan names who does what, even when only one or two staff are on duty:",points:["Reception confirms the alarm, calls 115 at once and gives the address, where the fire is and how many guests are in the hotel.","Reception takes the list of occupied rooms and the list of guests who need help to the assembly point.","Staff alert guests and guide them to the exits on their way out, and check rooms, toilets and corridors only where it is safe; they never enter smoke or open a hot door.","At the assembly point, compare the list of occupied rooms with the people present.","Meet the firefighters when they arrive and tell them which rooms may still be occupied, where guests who need help are waiting and about any special dangers, such as gas.","Nobody goes back inside until Civil Defense says it is safe."]},{heading:"6. Guests with disabilities",body:"Plan for them before the night they need it:",points:["At check-in, ask guests politely and privately whether they would need help to leave in an emergency, and keep a list of their names and room numbers at reception.","Where possible, give these guests a room on a low floor, close to an exit.","Never use an ordinary elevator in a fire. A guest who cannot use the stairs is moved away from the fire through the fire doors on the same floor, or waits with a member of staff in the refuge next to a protected stairway, as the hotel plan says.","Never leave a guest alone in a refuge, and tell the firefighters at once exactly where they are waiting.","The plan must not depend on the firefighters to rescue guests who are waiting: there must be enough trained staff to help them."]},{heading:"7. Training and drills",body:"At night, staff can only do what they have practiced:",points:["Train every new member of staff before their first night shift: the alarm, the exits, the fire doors, the room lists and their own role.","Hold drills, including at the times when the fewest staff are on duty, and practice as if one exit or stairway were blocked.","Keep the emergency plan and the room lists within easy reach at reception, and update the lists at every check-in and check-out.","How often drills are held and what training is required are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_hotel_night_evacuation.webp",title:"Night evacuation in a hotel",description:"A night staff member with a flashlight guides guests along the corridor to the exit stairs, with a closed fire door behind them"}],quiz:[{question:"What should the escape plan on the inside of a guest room door show?",options:[{text:"Where the room is and the routes to the two nearest exits",correct:!0},{text:"Only the hotel's telephone number and the checkout time",correct:!1},{text:"The way to the elevator and the way to the main entrance only",correct:!1},{text:"Where the hotel restaurant and the parking lot are located",correct:!1}],explanation:"The plan on the door shows where the room is and the routes to the exits, so that a guest can find the way quickly, even in the dark. Elevators are not used in a fire."},{question:"The alarm sounds at night, and a guest feels that the room door handle is hot. What should the guest do?",options:[{text:"Open the door quickly and run along the corridor to the nearest stairs",correct:!1},{text:"Open the door a little to look into the corridor, then decide what to do",correct:!1},{text:"Keep the door closed, seal the gaps, call 115 and signal for help",correct:!0},{text:"Go down in the elevator, because the corridor may be full of smoke",correct:!1}],explanation:"A hot handle means fire or hot smoke behind the door. The closed door protects the guest: seal the gaps and vents, call 115, give the room number and signal at the window."},{question:"During a night evacuation, which task belongs to reception?",options:[{text:"Staying at the desk to answer guests' calls until the fire is out",correct:!1},{text:"Calling 115 and taking the list of occupied rooms to the assembly point",correct:!0},{text:"Going into every room, including rooms full of smoke, to search them",correct:!1},{text:"Collecting the guests' luggage from their rooms before leaving the building",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reception calls 115 at once and takes the room lists, so that people can be counted at the assembly point and firefighters know which rooms may still be occupied. Nobody enters smoke."},{question:"A guest who uses a wheelchair is on the third floor when the alarm sounds. What is right?",options:[{text:"A staff member takes them down in the ordinary elevator, which is faster than the stairs",correct:!1},{text:"Staff leave them alone in the refuge and wait outside for the firefighters to come",correct:!1},{text:"Staff tell them to stay in their room until the alarm stops ringing by itself",correct:!1},{text:"A staff member takes them to the refuge, stays with them and tells the firefighters",correct:!0}],explanation:"Ordinary elevators are not used in a fire. The guest is moved away from the fire or waits with a staff member in the refuge, is never left alone, and the firefighters know at once where they are."},{question:"Why must fire doors in hotel corridors never be wedged open?",options:[{text:"Open doors let cold air from the stairs into the guest corridors late at night",correct:!1},{text:"A closed fire door slows smoke, heat and fire and protects the escape route",correct:!0},{text:"Open doors make guests hear the alarm far too loudly inside their rooms",correct:!1},{text:"Fire doors are heavy, and a wedge slowly damages their hinges over time",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fire door protects only when it is closed: it slows the spread of smoke, heat and fire and keeps the escape route safe for guests. A door wedged open gives no such protection."},{question:"A guest who is deaf checks in. What should reception offer?",options:[{text:"A room with a flashing-light or vibrating-pad alarm, where available",correct:!0},{text:"A quiet room on the top floor, far from the noise of the stairs",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing extra, as the corridor alarm is loud enough for all guests",correct:!1},{text:"A note asking the guest to leave the room door open at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"A guest who is deaf or hard of hearing may not hear the alarm. A flashing-light or vibrating-pad alarm can wake them; ask politely and privately whether they would need help and, if so, add them to the list. An open door lets smoke in."}],steps:[{title:"Confirm the alarm and call 115",detail:"Reception confirms the alarm and calls 115 at once, giving the address, where the fire is and how many guests are in the hotel.",critical:!0},{title:"Take the lists",detail:"Reception takes the list of occupied rooms and the list of guests who need help to the assembly point."},{title:"Alert and guide guests",detail:"On their way out, staff knock on doors, alert guests and guide them to the stairs, never to the elevator. They never enter smoke or open a hot door.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay with guests who need help",detail:"A guest who cannot use the stairs is moved away from the fire through the fire doors on the same floor, or waits with a member of staff in the refuge, as the hotel plan says. Never leave them alone.",critical:!0},{title:"Count and brief the firefighters",detail:"At the assembly point, compare the list of occupied rooms with the people present. Tell the firefighters which rooms may still be occupied and where guests who need help are waiting. Nobody goes back inside until Civil Defense says it is safe."}],warnings:["Never wedge or tie a fire door open: an open door lets smoke, heat and fire into the corridors and stairs.","Never use an ordinary elevator in a fire or send guests to one: it can stop and trap them.","Never open a door that is hot to the touch: fire or hot smoke may be behind it."],tips:["Update the room lists at every check-in and check-out and keep them with the emergency plan at reception.","At the start of each night shift, walk the escape routes: check that they are clear, the fire doors close and the exit doors open.","Keep a flashlight at reception for every member of the night team.","Practice the night plan with the real number of night staff, as if one stairway were blocked."],activity:{prompt:"It is the middle of the night. The fire alarm sounds and shows a fire on the third floor. You are at reception, and one colleague is on the floors. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I call 115 with the address, where the fire is and how many guests are in, take the room lists and ask my colleague to alert guests on the way to the exits",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the call to 115 comes at once, the lists let everyone be counted and the colleague alerts guests without entering smoke."},{text:"I silence the alarm so as not to frighten the guests, then go up to see what is happening",correct:!1,feedback:"Silencing the alarm stops sleeping guests from waking, and going up delays the call. Call 115 at once."},{text:"I phone every occupied room one by one before calling 115",correct:!1,feedback:"This takes far too long. The alarm wakes the guests; 115 is called at once, and staff alert guests on their way out."},{text:"I leave the building at once and leave the lists behind, to save time",correct:!1,feedback:"Without the lists nobody can be counted, and the firefighters will not know which rooms may still be occupied."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Late at night, smoke is reported in a second-floor corridor and 115 has been called. A guest phones reception from a second-floor room: the door handle is hot and smoke is coming in under the door. What do you tell the guest?",options:[{text:"Keep the door closed, block the gaps and vents with towels or bedding, switch off fans and air conditioning and signal at the window; I will give your room number to the firefighters",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a hot handle means fire or hot smoke behind the door. The closed door protects the guest until the firefighters arrive."},{text:"Open the door and run quickly along the corridor to the nearest stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"A hot handle means fire or hot smoke behind the door. Opening it lets the smoke and heat into the room."},{text:"Take the elevator down, because the stairs may be full of smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"Ordinary elevators are never used in a fire: they can stop and trap people, and the guest would have to cross the corridor."},{text:"Open the window and jump if the smoke gets thicker",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not jump from an upper floor. Keep the door closed, signal at the window and wait for the firefighters."}]},{prompt:"The firefighters arrive. At the assembly point, your list shows two occupied rooms whose guests are not present, and the guest from the second-floor room is still inside. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I meet the firefighters and give them the numbers of the three rooms, where the guest is waiting and any special dangers",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the firefighters need exact room numbers at once. Counting against the list is what makes this possible."},{text:"I go back inside with a colleague to look for the missing guests",correct:!1,feedback:"Nobody goes back inside until Civil Defense says it is safe. Give the firefighters the room numbers instead."},{text:"I wait until the fire is out before mentioning the missing guests",correct:!1,feedback:"The firefighters need the information as soon as they arrive; any delay can cost a life."},{text:"I tell the firefighters that everyone is out, because most of the guests are present",correct:!1,feedback:"An incomplete count can cost a life. Report exactly which rooms are not accounted for."}]}],realCase:"Late at night, a smoke detector went off in a hotel room. The night receptionist called 115 at once, gave the address and the floor and took the list of occupied rooms and the list of guests who need help. A colleague knocked on doors along the corridor and guided guests to the stairs. A second colleague stayed with a guest who uses a wheelchair in the refuge beside the protected stairway, and the receptionist told the firefighters exactly where they were. The fire doors stayed closed, the smoke stayed on one floor and the count at the assembly point matched the list."},{id:25,code:"BUS-125",icon:"Fuel",accent:"#b45309",title:"Fuel station safety",subtitle:"Ignition control, static, spills, emergency stop and LPG",summary:"Fuel stations store and dispense large amounts of flammable liquid and gas. This module shows attendants, supervisors and managers how to control ignition sources and static electricity, deal with spills, use the emergency stop, fill LPG safely and act when a fire starts at the pump.",blocks:[{body:"Gasoline gives off flammable vapor even in cold weather (its flash point is about -43°C), and the vapor is heavier than air: it spreads along the ground and collects in low places such as drains, pits and basements. A spark, a flame, a cigarette or a running engine can ignite it. The rules for licensing, layout and equipment of fuel stations are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority: follow their requirements. This module covers what every attendant, supervisor and manager must do."},{heading:"1. Ignition control at the pumps",body:"Before and during fueling, the attendant makes sure that:",points:["The engine of the vehicle is switched off at the pump.","No one smokes or uses a lighter or open flame near the pumps, and no-smoking and engine-off signs are posted close to the pumps.","Control is constant: you can see every vehicle and every person at the pumps, and you start a pump only when there is no danger.","You do not fuel when the engine is running, when someone is smoking nearby or when the container is not approved; call the supervisor if the customer refuses.","Phones: apply the station's own rule (some guidance asks that phones and radios are not used at the pumps), and keep your attention on the main ignition sources: smoking, flames, running engines and static electricity.","The forecourt is kept clean, with no rags, cardboard or other flammable materials near the pumps."]},{heading:"2. Static electricity and portable containers",body:"A static spark at the pump nozzle can ignite fuel vapor:",points:["Customers should stay outside the vehicle while fueling: sliding across the seat builds up static. If they must get back in, they touch a metal part of the car away from the filling point before they touch the nozzle again.","Fill a portable container only when it stands on the ground, never in a car trunk, on a seat or in the back of a pickup truck.","Touch the container with the nozzle before opening it, and keep the nozzle in contact with the container opening while filling.","Fill only suitable, approved fuel containers, never bottles, buckets or food containers; which containers and sizes are allowed is set by the licensing authority.","Close the container tightly; fuel containers and gas cylinders are never carried in the passenger compartment of a car."]},{heading:"3. Fuel spills",body:"Trained staff can deal with a small spill, for example from overfilling; a large spill is an emergency:",points:["1. Stop the flow: release the nozzle or stop the pump; if it does not stop, press the emergency stop.","2. Keep people and vehicles away; no one nearby starts an engine, smokes or switches on any electrical equipment.","3. Wearing gloves, cover the spill with sand or absorbent material from the spill kit; do not walk through the fuel.","4. Do not wash the fuel away with water: water carries it into drains, where the vapor can collect and explode.","5. Collect the soaked material in a closed metal container: it is still flammable.","Call 115 at once if the fuel reaches a drain or the street, if fuel vapor is smelled inside a building, if the spill is large or if it catches fire."]},{heading:"4. The emergency stop",body:"The emergency stop cuts the power to all pumps at once. Where it must be installed and how often it is tested are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority:",points:["Every member of staff knows where each emergency stop is, inside at the cash desk and outside on the forecourt, and has practiced pressing it.","Press it for any spill you cannot stop, any fire, any leak at the LPG point and any vehicle that hits a pump.","Keep it visible, clearly labeled and never blocked by goods, displays or parked cars.","The emergency plan of the station includes a site map showing the tanks, pumps, emergency stops, extinguishers, spill kit and assembly point.","Every attendant knows the exact address of the station and how to describe its location to 115."]},{heading:"5. LPG (autogas) filling",body:"LPG is stored as a liquid under pressure. A leak forms a gas cloud that is heavier than air and can ignite far from the leak. In the project's reference profile, LPG fires are NFPA Class B (gases); in the EN 2 system they are Class C.",points:["Only trained staff fill LPG; customers do not fill it themselves unless the licensing authority allows it.","The attendant stays with the vehicle for the whole filling, until the equipment is switched off and the valves are closed.","Engines are off, and no one smokes or uses a flame near the filling point.","Wear gloves and eye protection: liquid LPG causes cold burns on the skin.","Check the vehicle's filling valve before connecting; if a connector leaks, stop filling.","If gas keeps leaking or catches fire: press the emergency stop, move people away and call 115. Close the gas supply only if it can be reached safely; never put out a gas flame whose supply cannot be stopped."]},{heading:"6. Fire at the pump",body:"If a vehicle or a pump catches fire:",points:["Press the emergency stop at once and raise the fire alarm.","Tell the driver to leave the nozzle in the car and get away; get everyone out of the vehicles and away from the pumps.","Call 115 (and 122 if anyone is hurt) and give the station's name and address first.","Keep everyone at least 30 meters (100 feet) from the burning vehicle, and keep other vehicles off the forecourt.","Do not open the hood or trunk of a burning car: fresh air makes the fire grow.","A trained attendant may use an extinguisher only on a small fire that has just started, with an extinguisher whose label covers burning fuel (Class B; NFPA B liquids = EN B), with the way out behind them and the alarm already raised. Start from the distance on the label; if the fire does not go out at once, get away.","Practice these steps in regular drills with all staff, and keep a record of each drill."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_fuel_station_safety.webp",title:"Safe fueling at the pump",description:"Engine off, container on the ground, emergency stop and extinguisher ready"}],quiz:[{question:"A customer got back into the car during fueling and is now returning to the pump. What should the customer do before touching the nozzle?",options:[{text:"Touch bare metal on the car, away from the filler",correct:!0},{text:"Wipe the nozzle handle with a dry cloth first",correct:!1},{text:"Pull the nozzle out quickly and restart the pump",correct:!1},{text:"Wait inside the car until the pump clicks off by itself",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sliding across the seat builds up static electricity. Touching metal away from the filling point discharges it before the hand reaches the nozzle, where a spark could ignite the vapor."},{question:"Fuel is pouring onto the forecourt from a nozzle that will not shut off. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Wash the fuel away toward the drain with a water hose",correct:!1},{text:"Stop the flow at the pump or with the emergency stop",correct:!0},{text:"Start the car and drive it away from the spill",correct:!1},{text:"Spread absorbent around it but leave the pump running",correct:!1}],explanation:"First stop the source, then keep ignition sources and people away and absorb the spill. Water carries fuel into drains, where vapor collects, and starting an engine can ignite the vapor."},{question:"Where must a portable fuel container be while it is filled?",options:[{text:"In the back of a pickup truck, ready to drive away",correct:!1},{text:"On the back seat, held steady by a passenger",correct:!1},{text:"On the ground, with the nozzle touching it",correct:!0},{text:"In the car trunk, with the trunk lid fully open",correct:!1}],explanation:"Inside a vehicle, carpets or plastic bed liners insulate the container, so the static from the flowing fuel cannot escape and can spark at the nozzle (NIOSH). On the ground, with the nozzle touching it, the charge goes safely to ground."},{question:"During LPG filling, gas starts to leak from the connector. What should the attendant do?",options:[{text:"Stop filling, close the valves, keep people and engines away",correct:!0},{text:"Finish the filling quickly and tighten the connector afterward",correct:!1},{text:"Spray the leak with a CO₂ extinguisher to cool the connector",correct:!1},{text:"Ask the driver to start the engine and drive off at once",correct:!1}],explanation:"Leaking LPG forms a gas cloud that can ignite. Stop the filling, close the gas supply if it can be reached safely and keep ignition sources away; if the leak does not stop, press the emergency stop and call 115."},{question:"A car catches fire at a pump. Which action is correct?",options:[{text:"Pull the nozzle out of the car before doing anything else",correct:!1},{text:"Open the hood so the fire can be seen and reached",correct:!1},{text:"Spray water from a hose onto the burning fuel",correct:!1},{text:"Press the emergency stop, clear the area and call 115",correct:!0}],explanation:"The emergency stop cuts the fuel to all pumps. Leave the nozzle in place, keep the hood closed and keep everyone well away; water spreads burning fuel."},{question:"During LPG filling, a customer asks to fill the gas himself so that the attendant can serve other pumps. What does the attendant do?",options:[{text:"Fills it himself, as trained, and stays until the valves are closed",correct:!0},{text:"Hands over the nozzle and goes back to serve the other pumps",correct:!1},{text:"Lets the customer fill and watches from the cash desk window",correct:!1},{text:"Starts the filling and comes back when the meter has stopped",correct:!1}],explanation:"Only trained staff fill LPG, unless the licensing authority allows customers to do it, and the attendant stays with the vehicle for the whole filling until the equipment is off and the valves are closed. Any leak must be stopped at once."}],steps:[{title:"Press the emergency stop",detail:"It cuts the power to all pumps at once. Then raise the fire alarm.",critical:!0},{title:"Get people away from the pumps",detail:"Tell the driver to leave the nozzle in the car and get away. Get everyone out of the vehicles and well away, and keep other vehicles off the forecourt.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115",detail:"Call 115 (and 122 if anyone is hurt) and give the station's name and address first.",critical:!0},{title:"Extinguisher only after the go/no-go check",detail:"A trained attendant may tackle only a small fire that has just started, with an extinguisher whose label covers burning fuel (Class B), the exit behind them and the alarm raised. Start from the distance on the label; if the fire does not go out at once, get away."},{title:"Meet the 115 teams",detail:"Give them the site map and tell them where the tanks, the LPG point and the emergency stops are, and whether anyone is hurt or missing."}],warnings:["Never wash a fuel spill away with water: water carries the fuel into drains, where the vapor can collect and explode.","Never open the hood or trunk of a burning car: fresh air makes the fire grow. Leave the nozzle in the car.","Never put out a burning LPG leak if the gas supply cannot be stopped: unburned gas can build up and explode."],tips:["At the start of each shift, check that every emergency stop is visible and unblocked and that the spill kit is full.","Keep the station's exact address and a short description of how to find it next to the phone at the cash desk.","Practice pressing the emergency stop and using the spill kit in drills, and keep a record of each drill."],activity:{prompt:"A customer is filling a plastic container that stands in the trunk of his car, and the engine is running. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop the pump, ask him to switch off the engine and put an approved container on the ground; if he refuses, I call the supervisor and do not fuel",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a running engine is an ignition source, and a container inside a vehicle cannot lose its static charge. On the ground, with the nozzle touching it, the charge goes safely to ground."},{text:"I let him finish, because it is only a small amount of fuel",correct:!1,feedback:"The amount does not matter: a static spark or the running engine can ignite the vapor at once."},{text:"I fill it for him while he holds the container steady in the trunk",correct:!1,feedback:"The container is still inside the vehicle, where it cannot lose its static charge, and the engine is still running."},{text:"I ask him to move the car closer to the pump so that he finishes faster",correct:!1,feedback:"The engine would still be running and the container would still be in the trunk; neither danger has been removed."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A car's engine catches fire while it is being filled at a pump, and flames are coming from under the hood. You are the attendant. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I press the emergency stop, raise the alarm and tell the driver to leave the nozzle in the car and get away",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the emergency stop cuts the fuel to all pumps, and people move away before anything else."},{text:"I pull the nozzle out of the car and hang it back on the pump",correct:!1,feedback:"Leave the nozzle where it is. Going to the burning car puts you in danger and can spill fuel."},{text:"I open the hood so that the extinguisher can reach the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Fresh air makes the fire flare up. Keep the hood closed."},{text:"I spray water from the hose onto the car and the forecourt",correct:!1,feedback:"Water spreads burning fuel and carries it into drains."}]},{prompt:"The pumps have stopped and everyone is out of the cars. Other drivers are still turning into the station, and a passenger has a burned hand. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I call 115 and 122, give the station's name and address first, keep everyone well away and stop other vehicles from coming in",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: fire and an injury mean 115 and 122, the address comes first and the forecourt is kept clear of vehicles and people."},{text:"I let the other drivers fill up quickly at the pumps furthest from the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"All pumps are stopped for a reason. Keep every vehicle off the forecourt until the 115 teams say it is safe."},{text:"I drive the burning car away from the pumps myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Never get into a burning vehicle. Keep away and let the 115 teams deal with it."},{text:"I wait to see whether the fire goes out by itself before calling, to avoid a needless call",correct:!1,feedback:"Call at once. A car fire at a fuel station can spread within moments."}]}],realCase:"At a fuel station, a nozzle stuck open while a customer's car was being filled, and fuel ran across the forecourt toward a drain. The attendant pressed the emergency stop at once and asked everyone nearby not to start their engines or smoke. Wearing gloves, a trained colleague covered the spill with absorbent from the spill kit to stop it spreading. Some fuel had already reached the drain, so the supervisor called 115 and gave the station's name and address. The soaked material was collected in a closed metal container, and the pump stayed out of use until it was repaired."},{id:26,code:"IND-126",icon:"Package",accent:"#0f766e",title:"Warehouse and storage fire safety",subtitle:"Stacking, clearances, sprinklers, battery charging and security",summary:"In a warehouse a fire can climb a stack of goods to the roof in minutes. This module shows staff, supervisors and managers how to stack and store goods safely, keep sprinklers working, charge forklift batteries, handle lithium-ion batteries and hazardous goods, and protect the building at night.",blocks:[{body:"In stacked goods and high racking, flames spread quickly upward to the top of the stack and then sideways to every level; in tests with boxes on 10 m high racking, fires often reached the top within two minutes (HM Government fire safety guide for factories and warehouses). The fire load of a warehouse depends on what is stored and is often higher than in homes or offices. The rules below are general good practice; every building must first meet the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority."},{heading:"1. Stacking and clearances",body:"Stored goods must be stable and must not block people, sprinklers or firefighters:",points:["Stack goods so they are stable: blocked, interlocked and limited in height so they cannot slide or collapse.","Do not overstock: every storage location has a written maximum.","Leave at least 1 meter of clear space between the top of the stock and the roof, roof beams or light fittings (UK HSE guidance).","Under sprinkler heads, keep the clearance set by the sprinkler design; OSHA's general minimum is 45 centimeters (18 inches), and some sprinkler types need more.","Racking and stock must never shield the sprinkler heads.","Do not pile combustible goods against electrical equipment, light fittings or heaters, even when they are switched off.","Keep empty pallets, packaging and waste in a designated area away from the building, exits and windows, or in a sprinklered area."]},{heading:"2. Aisles, exits and fire doors",body:"Clear routes let people get out and let the 115 teams reach the fire:",points:["Keep aisles and passageways clear and in good repair; permanent aisles are marked on the floor.","Never store goods in escape routes or in front of exits, fire doors, fire shutters, extinguishers or alarm call points.","Leave a clear gap between stored goods and the inside walls so firefighters can reach a fire (UK guidance: 0.5 m).","Fire exits must open from the inside without a key whenever anyone is in the building.","Only trained and authorized operators drive forklifts, and pedestrians use the marked walkways."]},{heading:"3. Sprinklers: keep them working",body:"Sprinklers control a fire automatically, even at night when no one is there, but only if nothing stops them:",points:["Never hang anything from sprinkler heads or pipes, and never paint, cover or knock them; report a damaged or leaking head at once.","Sprinkler control valves stay fully open. Only an authorized person may close one, under the site's procedure for shutting down fire protection, with extra fire watch until it is reopened.","If you find a valve closed or a head blocked, tell the supervisor at once: the area may be without protection.","Sprinklers open only where the heat of the fire reaches them, so a fire under a shielded head can grow unseen.","Inspection, testing and maintenance follow NFPA 25 and the requirements of Civil Defense; keep the records.","Some products must not be stored in sprinklered areas at all; the safety data sheet (SDS) says so."]},{heading:"4. Forklift battery charging",body:"Charging lead-acid batteries gives off hydrogen, a highly flammable gas; if it collects and meets a spark or flame, it can explode:",points:["Charge only in the designated charging area: well ventilated (with ventilation high above the batteries), clear of stored goods and ignition sources.","No smoking, no flames, no hot work and no phones in the charging area.","Switch the charger off before connecting or disconnecting the battery, to avoid sparks.","Keep tools and other metal objects off the top of batteries, and take off watches, rings and other metal items before working on them.","Open the battery cover while charging, and wear the protective equipment set for the task (gloves, apron and eye protection against acid).","Change the LPG cylinders of gas-powered forklifts outside, away from ignition sources.","Only trained staff change or charge batteries."]},{heading:"5. Lithium-ion batteries",body:"Damaged, overheated or wrongly charged lithium-ion batteries can catch fire or explode, and heat from one failing cell can spread to the next (thermal runaway):",points:["Follow the manufacturer's instructions for storage, use and charging, and use only the charger made for that battery.","Store batteries and devices in dry, cool places away from heaters and other heat sources, and take them off charge when they are full.","Look for warning signs: bulging or cracking, hissing, leaking, rising temperature or smoke. A battery with any of these signs is taken out of use at once.","If it is safe to handle, move it to the designated place away from flammable materials, for example a fire-resistant metal container with sand, and send it for disposal by the approved route.","If a battery is smoking or burning, do not pick it up: warn others, raise the alarm, get out and call 115. Lithium-ion cells contain no lithium metal and are cooled with plenty of water; a fire in stored batteries is for the 115 teams.","Battery fires can flare up again after they seem to be out; tell the 115 teams where batteries are stored."],critical:!0},{heading:"6. Housekeeping and hazardous goods",body:"Less waste means less fuel for a fire, and correct storage keeps a small incident small:",points:["Keep storage areas free of loose packaging and rubbish; put waste in suitable containers and remove it regularly.","No smoking in storage areas.","Read the label and the safety data sheet (SDS) of every hazardous product before storing it; section 7 of the SDS gives the storage conditions and incompatible materials.","Keep incompatible substances apart: stored together, they can make a fire grow faster or release toxic smoke.","Do not put damaged or leaking containers into storage; place them in a tray or bund and follow the supplier's instructions.","Keep an up-to-date record of which hazardous goods are stored where, kept where it can be handed to the 115 teams in an emergency."]},{heading:"7. Night security and the alarm",body:"A fire that starts when the building is closed can grow unseen, and empty buildings are a target for arson:",points:["Keep the outside well lit and the perimeter secure; secure doors, windows and roof openings without blocking the escape routes.","Do not place large waste containers against the building; keep waste bins in a locked compound away from it.","Lock away flammable liquids and gases so intruders cannot use them.","The fire alarm and the intruder alarm are monitored, and someone acts on every alarm at once.","Guards know how to raise the alarm, how to call 115 with the exact address and how to meet the 115 teams at the gate with the keys and the site plan.","Report people acting suspiciously and any small unexplained fires; they can be a warning of arson.","Review the fire risk assessment at least once a year and after every major change in what you store or how you store it."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_warehouse_fire_safety.webp",title:"An orderly, fire-safe warehouse",description:"Clear aisles, clear space under the sprinklers and a separate charging area"}],quiz:[{question:"Under UK HSE guidance, how much clear space should be left between the top of stored goods and the roof, beams or light fittings?",options:[{text:"At least 1 meter",correct:!0},{text:"At least 10 centimeters",correct:!1},{text:"At least 25 centimeters",correct:!1},{text:"None, if the stack is stable",correct:!1}],explanation:"HSE warehousing guidance asks for at least 1 m between stock tops and the roof, roof beams or light fittings; below sprinklers, the clearance set by the sprinkler design applies (OSHA's general minimum is 45 cm)."},{question:"Which practice is correct when charging forklift batteries?",options:[{text:"Charge between the racks so the truck is ready for the next shift",correct:!1},{text:"Disconnect the battery while the charger is still switched on",correct:!1},{text:"Use the ventilated charging area, with no smoking or flames",correct:!0},{text:"Rest metal tools on the battery top so they are close at hand",correct:!1}],explanation:"Charging gives off hydrogen. A designated, ventilated area without ignition sources keeps it from collecting and igniting; switching the charger off before disconnecting and keeping metal tools off the battery prevent sparks."},{question:"During a stock check you find a sprinkler control valve closed. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Report it to the supervisor at once so it is reopened safely",correct:!0},{text:"Leave it closed, since the fire alarm will warn of any fire",correct:!1},{text:"Open it halfway so the water pressure in the pipes stays low",correct:!1},{text:"Hang a note on it and check it again at next month's inspection",correct:!1}],explanation:"A closed control valve can leave a whole area without sprinkler protection. It must be reported at once and reopened by an authorized person under the site's procedure."},{question:"A lithium-ion battery in storage is swollen and getting hot. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Take it out of use; if it smokes, raise the alarm and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Put it back on charge so that the cells can balance and cool down",correct:!1},{text:"Wrap it in cardboard and leave it on the shelf for collection",correct:!1},{text:"Spray it once with CO₂ and return it to the shelf",correct:!1}],explanation:"Swelling and heat are warning signs of battery failure. Take the battery out of use and, if it is safe to handle, isolate it away from flammable materials; if it smokes or burns, do not touch it: raise the alarm, get out and call 115."},{question:"Which measure best protects a closed warehouse against arson?",options:[{text:"Large waste containers placed against the wall, where guards can see them",correct:!1},{text:"The alarm switched off at night to avoid false alarms",correct:!1},{text:"Waste kept away from the building and a monitored alarm",correct:!0},{text:"Flammable liquids left near the door, ready for the morning delivery",correct:!1}],explanation:"Arsonists often use waste and flammable liquids found on site. Keeping waste containers away from the building, locking away flammables and having alarms that are monitored and acted on reduce the risk (HM Government guide)."},{question:"A forklift battery has finished charging and must be disconnected. What is the safe way?",options:[{text:"Switch the charger off first, then disconnect the battery",correct:!0},{text:"Disconnect the battery first, then switch the charger off",correct:!1},{text:"Pull the cable while the charger runs, to keep the full charge",correct:!1},{text:"Close the battery cover first, then pull the cable out quickly",correct:!1}],explanation:"Charging gives off hydrogen. Switching the charger off before disconnecting avoids a spark where the gas may have collected; the battery cover stays open while charging."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm and call 115",detail:"Press the nearest alarm call point and call 115 with the exact address of the site and what is burning.",critical:!0},{title:"Get everyone out by the marked aisles",detail:"Forklift drivers stop and leave their trucks where they do not block aisles or exits. Everyone goes to the assembly point, and nobody stays behind to save goods.",critical:!0},{title:"Do not fight a fire in the stacks",detail:"Flames climb a stack to the roof very quickly. Use an extinguisher only on a small fire that has just started, after the go/no-go check; otherwise get out and let the sprinklers and the 115 teams deal with it.",critical:!0},{title:"Battery smoke: hands off",detail:"If a battery or the charging area is smoking or burning, do not pick anything up: warn others, get out and tell 115 that batteries are involved.",critical:!0},{title:"Meet the 115 teams at the gate",detail:"Count everyone, then give the 115 teams the keys, the site plan and the record of hazardous goods, and tell them where batteries are stored."}],warnings:["Never stack goods so that they shield the sprinkler heads or fill the space under them: a fire under a shielded head can grow unseen.","Never close a sprinkler control valve unless you are authorized under the site's procedure: the area is left without protection.","Never pick up a smoking or burning lithium-ion battery: it can flare up again and give off toxic smoke."],tips:["At the end of each shift, walk the aisles and check that exits, fire doors, extinguishers and alarm call points are clear.","Keep the record of hazardous goods and the site plan at the gate or the guards' office, ready to hand to the 115 teams.","Mark on the floor the areas that must stay clear in front of exits and fire equipment, so that everyone can see them."],activity:{prompt:"A large delivery arrives and the racking is full. A supervisor suggests putting the extra pallets in the main aisle and on top of the highest rack, close under the sprinklers, until next week. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I refuse and find another storage area or delay part of the delivery, keeping the aisles and the space under the sprinklers clear",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: aisles are escape and access routes, every storage location has a written maximum and the space under the sprinklers must stay clear."},{text:"I agree, because the pallets will only be there for a few days",correct:!1,feedback:"A fire does not wait for next week. Blocked aisles and shielded sprinklers are dangerous from the first hour."},{text:"I put them in the main aisle but leave a narrow gap for people to walk through",correct:!1,feedback:"Aisles must stay clear for escape and for the 115 teams, and overstocking breaks the written maximum."},{text:"I put them in front of the back fire exit, which is hardly ever used",correct:!1,feedback:"Never store goods in front of exits. An exit that is rarely used may be the only way out in a fire."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During the night shift, a guard sees smoke coming from the battery charging area, where three forklifts are on charge. What does the guard do first?",options:[{text:"He raises the alarm and calls 115 with the exact address, saying that the smoke is in the battery charging area",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm and the call come first, and 115 needs to know that batteries and hydrogen may be involved."},{text:"He goes into the smoke to unplug the chargers one by one",correct:!1,feedback:"Nobody goes into smoke. Charging gives off hydrogen, and disconnecting while current flows can make sparks."},{text:"He drives the forklifts out of the charging area to save them",correct:!1,feedback:"Getting onto a forklift whose battery may be burning puts him in great danger. Goods and machines can be replaced."},{text:"He opens all the doors to let the smoke out before calling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Fresh air feeds the fire, and every minute without a call lets it grow. Raise the alarm and call 115 first."}]},{prompt:"The 115 teams are on their way. The alarm is sounding, and two workers from the loading area have joined the guard at the gate. What does the guard do now?",options:[{text:"He checks that everyone on site is counted, opens the gate and gives the 115 teams the keys, the site plan and where batteries and hazardous goods are stored",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the count, the keys and the site information let the 115 teams start at once and safely."},{text:"He goes back to the charging area with an extinguisher to fight the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"A fire in a charging area with several batteries is for the 115 teams. He stays at the gate to guide them."},{text:"He sends the two workers home so that they are not in the way",correct:!1,feedback:"Everyone stays at the assembly point to be counted and to answer the 115 teams' questions."},{text:"He locks the gate so that nobody can enter the site",correct:!1,feedback:"The 115 teams need to get in at once. A locked gate without a key holder costs time."}]}],realCase:"During a stock check in a warehouse, a worker noticed that pallets had been stacked so high that they shielded two sprinkler heads, and that a sprinkler control valve nearby had been left half closed after a repair. She reported both to the supervisor at once. The pallets were moved to another storage area, and an authorized person reopened the valve under the site's procedure and checked the system. The manager added both points to the regular walk-round. If a fire starts in that area now, the sprinklers can do their job."},{id:27,code:"IND-127",icon:"HardHat",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Construction site safety",subtitle:"Fire plan, hot work, temporary electrics, cylinders, excavations and UXO",summary:"Buildings under construction often have no working sprinklers or alarms yet, but they are full of ignition sources and combustible materials. This module shows workers, supervisors and managers how to plan for fire; control hot work, temporary electrics, fuel and gas cylinders; recognize danger at scaffolds and excavations; give first aid; and act when a suspicious object is found while digging.",blocks:[{body:"According to NFPA, the leading causes of fires in buildings under construction are cooking equipment, electrical distribution and lighting equipment, and heating equipment. Sprinklers, smoke detection and fire alarms may not be working yet, and open sites attract trespassers and arson. Site rules, permits and licenses are set by Civil Defense and the licensing authority: follow their requirements. This module gives the basic rules for everyone on site."},{heading:"1. Site fire plan and assembly point",body:"Before work starts, the main contractor in control of the site prepares the fire plan:",points:["A fire risk assessment and a written emergency plan, ready before work starts and updated as the building grows.","One named person manages fire prevention on site (in NFPA 241: the fire prevention program manager).","Escape routes from every work area, kept clear and signed and never locked while people are on site; wherever practicable, two routes in different directions.","A fire alarm that everyone can hear above the site noise, and fire instruction notices at fire points, site entrances and canteens.","Fire points with suitable extinguishers, and nominated people trained to use them.","An assembly point where everyone is counted, by a head count or a sweep of the area.","Fire drills to test the plan and the training; every new worker and visitor learns the plan at induction."]},{heading:"2. Hot work: permit and fire watch",body:"Welding, cutting, grinding and torch work start many site fires. Use another method if it can do the job; if not:",points:["Work only with a hot work permit issued by the person authorized to approve it.","Clear combustible materials within 11 meters (35 feet) of the work, protect them with fire-resistant blankets or curtains, or move the work to a safe area.","Keep a suitable fire extinguisher ready at the work point.","A fire watch stays during the work and during breaks, and for at least 1 hour after the work ends; the permit authorizing individual may order a longer watch (NFPA 51B).","Plan hot work to finish well before the end of the working day, so the checks can be done before the site closes (UK HSE: 2 hours before)."]},{heading:"3. Temporary electrics",body:"Temporary site wiring causes fires and electric shocks when it is overloaded, damaged or altered by unqualified people:",points:["Temporary installations are designed, installed, changed and checked only by a qualified electrician; no one else adds to them or alters them.","Sockets used by workers are protected by a residual current device (RCD), which cuts the power quickly when current leaks to ground; press its test button regularly. An RCD reduces the risk but does not replace a safe installation.","Do not overload sockets or multi-way adapters, do not plug extension leads into each other, and unwind cable reels fully before use.","Use suitable flexible cable for extension leads, supported off the ground; take damaged leads and tools out of use instead of taping them.","Do not run temporary cables or distribution boards through escape routes, and keep rubbish away from distribution boards."]},{heading:"4. Fuel and gas cylinders on site",body:"Fuel, LPG, acetylene and oxygen bring a lot of stored energy onto the site:",points:["Keep only the minimum quantity on site, in a secure, well-ventilated store away from combustible materials and work areas, never inside the building under construction.","Use only approved containers for fuel, and never store fuel in exits, stairways or passageways.","Refuel machines in a designated place, with engines off, no smoking and a fire extinguisher nearby.","Keep gas cylinders upright and secured against falling, with the valve caps on when they are not in use, and away from sparks, hot slag and flames.","Store oxygen cylinders apart from fuel-gas cylinders: at least 6 meters (20 feet) apart, or behind a fire-resistant barrier (OSHA).","Lock fuel and cylinders away at the end of the day so intruders cannot use them.","If a cylinder leaks or burns: close the valve only if it can be reached safely; never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped; get everyone away and call 115."]},{heading:"5. Scaffolds and excavations: recognize danger and stop",body:"Falls and trench collapses kill construction workers. Designing scaffolds and trench supports is work for competent specialists; everyone on site must recognize danger and stop:",points:["Use a scaffold only after a competent person has inspected it before the shift; never use one that is damaged, incomplete or missing guardrails, and never alter it yourself.","Work at 1.8 meters (6 feet) or more above a lower level, or near an open edge or hole, needs fall protection: guardrails, covers or a harness attached to a secure anchor.","Never enter an unprotected trench. Under OSHA, excavations 1.5 meters (5 feet) deep or more need a protective system designed for the site, unless they are cut in stable rock.","Keep soil heaps, materials and machines at least 0.6 meters (2 feet) back from the edge.","A competent person inspects excavations every day and after rain.","At signs of collapse (cracks in the ground, bulging walls, water seeping in, falling soil): get out at once, keep others out and tell the supervisor; work restarts only when the danger is controlled."]},{heading:"6. Access for 115 and first aid",body:"In a fire or an injury, every minute counts:",points:["Keep the site entrance, gates and access roads clear for 115 fire engines and ambulances; do not park or store materials on them.","Keep fire hydrants, water points and firefighting equipment visible and easy to reach.","Large sites contact Civil Defense before work starts and whenever access or hazards change, and keep an information pack (site plan, hazards, stored fuel, gas and batteries) ready for the 115 teams.","A person trained in first aid and a stocked first aid kit are available on site.","Post the site address, directions and the numbers 115 and 122 by the phones, in the site office and at the entrance.","After a fall from height, do not move the injured person unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed; call 122."]},{heading:"7. Suspicious object found while digging",body:"Old explosive ordnance can be found while digging, even in places thought to be safe, and it can explode if it is moved or hit. If a machine, a worker or a tool uncovers a suspicious object:",points:["Stop the machine and the work at once.","Never touch, move, lift, dig around or pull out the object, and never put it in a bucket or a vehicle.","Clear everyone from the area and keep people and machines out.","Move well away (at least 50 meters, UNMAS) before using a phone or radio, then call 115 and follow the instructions of Civil Defense.","From a safe place, note where the object is and tell the 115 teams; work restarts only when the authorities say the site is safe."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_construction_site_fire_safety.webp",title:"A fire-ready construction site",description:"Hot work permit with a fire watch, secured cylinders and a clear access road"}],quiz:[{question:"Under NFPA 51B, how long must the fire watch stay after hot work ends?",options:[{text:"At least 1 hour, or longer if the permit requires it",correct:!0},{text:"Until the welder has packed up the tools and left the site",correct:!1},{text:"About 10 minutes, if no smoke can be seen by then",correct:!1},{text:"No watch is needed when an extinguisher is close by",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sparks can smolder in hidden places and start a fire later. NFPA 51B requires the fire watch for at least 1 hour after the work, and the permit authorizing individual can order a longer watch."},{question:"An extension lead on site has damaged insulation. What should be done?",options:[{text:"Take it out of use and have it replaced by a qualified person",correct:!0},{text:"Wrap the damaged part with insulating tape and carry on working",correct:!1},{text:"Plug it into a second lead so each lead carries less current",correct:!1},{text:"Use it only for small hand tools until the job is finished",correct:!1}],explanation:"Damaged leads cause shocks and fires. They are taken out of use and replaced or properly repaired by a competent person, never taped (UK HSE); an RCD reduces the risk but does not make a damaged lead safe."},{question:"Cracks appear in the ground and soil starts falling into a trench where a colleague is working. What is correct?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, keep others out and tell the supervisor",correct:!0},{text:"Finish the section quickly before the trench wall moves",correct:!1},{text:"Climb in and hold the wall back with spare timber boards",correct:!1},{text:"Park the excavator at the edge so its weight holds the soil",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cracks and falling soil are signs of collapse. Workers leave at once and stay out until a competent person has made the trench safe; a heavy machine at the edge makes a collapse more likely."},{question:"An excavator uncovers a rusty object that looks like a shell. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Lift it gently with the bucket and set it to one side",correct:!1},{text:"Brush the soil off it to check whether it is real",correct:!1},{text:"Stop work, clear the area and call 115 from far away",correct:!0},{text:"Carry it to the site office until the police arrive",correct:!1}],explanation:"Explosive ordnance can explode when it is moved or hit. Stop at once, clear everyone away, never move it and call 115 from well away, avoiding phone or radio use close to the object (UNMAS)."},{question:"Which arrangement is correct for gas cylinders on a construction site?",options:[{text:"Laid on their side so they cannot fall over",correct:!1},{text:"In the stairway, because it is the coolest place on site",correct:!1},{text:"Oxygen and acetylene chained together in a single store",correct:!1},{text:"Upright and secured, caps on, away from sparks and heat",correct:!0}],explanation:"Cylinders are kept upright and secured with valve caps on, away from sparks and heat, and oxygen is kept apart from fuel gas (OSHA 1926.350). Stairways are escape routes and must stay free of fuel and cylinders."},{question:"Welding is still going on, but the fire watch has left for lunch. What should happen?",options:[{text:"The hot work stops until the fire watch is back in place",correct:!0},{text:"The work goes on, since an extinguisher is ready at the work point",correct:!1},{text:"The welder finishes quickly, before the fire watch comes back",correct:!1},{text:"The welder watches for sparks himself while he keeps working",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under NFPA 51B the fire watch stays during the work and during breaks. The welder cannot watch for sparks while working, and an extinguisher helps only if someone is there to see the fire."}],steps:[{title:"Permit before you start",detail:"Use another method if it can do the job. If not, work only with a hot work permit issued by the person authorized to approve it.",critical:!0},{title:"Clear or protect combustibles",detail:"Remove combustible materials from around the work as the permit says, cover what cannot be moved with fire-resistant blankets or curtains, or move the work to a safe area.",critical:!0},{title:"Extinguisher and fire watch in place",detail:"Keep a suitable extinguisher ready at the work point. The fire watch stays during the work and during breaks."},{title:"Keep watching after the work ends",detail:"The fire watch stays for at least 1 hour after the work ends, or longer if the permit says so (NFPA 51B), and raises the alarm at the first sign of smoke.",critical:!0},{title:"Finish early and check before the site closes",detail:"Plan hot work to finish well before the end of the working day, so that the area can be checked before everyone leaves."}],warnings:["Never touch, move or dig around a suspicious object found while digging: it can explode if it is moved or hit.","Never enter an unprotected trench or one with signs of collapse: the walls can fall in without warning.","Never store fuel or gas cylinders inside the building under construction or in exits and stairways: a fire there would cut off the way out."],tips:["Brief every new worker and visitor on the fire plan, the alarm and the assembly point at induction.","At the end of each day, check that hot work areas have been inspected, fuel and cylinders are locked away and access roads are clear.","Keep the site information pack at the gate, ready for the 115 teams."],activity:{prompt:"At the end of the day, a welder wants to cut a steel beam quickly. There is no permit, cardboard packaging is lying nearby and nobody is free to act as the fire watch. You are the supervisor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop the job until there is a permit, the cardboard is cleared, an extinguisher is ready and a fire watch is named, and plan it for earlier in a working day",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: hot work starts only with a permit, cleared or protected combustibles, an extinguisher and a fire watch, and it finishes well before the site closes."},{text:"I allow it, because the cut will take only a few minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"A spark needs only a moment, and fires from hot work can start after the work has ended."},{text:"I allow it if the welder keeps a bucket of water next to him",correct:!1,feedback:"A bucket of water does not replace a permit, cleared combustibles and a fire watch."},{text:"I allow it and ask the first worker in the morning to check the area",correct:!1,feedback:"By morning a hidden fire may have grown for hours on an empty site. The area is watched after the work and checked before the site closes."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"An excavator digging a foundation uncovers a rusty, pointed metal object. Workers gather round to look. You are the site supervisor. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I stop the machine and all work, clear everyone from the area and keep people and machines out",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an old explosive item can go off if it is moved or hit. Stop, move everyone away and keep them away."},{text:"I have a worker lift it into the bucket and carry it to the edge of the site",correct:!1,feedback:"Never move or lift a suspicious object; moving it can make it explode."},{text:"I brush the soil off it to see whether it really is old ordnance",correct:!1,feedback:"Never dig or brush around it. Identifying it is work for the specialists who come through 115."},{text:"I let work continue in another part of the trench while I decide",correct:!1,feedback:"All work stops: machines and digging nearby can hit or move the object. Work restarts only when the authorities say the site is safe."}]},{prompt:"Everyone has moved back from the object. A worker says he will take a close-up photo with his phone and call 115 from where he is standing, next to the trench. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell him not to go closer, move everyone well away before anyone uses a phone or radio, then call 115 and follow the instructions of Civil Defense",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: phones and radios are used only after moving well away, and the 115 teams are told from a safe place where the object is."},{text:"I let him take the photo, because it will help the 115 teams",correct:!1,feedback:"Going closer and using a phone next to the object are both dangerous. Describe its location from a safe place instead."},{text:"I cover the object with a sheet of plywood so that nobody steps on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Covering it means going right up to it and touching it. Keep everyone away and leave it exactly as it is."},{text:"I restart the excavator in another part of the site once the object is out of sight",correct:!1,feedback:"Work restarts only when the authorities say the site is safe. Other objects may be buried nearby."}]}],realCase:"On a building site, a worker was welding a bracket on an upper floor in the afternoon. The supervisor had issued a hot work permit, the cardboard and timber offcuts nearby had been cleared and a colleague had been named as the fire watch. After the welding ended, the colleague stayed to watch the area, as the permit required. Some time later he saw smoke rising from a gap in the floor, where a spark had fallen onto insulation below. He raised the alarm, the supervisor called 115 and the small fire was put out before it could spread. Without the fire watch, it could have grown after the site had closed."},{id:28,code:"BUS-128",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#b91c1c",title:"Fire blankets, wheeled and vehicle extinguishers",subtitle:"When and how to use them safely, and why lithium-ion battery fires need special care",summary:"Besides hand-held extinguishers, workplaces use fire blankets, large wheeled extinguishers and extinguishers in vehicles. Each has its limits. This lesson explains when and how to use them safely, and what to do when a lithium-ion battery overheats or catches fire.",blocks:[{body:`Every piece of fire equipment has a job and limits. The same go/no-go check applies to all of them: the fire is small and just starting, the equipment is right for it, the exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, and you are trained. If any answer is no, get out. Which extinguisher standard is enforced in Duhok (NFPA 10 or EN 3) and who may service extinguishers are not yet confirmed; follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority.

Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.`},{heading:"1. Fire blanket: a small pan fire",body:"A fire blanket is a sheet of fire-resistant fabric in a quick-release pack. It smothers a small fire in a pan or pot by cutting off the air:",points:["Keep it near the kitchen door, not next to the stove, so that you can reach it and still get out.","Pull the tabs down sharply to release the blanket.","Hold it in front of you with the edges rolled over your hands, to protect your hands and face.","Place it gently over the pan; do not throw it. Make sure it covers the whole pan.","Turn off the heat only if you can reach the control safely.","Leave the blanket in place and do not move the pan. Do not lift the blanket to look until everything has cooled completely.","Call 115 even if the fire seems to be out; if it does not go out at once, get out and close the door. Never put water on burning oil."]},{heading:"2. Fire blanket: clothes on fire",body:"If a person's clothes catch fire:",points:["The person stops, drops to the ground, covers their face with their hands and rolls until the flames are out.","If you have a fire blanket, wrap it around the flames and the person, and help them drop to the ground and roll.","Protect your own hands with the edges of the blanket.","Then cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes, do not burst blisters and call 122.","A fire blanket is used once and then replaced. Buy one whose label shows the standard it meets, for example EN 1869; a blanket with no standard mark may not work. Which standard is accepted locally is not yet confirmed."]},{heading:"3. Wheeled extinguishers",body:"Wheeled extinguishers carry far more agent than a hand-held unit, on a cart with a long hose. They protect places such as fuel stores, repair workshops and loading areas:",points:["Only staff trained on that model use it: the controls, the order of the steps and the range differ between models, so follow the label.","Work in pairs where you can: one person controls the cart and the valve as the label shows, the other handles the hose and nozzle. Some models are made for one trained person.","Take the unit to a position within the range shown on the label, with your exit behind you, and follow the label's steps in order.","The go/no-go check is the same: a wheeled unit does not make a large fire safe to fight.","After any use, it is recharged by a qualified technician."]},{heading:"4. Vehicle extinguishers",body:"Trucks, forklifts, buses and company cars often carry a small extinguisher, but most vehicle fires are not for the driver to fight:",points:["Pull over as soon as it is safe, switch off the engine, get everyone out and move well away from the vehicle and the traffic, at least 30 meters (100 feet).","Call 115 (and 122 if anyone is hurt) and warn other traffic; never go back for belongings.","Never open the hood or the trunk if you think the fire is inside: fresh air makes it flare up.","Use the vehicle extinguisher only on a small fire that has just started, only if you are trained, everyone is out and your way back is clear. For a fire in the engine compartment, keep the hood closed; some fire services advise aiming through the grille or the gap at the edge of the hood.","If in doubt, do not tackle the fire. Never put water on an engine fire.","Forklifts and other battery-powered vehicles: if the battery is involved, treat it as a battery fire (section 5)."]},{heading:"5. Lithium-ion battery fires",body:"Lithium-ion batteries are in phones, laptops, power tools, e-bikes, forklifts and battery storage systems. When damaged, overcharged or faulty, they can overheat, give off toxic gas, catch fire or explode:",points:["Warning signs: a strange smell, heat, swelling or a change in shape or color, popping noises or smoke. Stop using or charging it at once.","If it is safe, unplug it and move it away from anything that can burn.","If it is burning: get everyone out, close the door and call 115 from outside; say that it is a battery fire.","Do not breathe the smoke: a damaged battery can give off toxic gas that you may not see.","Cooling with large amounts of water is the recommended method, but only trained people with the right equipment do it; ordinary ABC powder and CO₂ do not fully stop the fire.","Lithium-ion batteries are not Class D: Class D agents are only for lithium-metal batteries and burning metals.","Prevention: use only the maker's charger or one the maker approves, charge on a hard surface away from things that burn and away from exits, and take damaged batteries out of use."],critical:!0},{heading:"6. Checks and service",body:"Keep all this equipment ready:",points:["In the regular visual check, look at fire blankets too: in place, easy to reach, pack undamaged and tabs hanging free.","Wheeled and vehicle extinguishers get the same visual check as other extinguishers: in place, pin and seal intact, gauge in the green if there is one, no damage.","Recharge after any use; yearly maintenance and pressure tests are done by a qualified technician. The local service rules and who may service extinguishers are not yet confirmed.","Replace a used fire blanket and report any used extinguisher at once."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_fire_blanket_pan.webp",title:"Using a fire blanket on a small pan fire",description:"A worker in a staff kitchen holds a fire blanket in front of her, hands protected behind the fabric, and lays it gently over a burning pan; the door behind her is open."}],quiz:[{question:"A small pan of oil catches fire on the stove. You have a fire blanket. What is the right method?",options:[{text:"Protect your hands with the edges, lay it gently over the pan and leave it there",correct:!0},{text:"Throw the blanket onto the pan from across the kitchen so that you keep your distance",correct:!1},{text:"Cover the pan, then lift the blanket after a minute to check the fire is out",correct:!1},{text:"Pour a glass of water on the pan first, then cover it with the blanket",correct:!1}],explanation:"Place the blanket gently over the pan with your hands protected behind it, turn off the heat if you can do so safely, leave it until everything has cooled and call 115. Never water on burning oil."},{question:"A coworker's clothes catch fire. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Get them to stop, drop and roll, wrapping a fire blanket around the flames if there is one",correct:!0},{text:"Tell them to run to the nearest tap so that the running water can put the flames out",correct:!1},{text:"Spray them with a CO₂ extinguisher aimed straight at their face and chest until the flames stop",correct:!1},{text:"Pull off their burning clothes with your bare hands as quickly as you possibly can",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stop, drop and roll smothers the flames, and a fire blanket wrapped around them helps; protect your own hands with its edges. Then cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122."},{question:"Who should use a wheeled extinguisher?",options:[{text:"Staff trained on that model, following its label, ideally working in pairs",correct:!0},{text:"Any employee who happens to be nearest to the fire when the alarm sounds",correct:!1},{text:"Only the security guard, because wheeled units are kept near the main gate",correct:!1},{text:"Anyone who has used a small hand-held extinguisher at home at least once",correct:!1}],explanation:"Wheeled units differ from model to model. Only staff trained on that model use them, following the label, ideally in pairs, and only after the same go/no-go check."},{question:"Smoke is coming from under the hood of a company truck. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Stop, switch off, get everyone out and away, call 115 and keep the hood closed",correct:!0},{text:"Open the hood fully so that you can aim the extinguisher straight at the engine",correct:!1},{text:"Keep driving to the nearest workshop so that a mechanic can look at it",correct:!1},{text:"Pour water from a bottle onto the engine through the gap under the hood",correct:!1}],explanation:"Opening the hood lets in fresh air and the fire flares up. Get everyone out and at least 30 meters (100 feet) away, call 115, and never put water on an engine fire."},{question:"A power-tool battery on a charging shelf is swelling and giving off smoke. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Unplug and move it if that is safe; if it catches fire, get out and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Cover it with Class D powder, because lithium-ion batteries are metal fires",correct:!1},{text:"Put it in a metal cupboard and shut the door tight so that the smoke stays inside",correct:!1},{text:"Keep charging it, because the swelling goes down once it is fully charged",correct:!1}],explanation:"Swelling and smoke are warning signs. If it is safe, unplug it and move it away from anything that can burn; if it catches fire, get everyone out, close the door and call 115. Lithium-ion batteries are not Class D."},{question:"A wheeled extinguisher stands next to the fuel store. A worker who has only used hand-held extinguishers sees a fire there that is already spreading. What is right?",options:[{text:"Leave it, get out and raise the alarm, because the fire is spreading",correct:!0},{text:"Use it, because it holds far more agent than a hand-held unit",correct:!1},{text:"Use it alone, following the same steps as a hand-held unit",correct:!1},{text:"Use it from very close, so that no agent is wasted on the way",correct:!1}],explanation:"A wheeled extinguisher is used only by staff trained on that model, and the go/no-go check is the same: a spreading fire means get out, raise the alarm and call 115. A larger unit does not make a large fire safe to fight."}],steps:[{title:"Go/no-go check first",detail:"Use the blanket only if the fire is small and just starting, it is in a pan or pot, your exit is behind you, others have been warned and you know how to use it. If any answer is no, get out and call 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Release the blanket and protect your hands",detail:"Pull the tabs down sharply to release the blanket. Hold it in front of you with the edges rolled over your hands, to protect your hands and face."},{title:"Lay it gently over the pan",detail:"Do not throw it. Make sure it covers the whole pan, then turn off the heat only if you can reach the control safely.",critical:!0},{title:"Leave it in place until it has cooled",detail:"Do not move the pan and do not lift the blanket to look until everything has cooled completely."},{title:"Call 115, and get out if it does not go out",detail:"Call 115 even if the fire seems to be out. If it does not go out at once, get out and close the door. Replace the blanket after use.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never put water on burning oil: the water turns to steam and throws the burning oil out in a fireball.","Never open the hood or the trunk of a vehicle if you think the fire is inside: fresh air makes it flare up.","Never pick up a smoking or burning lithium-ion battery or breathe its smoke: it can give off toxic gas and flare up again."],tips:["Keep the fire blanket near the kitchen door, not next to the stove, and check in the regular visual check that the tabs hang free.","Before an emergency, train on the wheeled extinguisher model your site has and read its label: the controls and the range differ between models.","Know where the extinguisher in your vehicle is kept, and include it in the regular visual check.","Charge batteries only with the maker's charger or one the maker approves, on a hard surface away from things that burn and away from exits."],activity:{prompt:"In a loading area, a forklift driver sees smoke coming from the battery compartment of his forklift. A wheeled extinguisher stands nearby, but he has never been trained on it. What should he do?",options:[{text:"Stop, switch off, get off and away from the forklift, warn others, raise the alarm and call 115, saying that a battery may be involved",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: if the battery is involved, it is treated as a battery fire. He gets away, warns others and lets the 115 teams deal with it."},{text:"Use the wheeled extinguisher at once on his own, working it out as he goes",correct:!1,feedback:"Only staff trained on that model use a wheeled extinguisher; the controls and the order of the steps differ. A battery fire is for the 115 teams."},{text:"Open the battery cover to see where the smoke is coming from",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening it lets in air and puts his face in the toxic gas a damaged battery can give off."},{text:"Drive the forklift quickly outside so that the smoke does not fill the building",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying on a vehicle that may catch fire puts him in danger, and driving it can block routes or spread the fire."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"The driver of a company truck on a busy road smells burning and sees smoke coming from under the hood. What does the driver do first?",options:[{text:"Pulls over as soon as it is safe, switches off the engine, gets everyone out and moves well away from the truck and the traffic",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stopping, switching off and getting everyone away from the vehicle and the traffic come first."},{text:"Keeps driving to the depot, where there are more extinguishers",correct:!1,feedback:"The fire grows while the truck is moving. Stop as soon as it is safe."},{text:"Stops and opens the hood to see where the smoke is coming from",correct:!1,feedback:"Fresh air makes an engine fire flare up. Keep the hood closed."},{text:"Stops and pours water from a bottle through the grille onto the engine",correct:!1,feedback:"Never put water on an engine fire; burning fuel and oil can spread."}]},{prompt:"Everyone is out and well away, and 115 has been called. Only a little smoke is coming through the grille, the driver is trained and the truck carries an extinguisher. What is right?",options:[{text:"Only if the fire is still small and his way back is clear, he uses it with the hood closed, through the grille or the gap at its edge; if it does not go out at once, he moves away",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the go/no-go check still applies, the hood stays closed and, if in doubt, he does not tackle the fire."},{text:"He goes back to the cab to collect his phone and the delivery papers",correct:!1,feedback:"Never go back to a burning vehicle for belongings."},{text:"He lifts the hood a little so that he can aim the extinguisher better",correct:!1,feedback:"Even a small opening lets in fresh air, and the fire can flare up into his face."},{text:"He waits right next to the truck so that he can tell the firefighters what happened",correct:!1,feedback:"He stays well away from the vehicle and the traffic, and meets the firefighters from a safe place."}]}],realCase:"In a staff kitchen, a worker was heating oil in a pan when it caught fire. She took the fire blanket from beside the door, pulled the tabs down sharply and held the blanket in front of her with the edges rolled over her hands. She laid it gently over the pan, turned off the stove, which she could reach safely, and left the blanket in place. A colleague raised the alarm and called 115. The firefighters checked the kitchen and confirmed that the fire was out. The used blanket was replaced the same day."},{id:29,code:"IND-129",icon:"Lock",accent:"#b45309",title:"Lockout and tagout of hazardous energy (LOTO)",subtitle:"Shut down, isolate, lock, release stored energy and verify before maintenance",summary:"A machine can move unexpectedly during maintenance, cleaning or clearing a jam because someone pressed a button or because stored energy remained in it. This module shows workers, technicians and supervisors how every energy source is cut off and locked, how stored energy is released and how to verify that the machine cannot move before anyone touches it.",blocks:[{body:"Many factory injuries happen during maintenance rather than normal work: a hand is inside a machine and someone who does not know starts it, a raised part falls under its own weight or a pressurized pipe is opened. Lockout/tagout controls these hazards: every energy source is cut off and locked, and a tag shows who applied the lock and why. The factory writes a LOTO procedure for each machine; follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the competent authority."},{heading:"1. Types of hazardous energy",body:"Before work, list every energy source of the machine, not only electricity:",points:["Electrical: panels, plugs, batteries and capacitors that can stay charged after switching off.","Air and oil pressure (pneumatic and hydraulic) in lines, cylinders and accumulators.","Mechanical energy: springs, flywheels still turning and raised parts that fall by gravity.","Heat, steam, chemicals and gases in pipes and vessels."]},{heading:"2. A lock for each person and the tag",body:"A lock is a physical barrier; a tag is only a warning. The basic rules:",points:["Everyone who works on the machine has a personal lock and key; in group work each person puts a lock on a group hasp.","The tag shows the person’s name, the time and the reason for the lockout.","Only the person who applied a lock removes it; another person’s lock is removed only under the factory’s written procedure, after confirming that person is not on site.","A tag alone is not enough when the isolating device can be locked."]},{heading:"3. What is not isolation",body:"Stop buttons, emergency stops, control switches and door interlocks can stop a machine but do not cut its energy: a short in a wire, a software fault or a person can start it again. Real isolation uses an energy-isolating device that is operated by hand and locked: the main electrical disconnect, a line valve or unplugging when the plug stays under the control of the person doing the work. If you do not know which device cuts all the energy, do not work and ask your supervisor."},{heading:"4. Returning the machine to service",body:"After the work the same care is needed: remove tools and leftover parts from the machine, put the machine guards back, make sure everyone is clear of the danger zone, inform the affected workers, and then each person removes their own lock. Only then is energy restored. If work continues across a shift change, the new shift applies its locks before the old shift removes theirs, so the machine is never left without a lock."},{heading:"5. If someone is caught in a machine",body:"Stop the machine with the emergency stop and cut the energy if you can do so safely. Do not put your hand into a machine that is still moving. If a person has had an electric shock, do not touch them until the power is off. Call 115 and 122, and stop severe bleeding with direct pressure as in the first aid lesson. Do not reverse the machine yourself to free the person if you do not know what will happen; follow the instructions of the 115 teams.",critical:!0}],steps:[{title:"1. Prepare and notify",detail:"Read the machine’s written LOTO procedure, identify every energy source and tell other workers the machine will be shut down."},{title:"2. Shut down",detail:"Stop the machine by its normal operating method, not by pulling a cable or cutting power while it is still under load."},{title:"3. Isolate",detail:"Operate every isolating device on the list (main disconnect, valve, plug) so that all energy sources are cut off.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Lock and tag",detail:"Put your own lock and a tag with your name on every isolating device; keep the key with you.",critical:!0},{title:"5. Release stored energy",detail:"Bleed off remaining pressure, block raised parts with suitable supports or lower them and discharge capacitors as the written procedure says.",critical:!0},{title:"6. Verify",detail:"With everyone clear, try to start the machine to confirm it does not run, and a qualified person tests for voltage with a meter; then return the controls to off.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never reach into a machine just because the stop button was pressed: the button does not cut the energy and the machine can move unexpectedly.","Do not remove someone else’s lock and do not lend your key: the lock protects the life of the person who applied it.","Do not skip verification: without a test you do not know whether all energy is off or a forgotten source remains."],tips:["Post a short list of energy sources and isolating device locations on each machine.","Keep LOTO locks a different color and shape from other locks in the factory so they are easy to recognize.","Ask before work: “What happens if someone presses the button now?”","New workers and contractor workers follow the same LOTO procedure."],activity:{prompt:"A conveyor has stopped because a carton is jammed in it. The supervisor says, “Hurry, just hit the emergency stop and pull the carton out.” What do you do?",options:[{text:"I hit the emergency stop and quickly pull the carton out.",correct:!1,feedback:"The emergency stop does not cut the energy; the belt can move unexpectedly and catch a hand."},{text:"I isolate the energy under the LOTO procedure, apply my lock and tag, test that the belt will not run and then remove the carton.",correct:!0,feedback:"Clearing a jam is servicing work and needs LOTO even when it is short."},{text:"I leave another worker by the button so no one presses it while I work.",correct:!1,feedback:"A person can make a mistake or walk away; this does not replace your lock or remove stored energy."},{text:"I push the carton out with a long stick so my hand does not go in.",correct:!1,feedback:"The stick can be caught and thrown by the machine; the machine is still energized and can move."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A team of three is going to repair a hydraulic pump. The electrical disconnect is off with one lock on it, the team leader’s. You are on the team. What do you do before starting?",options:[{text:"I put my own lock on the group hasp and ask whether the hydraulic pressure has been released.",correct:!0,feedback:"Each person applies a lock, and hydraulic pressure is stored energy that cutting the power does not release."},{text:"The team leader’s lock is enough because he is in charge.",correct:!1,feedback:"The leader could remove his lock while you are still working; only your own lock protects you."},{text:"I open the hydraulic line right away to look at the pump.",correct:!1,feedback:"Remaining pressure can spray oil with force and cause serious injury."}]},{prompt:"The work is finished. The team leader has gone home and his lock is still on the hasp. The manager wants the machine running at once. What should happen?",options:[{text:"We cut the lock with bolt cutters and start the machine.",correct:!1,feedback:"Removing another person’s lock without the written procedure can injure him or someone else still at the machine."},{text:"The factory’s written procedure: the supervisor confirms he is not on site, contacts him and removes the lock after checking the machine.",correct:!0,feedback:"This is correct: confirming no one is at risk and informing the lock owner before removal."},{text:"We remove the tag, leave the lock and power the machine through another switch.",correct:!1,feedback:"Bypassing the lock through another source defeats LOTO completely and carries the same danger."}]}],realCase:"Training scenario: in a packaging factory, a technician on the night shift reached into a wrapping machine to remove a jammed part. He had only pressed the stop button. Another worker, who did not see anyone inside, saw the machine was stopped and started it with the start button. The technician survived with a serious hand injury. Afterward the factory wrote a LOTO procedure for every machine, issued a personal lock to each worker and made verification training a requirement before any maintenance work.",quiz:[{question:"You are about to change a belt on a machine. The disconnect is off and locked. What is the next step before touching it?",options:[{text:"Release stored energy and test that the machine will not start",correct:!0},{text:"Start work because the lock is on the main disconnect",correct:!1},{text:"Add a tag with your name on the start button",correct:!1},{text:"Ask another worker to stay by the panel",correct:!1}],explanation:"Springs, gravity, pressure or capacitors can move the machine after isolation; the test proves isolation worked."},{question:"Which of these is an energy-isolating device used for LOTO?",options:[{text:"The emergency stop on the control panel",correct:!1},{text:"The main disconnect, opened and locked by hand",correct:!0},{text:"The machine door interlock",correct:!1},{text:"The operating mode selector on the screen",correct:!1}],explanation:"Buttons, control switches and interlocks do not cut the energy and can restart the machine by mistake."},{question:"In group work four workers are working on one machine. How many locks are needed?",options:[{text:"One lock, the supervisor’s, because he is responsible for everyone",correct:!1},{text:"Each worker puts a personal lock on the group hasp",correct:!0},{text:"Two locks: one for the morning shift and one for the evening shift",correct:!1},{text:"No locks, just one large tag with everyone’s name",correct:!1}],explanation:"Each person is protected only by their own lock; the machine cannot be energized until the last lock is removed."},{question:"Which action is wrong when returning a machine to service after maintenance?",options:[{text:"Removing tools from the machine and refitting the guards",correct:!1},{text:"Telling affected workers before energy is restored",correct:!1},{text:"One person removing all the locks to finish faster",correct:!0},{text:"Confirming everyone is clear of the danger zone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Each person removes their own lock, so no one still at the machine is forgotten."},{question:"A coworker’s hand is caught in a machine that is still turning. What is the right first action?",options:[{text:"Pull his hand out hard before the machine drags it further",correct:!1},{text:"Stop the machine with the emergency stop and call 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"Run the machine in reverse so the hand comes out",correct:!1},{text:"Stay back and wait for the supervisor to decide",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stopping the motion at once is essential; pulling or reversing can make the injury worse. Then 115 for rescue and 122 for the injury."},{question:"The morning shift did not finish work on a machine and the evening shift will continue. How are the locks changed over?",options:[{text:"The morning shift removes its locks and the evening shift applies theirs later",correct:!1},{text:"The morning locks stay and their keys are handed to the evening shift",correct:!1},{text:"The evening shift applies its locks before the morning shift removes theirs",correct:!0},{text:"Only a tag stays on during the shift change",correct:!1}],explanation:"This way the machine is never without a lock and no keys are handed to someone else."}]},{id:30,code:"IND-130",icon:"CloudFog",accent:"#a16207",title:"Combustible dust and explosive atmospheres",subtitle:"The dust explosion pentagon, secondary explosions, housekeeping and ignition control",summary:"Flour, sugar, wood, plastics, animal feed, coal and some metals can burn in a violent explosion when they become fine dust and spread through the air. This module shows workers and supervisors how combustible dust becomes a hazard, why a secondary explosion is larger than the first and how housekeeping and ignition control prevent it.",blocks:[{body:"Dust lying on the floor or on machines may only smolder slowly, but the same dust, when it spreads through the air as a cloud inside an enclosed space, can burn all at once in a very short time and build up great pressure. This is a dust explosion. Rules on combustible dust and factory inspections are set by Civil Defense and the competent authority; follow their requirements."},{heading:"1. The dust explosion pentagon",body:"The fire triangle (fuel, oxygen, heat) gains two more sides for an explosion. If one of the five is missing, there is no explosion:",points:["Fuel: fine combustible dust.","Oxygen: ordinary air.","Ignition: a spark, a hot surface, a flame or static electricity.","Dispersion: dust suspended in the air as a cloud.","Confinement: equipment, a silo, a duct or a closed building that holds in the pressure."]},{heading:"2. The secondary explosion",body:"Often a small explosion inside equipment (for example a dust collector or a mill) shakes the building. The shaking lifts dust that has settled on beams, ledges, light fittings and above the ceiling into a large cloud. The flame from the first explosion ignites this cloud and a much larger explosion runs through the whole building. That is why dust built up in high and hidden places is more dangerous than dust on the floor, and regular housekeeping is the main control."},{heading:"3. Controlling dust",body:"The aim is that dust neither builds up nor becomes a cloud:",points:["Capture dust at the source with a collection system designed for combustible dust.","Clean regularly, especially high and hidden places, using a vacuum suitable for combustible dust.","Do not remove dust with compressed air or vigorous sweeping, because that creates a cloud.","Report and repair dust leaks from equipment and ducts quickly.","A competent person carries out a dust hazard analysis (DHA) of the factory’s processes."]},{heading:"4. Controlling ignition sources",body:"In combustible dust areas: hot work only under a hot work permit and after cleaning around the work; electrical equipment suitable for dust locations; bonding and grounding of equipment against static electricity; magnets or screens to remove tramp metal before milling; watching for overheating bearings and belts; and a ban on smoking and open flames."},{heading:"5. Fire or smoke in a dust area",body:"Sound the fire alarm, get everyone out under the evacuation plan and call 115. Do not open the door of a dust collector or equipment that is giving off smoke, because air rushes in and the fire can explode. Do not aim a strong water jet or a fire extinguisher at settled dust, because it raises a new cloud. Tell the 115 teams which dust is present; some metal dusts become more dangerous with water (see the SDS). Cool burns under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122.",critical:!0}],steps:[{title:"1. Know your combustible dusts",detail:"Check each material’s SDS for whether its dust can burn or explode, and list where dust is produced."},{title:"2. Stop the build-up",detail:"Clean high and hidden surfaces with a suitable vacuum and do not use compressed air for cleaning.",critical:!0},{title:"3. Control ignition sources",detail:"Hot work only with a permit, grounded equipment and no smoking or flames in dust areas.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Report",detail:"Tell your supervisor at once about built-up dust, leaks from equipment, a burning smell or hot equipment."},{title:"5. In a fire",detail:"Sound the alarm, leave under the plan, call 115 and do not open dust equipment.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Do not use compressed air to clean dust: it makes a cloud that a small spark can ignite.","Do not open dust equipment that is giving off smoke: fresh air can turn the fire into an explosion.","Do not use a household or ordinary vacuum for combustible dust: its motor can be an ignition source."],tips:["Look at the color of high surfaces: if dust hides the color, it is time to clean.","Set a cleaning schedule for high places and record who does the cleaning.","Practice the escape routes from dust areas during evacuation drills.","After any process change (a new material or machine) ask whether the dust hazard analysis has been updated."],activity:{prompt:"In a flour mill you see a thick layer of dust on the roof beams and light fittings. A coworker says, “We’ll blow it down with the air hose and then sweep it.” What do you say?",options:[{text:"Fine, but let’s do it while the machines are running so we don’t lose time.",correct:!1,feedback:"A dust cloud near running, hot equipment can explode at once."},{text:"No; that makes a dangerous cloud. We clean it with a vacuum suitable for combustible dust and tell the supervisor.",correct:!0,feedback:"A suitable vacuum removes dust without making a cloud; a thick build-up must be reported so its cause is fixed."},{text:"We spray water on it so the dust falls.",correct:!1,feedback:"Water near electrical equipment is dangerous and some dusts become worse with water; the cleaning method follows the factory’s written procedure."},{text:"Dust up high is no danger; we will just clean the floor.",correct:!1,feedback:"Dust up high is the fuel for a secondary explosion and is more dangerous than floor dust."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In a woodworking plant a worker sees a little smoke coming from the dust collector and smells burning. The machines are still running. What comes first?",options:[{text:"He opens the collector door to see where the fire is.",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the door lets air in and can cause an explosion."},{text:"He sounds the fire alarm, gets everyone out under the plan and calls 115.",correct:!0,feedback:"A fire inside the collector can become an explosion; getting people away and alerting 115 come first."},{text:"He discharges a powder extinguisher onto the collector and the dust around it.",correct:!1,feedback:"A forceful discharge onto settled dust raises a new cloud and spreads the fire outside the equipment."}]},{prompt:"Everyone is at the assembly point and the 115 teams have arrived. The team leader asks what is inside. What do you tell him?",options:[{text:"The type of dust, where the collector is, whether anyone is missing and whether the power is off.",correct:!0,feedback:"This information helps the team control the fire and rescue anyone safely."},{text:"I say it is a small fire and I can go back in and put it out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back in without the 115 team’s permission is dangerous; a secondary explosion can still happen."},{text:"I only say there was smoke and I don’t know what is inside.",correct:!1,feedback:"The team needs the dust type and who is missing; too little information can lead to the wrong firefighting method."}]}],realCase:"Training scenario: in a sugar plant, sugar dust had built up for a long time on the roof beams and the conveyors. One day an overheated bearing inside an enclosed conveyor ignited a small cloud. The small explosion shook the building, the dust on the beams was thrown into the air and a larger explosion followed. The fire alarm and evacuation drills helped the workers get out quickly. Afterward the plant set a cleaning schedule for high places, used suitable vacuums and started monitoring bearing temperatures.",quiz:[{question:"Why is a secondary explosion usually larger than the first?",options:[{text:"Because the shaking lifts settled dust in the building into a large cloud",correct:!0},{text:"Because firefighters spray water on the hot equipment",correct:!1},{text:"Because the oxygen in the air increases during a fire",correct:!1},{text:"Because the power cuts out by itself in the first explosion",correct:!1}],explanation:"Dust on beams and ledges fuels the secondary explosion; cleaning it is the main control."},{question:"Which method is right for cleaning combustible dust off equipment?",options:[{text:"A compressed air hose so the cleaning finishes faster",correct:!1},{text:"A vacuum suitable for combustible dust",correct:!0},{text:"A stiff broom so all the dust is knocked loose",correct:!1},{text:"The ordinary household vacuum kept at the factory",correct:!1}],explanation:"A suitable vacuum removes dust without a cloud and without becoming an ignition source; compressed air and stiff brooms make clouds."},{question:"What happens if one side of the dust explosion pentagon is missing?",options:[{text:"The explosion still happens but is smaller",correct:!1},{text:"A dust explosion cannot happen",correct:!0},{text:"Only a secondary explosion happens",correct:!1},{text:"The dust turns into a toxic gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"All five are needed, so controlling dust, ignition and dispersion prevents the explosion."},{question:"A welding job is needed in a combustible dust area. Which approach is right?",options:[{text:"Welding while the machines run so production doesn’t stop",correct:!1},{text:"Welding behind a wall without cleaning the surroundings",correct:!1},{text:"A hot work permit, cleaning the area and a fire watch",correct:!0},{text:"A powder extinguisher nearby and starting right away",correct:!1}],explanation:"Welding sparks are a strong ignition source; the permit, cleaning and fire watch reduce the risk (NFPA 51B)."},{question:"Smoke is coming from a mill. Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Sounding the alarm and leaving under the plan",correct:!1},{text:"Opening the mill door to see the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Calling 115 and naming the type of dust",correct:!1},{text:"Counting workers at the assembly point",correct:!1}],explanation:"Opening smoking equipment lets air in and can cause an explosion."},{question:"A worker gets a burn on his hand during a dust fire. What is the right first aid?",options:[{text:"Cool running water for 20 minutes and a call to 122",correct:!0},{text:"Ice on the burn so it cools quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Oil or cream on the burn to keep the air off",correct:!1},{text:"Bursting the blisters to let the fluid out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool running water for 20 minutes limits burn damage; ice, oil and bursting blisters make it worse."}]},{id:31,code:"IND-131",icon:"Fuel",accent:"#0e7490",title:"Boilers, pressure vessels and gas cylinders",subtitle:"Operating limits, the safety valve, storing and moving cylinders and what to do in a leak or fire",summary:"Boilers, compressed air receivers and gas cylinders hold a great deal of energy. When a vessel opens unexpectedly, steam or gas escapes with force, parts are thrown and fires or burns follow. This module shows workers and operators how to work within safe limits, recognize warning signs, store and move cylinders safely and what to do in a gas leak or fire.",blocks:[{body:"Every pressure vessel has a nameplate that shows its maximum allowable pressure and temperature. Operating outside these limits, modifying the vessel, welding on it or tampering with protective devices can make it burst. Boilers and pressure vessels are operated only by trained, authorized people and examined by a competent person. Licenses and examination intervals are set by the competent authority; follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority."},{heading:"1. Boilers: warning signs",body:"The operator checks the gauges and water level as the manufacturer instructs and reports these signs at once:",points:["Pressure or temperature rising above the normal range.","The water level in the gauge glass dropping or not visible.","The safety valve lifting again and again.","Steam or water leaks, unusual noises or a burning smell."]},{heading:"2. The safety valve and opening vessels",body:"The safety valve is the last protection against a burst. Never block it, tie it down, add weight to it or change its setting; if it lifts often, that is a sign of a problem a competent person must fix. Before opening any vessel, pipe or cover, release the pressure completely and isolate and lock the pressure sources as in the lockout and tagout (LOTO) lesson. Drain condensate from compressed air receivers as the manufacturer instructs, because it causes rust that weakens the shell.",critical:!0},{heading:"3. Storing and moving gas cylinders",body:"Gas cylinders are filled at high pressure; if the valve breaks off, the cylinder can fly like a missile:",points:["Keep cylinders upright and secured with a chain or strap, with the valve cap on when not in use.","Store them in a well-ventilated place away from heat, flames and electrical wiring.","Store oxygen cylinders apart from fuel gas cylinders: at least 6 m (20 ft) or behind a fire-resistant wall (OSHA).","Move cylinders on a suitable trolley; do not roll, drag or lift them by the valve.","Keep oil and grease off oxygen valves and regulators, and never use oxygen to blow off clothing or dust.","Test for leaks with soapy water, never with a flame."]},{heading:"4. Gas leaks, steam and fire",body:"If you smell gas: do not use switches, lights, phones or flames there. Close the valve only if it is close and can be reached without going into the area where the gas has collected and without any spark; otherwise do not approach, get out, warn others and call 115 from outside. If a cylinder is on fire or a fire is near cylinders: do not put out a gas flame you cannot stop, do not go near hot cylinders, get everyone away and tell the 115 teams how many cylinders there are and where. If steam is escaping from a boiler, move away and call 115. Cool steam burns under cool running water for 20 minutes and call 122.",critical:!0}],steps:[{title:"1. Know the limits",detail:"Before work, read the nameplate and the manufacturer’s instructions: allowed pressure and temperature, and who is authorized to operate."},{title:"2. Check the gauges",detail:"Check and record pressure, temperature and water level on the factory schedule; report anything abnormal at once."},{title:"3. Leave protective devices alone",detail:"Do not block, tie down or reset the safety valve or cut-out devices.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Release pressure before opening",detail:"Before opening any vessel or pipe, release all pressure and lock the sources out under LOTO.",critical:!0},{title:"5. Secure cylinders",detail:"Keep cylinders upright, secured, capped and away from heat, and move them on a trolley."},{title:"6. In a leak or fire",detail:"Follow the gas leak rule, keep away from hot cylinders, get everyone away and call 115 from outside.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never block or weight the safety valve: it is the last thing that keeps the vessel from bursting.","Do not test for a gas leak with a flame: the flame ignites the gas; use soapy water.","Do not go near a cylinder that is in a fire or has been heated: it can burst even after the fire is out."],tips:["Keep a log at each boiler for gauge readings and inspections.","Store full and empty cylinders separately and labeled.","Do not use a damaged, rusted or unlabeled cylinder; return it to the supplier.","Mark boilers, cylinders and main valves on the emergency plan map for the 115 teams."],activity:{prompt:"During your shift you notice the safety valve on a compressed air receiver keeps releasing air. A worker says, “The noise is annoying, we’ll tie it shut with wire.” What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop him, shut the compressor down normally and report it to the supervisor and a competent person.",correct:!0,feedback:"A valve that keeps lifting signals overpressure or a failed controller; tying it shut turns the vessel into a bomb."},{text:"I let him tie it but I watch the pressure gauge myself.",correct:!1,feedback:"No one can watch a gauge all the time, and there is no time to react when pressure rises fast."},{text:"I raise the valve setting so it opens less often.",correct:!1,feedback:"The valve setting follows the nameplate and is set only by a competent person; raising it pushes the vessel beyond its limit."},{text:"I stay away from the receiver and leave it until the end of the shift.",correct:!1,feedback:"The fault remains and the worker may still tie the valve; it must be reported and fixed."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the cylinder store you notice a strong smell of LPG. The main valve is inside the store, next to the cylinders. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I go in, turn on the light and close the valve.",correct:!1,feedback:"The light switch makes a spark and going into the gas area puts you in danger."},{text:"I do not go near, warn others, get out and call 115 from outside.",correct:!0,feedback:"The valve is inside the area where gas has collected, so the rule is not to approach and to call 115 from outside."},{text:"I take a phone photo inside the store to show where the leak is.",correct:!1,feedback:"A phone inside the gas area can be an ignition source."}]},{prompt:"A little later a small fire starts at the store door with cylinders close to it. The 115 teams have not arrived yet. A worker wants to drag the cylinders out. What do you say?",options:[{text:"Fine, we’ll both drag them out quickly.",correct:!1,feedback:"Cylinders near a fire can burst at any moment; approaching risks lives."},{text:"No; we all move well away, keep people back and tell 115 how many cylinders are where.",correct:!0,feedback:"Distance and accurate information for the 115 teams save lives; hot cylinders are a job for trained teams."},{text:"We throw water on the fire and stay by the cylinders.",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying near cylinders by a fire is dangerous, and putting out the flame alone while gas leaks lets unburned gas build up."}]}],realCase:"Training scenario: in a food factory, a new operator noticed that the safety valve on a boiler kept releasing steam. He told the supervisor and the boiler was shut down normally. A competent person found that the pressure controller had failed and the pressure was rising above the normal range. Had the valve been blocked, the vessel could have burst. The factory replaced the controller, thanked the operator and made a gauge reading log mandatory on every shift.",quiz:[{question:"A boiler’s safety valve keeps lifting. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Report it so a competent person finds the cause",correct:!0},{text:"Wire the valve shut until the end of the shift",correct:!1},{text:"Raise the valve setting so it lifts less",correct:!1},{text:"Put a weight on the valve to stop the noise",correct:!1}],explanation:"Frequent lifting signals a fault; blocking, weighting or resetting removes the last protection."},{question:"How do you check for a gas leak at a cylinder regulator?",options:[{text:"With a match held near the joint",correct:!1},{text:"With soapy water on the joint",correct:!0},{text:"By sniffing the joint up close for a long time",correct:!1},{text:"By opening the valve fully and listening",correct:!1}],explanation:"Bubbles show the leak without any ignition source; a flame ignites the gas."},{question:"What is the right way to move an oxygen cylinder inside the factory?",options:[{text:"Rolling it along the floor with your foot",correct:!1},{text:"Lifting it by the valve with one hand",correct:!1},{text:"On a trolley, secured and capped",correct:!0},{text:"Dragging it with a strap across the floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"A trolley, securing and the cap protect the valve; a broken valve can launch the cylinder."},{question:"What is required before opening the cover of a compressed air receiver for inspection?",options:[{text:"The compressor stopped with its button",correct:!1},{text:"All pressure released and sources locked out with LOTO",correct:!0},{text:"The gauge showing half the normal pressure",correct:!1},{text:"A worker standing by the compressor",correct:!1}],explanation:"Remaining pressure can blow the cover off; a button does not cut the energy and no person replaces a lock."},{question:"A fire starts near a group of gas cylinders. Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Getting everyone away and keeping people back",correct:!1},{text:"Calling 115 and giving the number and location of cylinders",correct:!1},{text:"Going up to the hot cylinders to drag them away by hand",correct:!0},{text:"Not putting out a gas flame that cannot be shut off",correct:!1}],explanation:"Heated cylinders can burst; approaching risks lives. This is a job for the 115 teams."},{question:"A worker has been scalded by boiler steam. What is the right first aid?",options:[{text:"Cool running water for 20 minutes and a call to 122",correct:!0},{text:"Ice on the burn until the pain stops",correct:!1},{text:"Toothpaste on the burn to cool it",correct:!1},{text:"Cooking oil so the skin does not dry out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool running water for 20 minutes cools the burn; ice, toothpaste and oil make it worse."}]},{id:32,code:"IND-132",icon:"Thermometer",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Heat stress at work",subtitle:"Water, rest, shade, acclimatization, recognizing heat exhaustion and first aid for heat stroke",summary:"Working in heat, outdoors in the sun or near furnaces, ovens and boilers, drains the body of water and salt and can cause heat exhaustion and heat stroke, which can kill. This module shows workers and supervisors how to control heat, recognize the early signs and what to do at once in heat stroke.",blocks:[{body:"The body cools itself by sweating. When the air is hot and humid, the work is heavy or protective clothing traps heat, sweating is not enough and body temperature rises. There is no temperature at which the weather can be called completely safe; the risk depends on heat, humidity, sun, workload and clothing. Working hours and rules for work in heat are set by the competent authorities; follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority."},{heading:"1. Who is most at risk",body:"Any worker can develop heat illness, but the risk is higher for:",points:["New workers and those returning after a long absence, because their bodies are not yet used to the heat.","Heavy work, work in the sun or near heat sources.","Protective clothing that does not let air through.","Illness, dehydration, some medicines and earlier heat illness; a worker can tell the supervisor in confidence."]},{heading:"2. Prevention: water, rest, shade",body:"The factory sets a heat plan and every worker follows it:",points:["Cool, clean water close to the work, drunk regularly before you feel thirsty.","Rest breaks in a cool or shaded place, and heavy work in the coolest part of the day.","Acclimatization: new workers start with lighter work and build up gradually.","Shade, ventilation, fans and shields against radiant heat in hot areas.","Buddy system: each worker watches a partner, because a sick person often does not notice their own signs."]},{heading:"3. Heat exhaustion",body:"Signs of heat exhaustion: heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea or vomiting, thirst and muscle cramps. Stop the worker from working, move them to a cool place, loosen their clothing and cool them with cool water and wet cloths. If fully alert, let them sip water. If the signs get worse or do not improve, call 122. If their thinking becomes confused, this is heat stroke."},{heading:"4. Heat stroke: a life-threatening emergency",body:"Confusion, strange behavior, slurred speech, seizures or collapse in the heat are signs of heat stroke; the skin may still be sweating. Call 122 and get help, and start cooling at once: the best method is cold-water immersion from the neck down for 15 minutes or until their thinking clears, whichever comes first. If that is not possible, use a cold shower, wet towels, ice packs wrapped in cloth and fanning. Give nothing to drink while they are confused. If they become unresponsive but breathe normally and there is no suspected neck or back injury, place them on their side (recovery position) and stay with them.",critical:!0}],steps:[{title:"1. Drink water",detail:"Drink water regularly before you are thirsty and keep cool water close to the work."},{title:"2. Rest and shade",detail:"Take breaks in a cool place under the heat plan and keep heavy work for cooler times."},{title:"3. Watch your buddy",detail:"Know the signs of heat exhaustion in yourself and your buddy and report them at once."},{title:"4. Exhaustion: stop work and cool",detail:"Move them somewhere cool, loosen clothing, cool them and give water if alert; call 122 if they do not improve.",critical:!0},{title:"5. Heat stroke: 122 and cool at once",detail:"Call 122 and cool at once with cold water, best by immersing the body from the neck down; give nothing to drink if confused.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Do not give drinks to a person who is confused or unresponsive: they can choke.","Do not delay cooling while waiting for the ambulance: in heat stroke every minute counts.","Do not leave a sick worker alone or let them drive home alone."],tips:["Urine color is a simple check: dark urine is a sign of dehydration.","Wear light, light-colored, breathable clothing where protective clothing allows.","Before summer, prepare a cooling area and a tub for cold water at the factory.","Update the work plan with your supervisor during unusual heat waves."],activity:{prompt:"Around midday a new coworker at the oven says he has a headache, feels dizzy and is sweating heavily. He is speaking normally. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell him, “Drink some water and keep working, the shift is almost over.”",correct:!1,feedback:"These are signs of heat exhaustion; continuing at the oven can lead to heat stroke."},{text:"I stop his work, take him somewhere cool, cool him and give him water, and call 122 if he does not improve.",correct:!0,feedback:"He is alert, so water and cooling are right; he must be watched because he can get worse."},{text:"I give him a headache pill and leave him at his post.",correct:!1,feedback:"Medicine does not remove the heat and he is still by the heat source; he needs cooling."},{text:"I tell him to drive home and rest.",correct:!1,feedback:"He could become confused on the way; a sick person is not left alone."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In an outdoor loading yard a worker starts slurring his words, does not know where he is and collapses. His skin is still sweaty. What comes first?",options:[{text:"I give him cold water to drink until he feels better.",correct:!1,feedback:"He is confused; drinking can make him choke."},{text:"I call 122, get help and start cooling him with cold water at once.",correct:!0,feedback:"This is heat stroke; sweaty skin does not rule it out. Immediate cooling and 122 save lives."},{text:"I wait for him to stop sweating to know whether it is heat stroke.",correct:!1,feedback:"In heat stroke the skin can still sweat; waiting delays cooling."}]},{prompt:"Helpers have arrived. The factory has a large tub of cold water and the ambulance has not arrived yet. The worker is still confused but breathing. What do they do?",options:[{text:"They immerse him from the neck down in the cold water, holding his head above it, for up to 15 minutes or until his thinking clears.",correct:!0,feedback:"This is the fastest way to cool; the head must stay above water and someone must stay with him."},{text:"They put him in a car with a fan until the ambulance arrives.",correct:!1,feedback:"A fan alone cools far too slowly when cold water is available; fast cooling is needed."},{text:"They only wipe his face with a wet cloth and wait for the ambulance.",correct:!1,feedback:"A cloth on the face does not lower body temperature; the whole body must be cooled fast."}]}],realCase:"Training scenario: at a block-making plant, a new worker was working in the sun on his second day. His buddy noticed his speech had become slurred and he did not know where he was. The buddy called the supervisor and 122. The factory team immediately immersed him in a tub of cold water, keeping his head above the water, and gave him nothing to drink. After some minutes his thinking became clearer, and the ambulance took him to the hospital. Afterward the factory introduced an acclimatization plan for new workers and made the buddy system mandatory.",quiz:[{question:"A worker in the heat is slurring his speech but his skin is still sweating. What does this show?",options:[{text:"It can be heat stroke; sweating does not rule it out",correct:!0},{text:"Only normal tiredness, since he is still sweating",correct:!1},{text:"A sign of low blood sugar that needs a sweet",correct:!1},{text:"Muscle cramps that get better with massage",correct:!1}],explanation:"Confusion in the heat is the key sign of heat stroke even with wet skin; 122 and immediate cooling are needed."},{question:"What is the best way to cool a person with heat stroke if it is available?",options:[{text:"Cold-water immersion from the neck down",correct:!0},{text:"Putting him in a room and waiting for the ambulance",correct:!1},{text:"Giving warm water so the body adjusts slowly",correct:!1},{text:"Wrapping him in a blanket to stop shivering",correct:!1}],explanation:"Immersion for 15 minutes or until thinking clears cools fastest; otherwise a shower, wet towels, ice packs and fanning."},{question:"Which action is wrong for a worker who is confused from heat stroke?",options:[{text:"Calling 122 and getting help",correct:!1},{text:"Giving him water until he is not thirsty",correct:!0},{text:"Cooling with cold water and wet towels",correct:!1},{text:"Staying with him and watching his breathing",correct:!1}],explanation:"A confused person cannot swallow safely and may choke; give nothing to drink."},{question:"A new worker starts his first week next to a furnace. What is right?",options:[{text:"The same work and hours as experienced workers from day one",correct:!1},{text:"Lighter work at first, built up gradually with monitoring",correct:!0},{text:"Working only at midday so he gets used to the heat faster",correct:!1},{text:"Drinking only at the end of the shift so work does not stop",correct:!1}],explanation:"The body needs time to adjust to heat; new and returning workers are at higher risk."},{question:"A worker is sweating heavily, dizzy and nauseous but fully alert. What comes first?",options:[{text:"Stopping work, moving to a cool place and cooling",correct:!0},{text:"Carrying on until the scheduled break",correct:!1},{text:"A sugary caffeinated drink for energy",correct:!1},{text:"Switching him to another job in the sun",correct:!1}],explanation:"These are signs of heat exhaustion; stopping and cooling prevent heat stroke. If he does not improve, call 122."},{question:"A supervisor says, “The temperature is normal today, so there is no heat risk.” Why is this wrong?",options:[{text:"Because temperature is always measured wrongly in factories",correct:!1},{text:"Because humidity, sun, workload and clothing matter too",correct:!0},{text:"Because heat illness only happens at night",correct:!1},{text:"Because experienced workers never get heat illness",correct:!1}],explanation:"No temperature alone defines safety; the risk depends on several factors."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
